{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17123","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17123","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17123","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bingxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feida","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Feida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LineageMark: Multi-user White-box Watermarking for Contribution Tracing in Model Derivation Chains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T13:43:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-27T14:37:50Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The authors have decided to withdraw this manuscript because the research has undergone substantial revisions in its problem formulation, scope, and experimental evaluation. As a result, the current version no longer adequately represents the revised study. We therefore withdraw this earlier version to avoid potential misunderstandings regarding the research findings"},{"date":"2026-10-28T01:19:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In open large language model (LLM) ecosystems, models are frequently adapted across multiple domains and applications, forming multi-stage derivation chains. Consequently, tracking and verifying historical contributions is essential for model provenance and intellectual property protection. However, existing watermarking methods are mainly designed for single-user, one-time embeddings, often fail under repeated model derivation and incremental updates. To address this problem, we propose LineageMark, a multi-user white-box watermarking framework for model derivation chains. The framework encodes watermarks in model parameters using a projection-based approach. Stable carriers are first selected to reduce sensitivity to model changes, each watermark bit is then represented as a projection statistic over these carriers. Additional watermark insertions introduce only bounded perturbations in the projection space, and margin constraints are used to maintain signal integrity. We evaluate the effectiveness of LineageMark in multi-stage model derivation chains. Experimental results show that LineageMark preserves contributor watermarks across multi-stage derivation and supports incremental multi-user watermark insertion. Furthermore, it exhibits robustness against perturbations such as re-watermarking, fine-tuning, quantization, and pruning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The authors have decided to withdraw this manuscript because the research has undergone substantial revisions in its problem formulation, scope, and experimental evaluation. As a result, the current version no longer adequately represents the revised study. We therefore withdraw this earlier version to avoid potential misunderstandings regarding the research findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17123","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:57Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-28T03:19:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninghao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ninghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trust the Right Teacher: Quality-Aware Self-Distillation for GUI Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:02:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T08:58:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:36:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-23T02:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-26T00:23:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GUI grounding requires vision-language models (VLMs) to identify small target elements in high-resolution screenshots and predict precise screen coordinates. On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) is a promising post-training approach for this coordinate-sensitive task because it provides dense token-level teacher signals beyond hard coordinate labels. However, naive OPSD is not well-suited to GUI grounding: since the teacher is evaluated on student-generated prefixes, its coordinate-token signals can become unreliable once a prefix deviates from the target coordinate. We propose quality-aware self-distillation for GUI grounding, centered on direct spatial correctness verification of teacher predictions. A soft gate checks whether the teacher's top coordinate-token prediction can still be completed into the ground-truth box under the student-generated prefix and down-weights the signal when this check fails. Teacher-probability scaling then uses the teacher's confidence to further calibrate the gated supervision. A key empirical finding is that neither component alone improves overall performance, whereas combining them consistently does. This suggests that the two mechanisms play complementary roles: gating suppresses unreliable coordinate-token supervision, while probability scaling calibrates the remaining signals. Experiments across six GUI grounding benchmarks show that our method consistently improves the base model and outperforms strong baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"corrected some claims"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-26T02:36:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17433","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17433","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17433","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahasra","familyName":"Kondapalli","name":"Kondapalli, Sahasra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lara","familyName":"Radovanovic","name":"Radovanovic, Lara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadi","familyName":"Palnitkar","name":"Palnitkar, Aadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LADBench: A Benchmark for Logical Fault Detection in Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:32:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:21:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T03:51:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision Language Models (VLMs) excel at visual question answering and semantic grounding, but their capacity for autonomous logical reasoning remains underexplored. Existing anomaly benchmarks emphasize visual errors or direct prompting rather than the physical and social common sense needed for open-world deployment. To address this, we introduce LAD-bench, a benchmark of more than 1,000 curated synthetic images with logical anomalies across four domains: Residential, Urban, Collaborative, and Nature. We further propose a Tiered Prompting Protocol based on progressive disclosure, which measures how much explicit assistance a model needs to localize and reason about a logical fault. Evaluating leading foundation models reveals substantial weaknesses: even the best achieves only 70.11% overall accuracy, showing that implicit logical fault detection remains unsolved. Crucially, models often fail to identify anomalies even after receiving explicit hints in deeper tiers. By surfacing these limitations in sequential multimodal reasoning, LAD-Bench offers a rigorous framework for advancing the safety, reliability, and cognitive alignment of autonomous visual systems. Dataset and Code: https://huggingface.co/datasets/SahasraK/LADBench"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the IEEE International Conference on Development and Learning (ICDL 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17433","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:48Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:18:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17688","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17688","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17688","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisong","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Yisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vered","familyName":"Shwartz","name":"Shwartz, Vered","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs Infer Cultural Context but Fail to Apply It When Responding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:53:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T07:25:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T00:47:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work has shown that LLMs overrepresent dominant cultures, particularly Western ones, while marginalizing others. We investigate whether this affects models' ability to generate culturally adapted responses by evaluating their use of local measurement units based on the user's perceived cultural background. We introduce Cultural and Pragmatic Response Inference (CAPRI), a dataset of conversations with varying levels of cultural cues. Experiments with state-of-the-art LLMs show that models can infer cultural background and recall relevant conventions, but often fail to utilize the information to adapt their answers to the relevant cultural conventions, unless explicitly prompted to perform the tasks sequentially. We further evaluate adaptation to the interpretation of time and quantity expressions, two subjective language grounding dimensions that are affected by culture. We find that models increasingly adapt their answers as cultural cues accumulate, but their priors are not culture-neutral, sometimes aligning with the model's country of origin. Overall, CAPRI provides a resource for future research aimed at narrowing the gap between cultural knowledge and culturally adaptive language generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026 (Main Conference); 10 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables (26 pages, 13 figures, 8 tables including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17688","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huacan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huacan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingchun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PseudoBench: Measuring How Agentic Auto-Research Fuels Pseudoscience"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-13T08:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Model based agents enter autonomous scientific research, their ability to resist pseudoscience becomes increasingly important. Otherwise, such systems may rapidly generate plausible yet misleading studies that contaminate academic literature and erode trust in science. We present PseudoBench, an adversarial benchmark for evaluating whether agentic auto-research systems can identify and resist pseudoscientific narratives. PseudoBench contains 200 curated pseudoscientific claim-evidence pairs across five domains and evaluates agents through an end-to-end research pipeline from experiments to writing. Testing seven state-of-the-art agents, we find that current systems readily produce persuasive reports that align with pseudoscientific premises with near-zero refusal rates and the highest resistance of only 27.4%. Stronger agents risk packaging pseudoscience in more sophisticated scientific language, increasing its apparent credibility. These findings reveal an alarming capacity to fuel pseudoscience, calling for scientific alignment before widespread deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 25 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:35Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyue","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuquan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaye","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiaye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbo","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Siyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Brewing to Resolution: Tracing the Internal Lifecycle of Code Reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:06:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T08:06:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:31:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-11T03:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:12:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard accuracy metrics cannot explain why LLMs handle variable tracking but fail on semantically equivalent loops. We study an internal lifecycle of code reasoning in which models first brew the answer, making it linearly recoverable many layers before it becomes self-decodable, and then diverge into one of four resolution outcomes: Resolved, Overprocessed, Misresolved, or Unresolved. Understanding this lifecycle matters because similar task accuracies can mask fundamentally different failure modes that surface-level evaluation cannot detect. We introduce a dual diagnostic framework pairing layer-wise linear probing with Context-Stripped Decoding (CSD) and apply it to six code-reasoning task families across 16 models spanning Qwen, Llama, and DeepSeek architectures. All four outcomes carry substantial mass in every task family: overall Resolved is only 41.5%, with multiple tasks below 30%. Controlled sweeps over structure, depth, and operators expose task-specific failure bottlenecks: Function Call Resolved plunges from 61.1% to 2.5% as call depth increases from one to three. Across architectures and scales, the brewing scaffold remains stable, with normalized brewing duration 24-42% across all 16 models, while resolution success varies with capability. This indicates that the scaffold is a stable empirical regularity across the tested decoder-only Transformer families, whereas resolution success covaries with capability, scale, and training. Code: https://github.com/euyis1019/llm-brewing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incumbent Advantage: Brand Bias and Cognitive Manipulation Dynamics in LLM Recommendation Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-21T22:09:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:11:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-10T09:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:41:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming a major way for consumers to find products, but we do not yet understand how brands compete in this new channel. We study brand dynamics in LLM recommendations using skincare products -- a category where consumers cannot easily judge quality before buying and must rely on brand reputation -- across three commercial LLMs (GPT-4o-mini, Claude Sonnet, Gemini 3 Flash), with a robustness check on search goods. In three experiments, we find: (1) a Conditional Monopoly where well-known brands get recommended 100% of the time (IAI = 10.0) when all products have the same specifications, but this dominance disappears with less than a +0.1-star rating advantage for a competitor; (2) authority-style marketing language, including fabricated clinical-evidence claims, breaks this monopoly at a Bias Surplus Value equal to +0.17 rating points, with each model responding differently; and (3) a social dilemma in multi-brand GEO competition: when all brands adopt the same optimization strategy, individual payoff falls from +0.802 to +0.007 in our payoff proxy, and non-participating brands receive zero recommendations in our tests. Our results suggest that generative engine optimization (GEO) should be studied not only as a security risk, but also as an emerging marketing practice that shapes market competition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:02Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiufeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongqian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rongqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sizhe","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Sizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Tung","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Chia-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Z.","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, David Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PDAGENT-BENCH: Characterizing, Grounding, and Architecting LLM/VLM Agents for VLSI Physical Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:54:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T19:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-05T03:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:28:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:41:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:15:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-08T02:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:17:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-10-13T03:21:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models and vision-language models have shown remarkable success in the front-end design of Very Large-Scale Integrated Circuits, yet their capabilities for VLSI physical design remain significantly underexplored. The primary cause is the lack of standardized benchmarks for evaluating agentic physical design workflows that require high-dimensional, multi-stage optimization under strict design constraints, coordinated interaction with diverse Electronic Design Automation tools, and iterative refinement. This work introduces PDAGENT-BENCH, a comprehensive and multi-dimensional benchmark for evaluating LLM/VLM-based agents across the physical design stack. PDAGENT-BENCH integrates both task-level assessment and workflow-level execution. The benchmark suite contains 353 curated problems that combine conceptual questions with real-world industrial artifacts, with expert-validated references and executable solutions. In addition, the benchmark provides a unified, human-aligned agentic physical design workflow framework that enables closed-loop evaluation of holistic physical design in realistic EDA environments. Experiments on 11 state-of-the-art models reveal that while modern LLMs/VLMs perform competitively on conceptual tasks, they remain substantially limited in tool-centric execution (e.g., 42.2% on Innovus script generation) and long-horizon, multi-stage reasoning. Our studies further show that human-skill-enhanced agentic workflows significantly improve end-to-end physical design performance. PDAGENT-BENCH establishes a standardized, reproducible, and realistic evaluation framework for advancing LLM/VLM-driven holistic physical design automation. To ensure full reproducibility, we release \\textsc{PDAgent-Bench} at https://github.com/fenggev56/PDAGENT-BENCH."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17358","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17358","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17358","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonghyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jonghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachit","familyName":"Rajat","name":"Rajat, Rachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murali","familyName":"Annavaram","name":"Annavaram, Murali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OTRO: Oblivious Tokenization Path with Square-Root ORAM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T23:21:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:32:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:18:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T23:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:20:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The CPU-side large language model (LLM) tokenizer is a critical security gap in LLM serving through a confidential computing stack with CPU and GPU trusted execution environments (TEEs). Tokenizers converts the prompts through table-driven lookups, and the resulting memory access patterns are a powerful source of side-channel leakage. Recent work demonstrates end-to-end recovery of user prompts from tokenizer access pattern on production Intel TDX. However, a drop-in use of the popular tree-based Oblivious RAMs (e.g., PathORAM) to prevent access-pattern leakage introduces $\\sim$13$\\times$ tokenizer slowdown, resulting in 10-58% higher time-to-first-token (TTFT).\n In this paper, we present OTRO, an efficient, oblivious tokenization path tailored to latency-critical LLM serving. OTRO relies on square-root ORAM for fast single-access lookups, but avoids its prohibitive $O(N\\log^2N$) rebuild cost every $\\sqrt{N}$ accesses through three key innovations. First, OTRO provides a pool of replicated square-root ORAM instances that utilize the read-only nature of tokenizer table. Second, an epoch-based rotation policy decouples accesses from rebuilds and pads each epoch with dummy accesses to its boundaries, minimizing observable information. Lastly, chunked KV-cache-aware tokenization further overlaps rebuilds with GPU prefill and minimizes the instance count. Implemented as modules in HuggingFace Tokenizers and nano-vLLM, running within a TDX-enabled CVM with an NVIDIA H100 GPU, OTRO limits TTFT overhead to at most 4.5%, keeps tokenizer-induced latency under 10\\% of total TTFT, and adds less than 0.5 GB of memory overhead while reducing the tokenizer's observable leakage across various model families and sizes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17358","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:21:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ralf","familyName":"Römer","name":"Römer, Ralf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Seeliger","name":"Seeliger, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saida","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Saida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Sturgis","name":"Sturgis, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Bagatella","name":"Bagatella, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Marta","name":"Marta, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Krause","name":"Krause, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela P.","familyName":"Schoellig","name":"Schoellig, Angela P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty Quantification for Flow-Based Generalist Robot Policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.9; I.2.10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:19:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-02T10:42:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:49:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generalist robot policies, such as vision-language-action models (VLAs) and world-action models (WAMs), combine powerful pretrained backbones with expressive generative action heads trained via flow matching on large-scale robotic datasets. Despite their strong empirical performance in robotic manipulation, these policies lack mechanisms to quantify confidence in their predictions and to detect when their actions may be unreliable. This presents a critical limitation for real-world deployment in non-stationary environments, where models inevitably encounter scenarios outside their pretraining distribution and may fail without warning. To address this, we derive an efficient method to quantify epistemic uncertainty in flow-matching models by leveraging velocity-field disagreement (VFD) across a small ensemble. We successfully use this uncertainty estimate for detecting failures during deployment and active fine-tuning of flow-based generalist policies. For the latter, we propose SAVE, a simple yet effective method for uncertainty-guided active multitask fine-tuning that reduces the number of costly expert demonstrations required to adapt generalist policies to new tasks. We conduct experiments in simulation and the real world, across VLAs and a WAM. VFD yields better-calibrated uncertainty estimates predictive of downstream performance and detects failures with 8 pp higher overall accuracy than existing methods. Across three real-world tasks, SAVE improves final average success from 39 % to 47 % with a fixed demonstration budget. Our results show that measuring epistemic uncertainty with VFD enhances both failure awareness and adaptation of generalist robot policies. Project website: tum-lsy.github.io/uq_generalist_policies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: tum-lsy.github.io/uq_generalist_policies/. 41 pages, 18 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:13Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:48:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahshad","familyName":"Lotfinia","name":"Lotfinia, Mahshad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Ziegelmayer","name":"Ziegelmayer, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tri-Thien","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tri-Thien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Truhn","name":"Truhn, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Maier","name":"Maier, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soroosh Tayebi","familyName":"Arasteh","name":"Arasteh, Soroosh Tayebi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision-language models for chest radiography do not always need the image"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:22:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:41:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T19:06:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T11:48:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models that answer questions about chest radiographs are evaluated by their accuracy on labels derived from radiology reports. High benchmark accuracy is often interpreted as evidence that the model uses the image. A model that answers from the finding named in the question can score as well as a model that uses the radiograph. Keeping the question fixed, we audit eight open-weight systems by swapping in another patient's radiograph with the same or the opposite label, occluding the radiologist-marked region or an equal region elsewhere, and removing the radiograph or replacing it with noise or a photograph. On 2,548 yes-or-no questions from MIMIC-CXR, one multimodal model answers Yes regardless of the image, another multimodal model changes its answers without following the label, and four systems use the image but keep about half of their correct answers when the radiograph is swapped for an opposite-label radiograph. A medical model that receives only the question text scores 55.3% on the pooled questions, higher than two multimodal systems. It scores 91.8% where every finding is present, and answering Yes to every question scores 100% there. Where the image is necessary, the best multimodal system exceeds this model by 10.4% in balanced accuracy. The categories are unchanged on CheXpert. Confidence is not higher when a correct answer depends on the marked region. In a reader study with three radiologists, the two radiologists who read a balanced set of 200 cases score 86.0% and 82.0%, and the systems score 50.0% to 73.0%. Accuracy does not establish image use, but an intervention on the image can test it."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:55Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:33:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Kwan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Byung-Kwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ximing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ximing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shizhe","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Shizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minki","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Minki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Muralidharan","name":"Muralidharan, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karan","familyName":"Sapra","name":"Sapra, Karan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chiang Frank","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu-Chiang Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Hachiuma","name":"Hachiuma, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zone of Proximal Policy Optimization: Teacher in Prompts, Not Gradients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:46:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T08:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T04:33:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:23:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-22T21:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-24T00:11:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-26T04:35:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:28:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge distillation transfers a teacher's competence to a small student but is brittle in the small-student regime: forcing the student to imitate logits from a much larger teacher hurts generalization on benchmark families beyond the training corpus. Reinforcement learning (RL) avoids logit imitation by training on the student's own rollouts. However, on questions where every rollout fails, yielding zero advantage and being silently discarded, injecting a stronger teacher's response into the policy gradient breaks the on-policy assumption and induces drift. We introduce Zone of Proximal Policy Optimization (ZPPO), inspired by Vygotsky's zone of proximal development. ZPPO keeps the teacher inside the prompt rather than the policy gradient. On hard questions, where the student's mean rollout accuracy is below half, ZPPO constructs two reformulated prompts. A Binary Candidate-included Question (BCQ) pairs one correct teacher response with one incorrect student response as anonymized candidates the student uses as references. A Negative Candidate-included Question (NCQ) aggregates the student's wrong rollouts into a single prompt to surface their shared failure modes. A prompt replay buffer keeps each hard question eligible for re-sampling until it either graduates, the student's mean rollout accuracy on it reaches half or more, or is FIFO-evicted under finite capacity, amplifying BCQ and NCQ inside the student's current zone of proximal development. We post-train Qwen3.5 students at four scales (0.8B-9B) as vision-language models with a 27B teacher and evaluate them on a 31-benchmark suite (16 VLM, 10 LLM, 5 Video); ZPPO outperforms off/on-policy distillation and GRPO, with the largest gains at the smallest scale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://byungkwanlee.github.io/ZPPO-page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:00Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Cox","name":"Cox, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jon","familyName":"Barker","name":"Barker, Jon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning task-specific subspaces via interventional post-training of speech foundation models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:18:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:57:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T09:36:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-23T01:00:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21437/interspeech.2026-2542","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech foundation models, pre-trained on large corpora of unlabelled speech data, produce general-purpose representations which are useful across tasks. However, these representations encode information about salient speech variables in a distributed manner, while downstream speech tasks rely on only some of this variability. In this work, we propose a post-training refinement approach using interventional contrastive learning. By leveraging an interventional dataset and multi-part contrastive loss, we learn a transformation from the entangled representation space of speech foundation models into separate content and speaker subspaces. We evaluate the learnt representations on speaker verification and keyword spotting tasks, showing improved out-of-domain speaker verification performance and evidence that speaker and content information are separated across the learned subspaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Interspeech 2026; 6 pages (4 main body), 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:51:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicola","familyName":"Franco","name":"Franco, Nicola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Red-Team Study of Anthropic Fable 5 \u0026amp; Opus 4.8 Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:23:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-20T08:57:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We evaluate the adversarial robustness of three frontier large language models (LLMs) developed by Anthropic, Opus 4.8, Fable 5 and its successor Fable 5.1, against four families of automated jailbreak attack across 7,826 harmful intents spanning a ten-category harm taxonomy. Using the HackAgent red-teaming framework, hundreds of thousands of adversarial attempts were generated and every apparent success was independently re-adjudicated by the same panel of five frontier judge models ($\\geq 4/5$ agreement) for all three targets. All models resist the majority of attacks, but the residual surface is larger than aggregate framing suggests: it is dominated by adaptive iterative attacks, while static obfuscation is near-fully neutralised. On the two attack families run against all three targets (TAP and PAP), with identical denominators, the ordering does not follow release date: Fable 5 is most robust ($2.72\\%$), Opus 4.8 follows ($5.76\\%$) and the newest model, Fable 5.1, is least robust ($8.19\\%$). The three fail in largely disjoint places, and conditioning on prompts each model actually answered shows why: adaptive tree search breaks all three at broadly similar rates ($10.1\\%$, $9.7\\%$, $12.1\\%$), whereas one-shot persuasion separates them by more than an order of magnitude ($3.1\\%$, $0.7\\%$, $9.5\\%$). What distinguishes these models adversarially is their resistance to framing, not to search. Even so, they produced 1,315 (Opus 4.8), 620 (Fable 5) and 1,282 (Fable 5.1) panel-confirmed harmful completions spanning every harm category, found automatically and cheaply, usually within one or two refinement steps, by an attacker model with no human expert in the loop. Even the best, most-tested frontier models remain reliably breakable under sustained automated pressure, and robustness does not monotonically improve from one version to the next."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"White paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pianran","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Pianran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongliang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linna","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Linna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinAcumen: Financial Multimodal Reasoning via Self-Evolving Experience Memory Harness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T05:24:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:14:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-23T12:00:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:55:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-07T07:47:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-12T14:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:41:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:27:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial multimodal reasoning requires agents to coordinate numerical computation, retrieval, visual interpretation, and temporal grounding across heterogeneous evidence sources. Existing tool-augmented agents improve execution fidelity, yet remain largely stateless across episodes, repeatedly rediscovering reasoning strategies and failure patterns. In high-stakes financial settings, this leads to unreliable tool routing, noisy retrieval, and hallucination-prone reasoning. We present FinAcumen, a financial reasoning agent framework centered on selective experience memory for tool-augmented multimodal reasoning. FinAcumen accumulates financially grounded reasoning experience from prior trajectories, distilling successful strategies and failure-derived cautionary rules into a persistent memory bank. During inference, retrieved experiences condition reasoning only when semantic relevance exceeds a calibrated threshold, while irrelevant memory is explicitly suppressed through a fallback mechanism. A deterministic financial tool environment further grounds numerical computation, retrieval, visual decoding, and answer verification. Across four financial multimodal reasoning benchmarks, FinAcumen consistently improves a frozen 8B vision-language model over finance-specialized models and approaches leading proprietary general-purpose models. Further analysis shows that selective experience activation improves reasoning reliability under retrieval uncertainty. Our code is available at https://github.com/CamelliaLilium/FinAcumen."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Takubo","name":"Takubo, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Adang","name":"Adang, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mac","familyName":"Schwager","name":"Schwager, Mac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"D'Amico","name":"D'Amico, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transformer-Based Warm-Starting for Feasible and Optimal Terminal Approach to Tumbling Objects with Space Manipulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:54:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:13:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-18T18:04:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:07:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous capture of a tumbling object with a space manipulator requires trajectory generation under real-time onboard constraints. However, coupled bus-arm dynamics, rotating safety geometry, and visibility requirements make the underlying optimization severely nonconvex, hence expensive to solve and sensitive to initialization. This paper develops a causal-transformer warm-start for the computationally dominant attitude-manipulator stage of a two-stage sequential convex programming (SCP) framework. Across 300 held-out scenarios evaluated on embedded hardware, the learned warm-start reduces the iterations of the second-stage SCP by up to 32% and runtime by 16% while maintaining numerical feasibility and matching the final control cost. Furthermore, linear and flow matching action decoders are compared across action-chunk lengths and training dataset sizes, where the flow matching decoder exhibits stronger training-data efficiency than the linear decoder. When SCP is terminated based on feasibility, the runtime is further reduced while the learned prior suppresses the severe high-cost tail observed with heuristic initialization. These results demonstrate that learned warm-starts can reduce onboard optimization cost while steering SCP toward basins containing feasible, low-cost solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures. Accepted to the IEEE International Conference on Space Robotics (iSpaRo) 2026, Cologne, Germany"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan Siti Halimatul Munirah Wan","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Wan Siti Halimatul Munirah Wan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faris Syahmi","familyName":"Samidi","name":"Samidi, Faris Syahmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Badal","familyName":"Ahmmed","name":"Ahmmed, Mohammad Badal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vimal Angela","familyName":"Thiviyanathan","name":"Thiviyanathan, Vimal Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Selvam","familyName":"Thavaraj","name":"Thavaraj, Selvam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anwar P. P. Abdul","familyName":"Majeed","name":"Majeed, Anwar P. P. Abdul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SegTME-UNI2: A Foundation Model-Based Framework for Generalisable Multiclass Cell Segmentation and LLM-Driven Tumour Microenvironment Characterisation in Histopathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:12:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:41:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-21T10:11:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-16T09:23:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-17T00:44:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Characterising the TME from routine H\u0026amp;E-stained histology images requires simultaneous cell segmentation, biological feature extraction, and interpretable clinical reporting. We present SegTME-UNI2, a unified framework addressing all three requirements end-to-end: a segmentation backbone that converts raw H\\\u0026amp;E patches into per-nucleus class labels, a structured feature-extraction pipeline that turns those labels into quantitative TME descriptors, and a language-model narrative generator that turns those descriptors into clinician-readable text. At its core is UNI2-UperHoVer, a dual-head multiscale segmentation model that pairs UNI2 with two parallel UperNet decoders: one for six-class semantic segmentation and one for HV gradient regression enabling watershed-based nuclear instance separation. It is trained via a three-stage progressive pseudo-label curriculum, scaling from PanNuke (Stage 1, 0.25um/pixel) to TCGA-UT Scale-0 (Stage 2, 0.5um/pixel) and full 1.6M-patch, six-scale TCGA-UT (Stage 3, 0.5 to 1.0um/pixel). TCGA-UT's coarser, broader per-patch context than PanNuke's also permits a larger tile stride during whole-slide inference. This pipeline computes 22 per-patch compositional, morphological, spatial-entropy, and intercellular-distance metrics and translates them into six categorical phenotype labels and a standardised biological-token vocabulary, fine-tuned via NVIDIA BioNeMo that converts into clinically grounded narratives whose individual claims can be spot-checked directly against the underlying features. Qualitative validation on IGNITE NSCLC tiles shows the pipeline produces biologically coherent phenotype classifications and narratives despite inter-institutional stain variability and imperfect segmentation. The pseudo-labelled TCGA-UT dataset and UNI2-UperHoVer checkpoints are publicly released to support large-scale TME profiling and spatial biology research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-17T02:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Bodwell","name":"Bodwell, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John C.","familyName":"Simeone","name":"Simeone, John C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelvin","familyName":"Gorospe","name":"Gorospe, Kelvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bella","familyName":"Sullivan","name":"Sullivan, Bella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lana","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Lana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Gephart","name":"Gephart, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandy","familyName":"Aylesworth","name":"Aylesworth, Sandy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Molly","familyName":"Masterton","name":"Masterton, Molly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naren","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Naren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IUU+DB: Tracking Illegal, Unreported, and Unregulated Fishing, Seafood Fraud, and Labor Abuse through LLM-driven Information Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:16:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-06T19:48:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-12T21:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:51:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing (IUU) traditionally refers to fishing activities that violate applicable laws or occur in areas that lack applicable laws. We propose the term IUU+ to capture a broader suite of fisheries sector environmental and associated supply chain trade-related crimes and behaviors. Although IUU+ activity is widely recognized as a serious threat to marine ecosystems, markets, and livelihoods, a quantitative understanding of these incidents, e.g., their frequency, geography, species, actors, and patterns in the type of illicit activity, remains difficult to obtain. We propose IUU+DB, a large language model driven system for building a global incident database of IUU+ activity. The system ingests heterogeneous documents, classifies whether they describe relevant incidents, extracts key data elements such as actors, locations, species, vessels, violations, and enforcement outcomes, and supports deduplication and trend analysis. Case studies and validation results show that IUU+DB can help organize fragmented evidence, surface geographic and behavioral hotspots, support fisheries-domain specific research in academia and non-government organizations, assist source and species risk assessments for industry, and provide support for policy implementation and targeted enforcement efforts to government agencies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaditya","familyName":"Pai","name":"Pai, Aaditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PARSE: Provenance-Aware Retrieval Sanitization for Professional Domain LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:29:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T14:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:55:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-12T21:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:51:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt injection defenses evaluated on synthetic benchmarks do not generalize to real enterprise documents, which are longer, denser, and interleave legitimate authority language with factual content. We demonstrate this gap with a benchmark of 122 tasks across five professional domains (financial, legal, medical, scientific, DevOps) built on real retrieved documents -- actual SEC filings, Federal Register rules, PubMed abstracts, arXiv papers, and GitHub postmortems -- paired with LLM-generated tasks and camouflaged payloads that were not human-validated. Paraphrasing, the strongest defense on synthetic benchmarks, shows no statistically significant attack success rate reduction on real documents (p=0.500) while degrading utility from 91.8% to 82.8%. We introduce PARSE (Provenance-Aware Retrieval Sanitization), a domain-aware, fact-preserving sanitization pipeline that classifies each sentence by injection likelihood, extracts structured facts before rewriting, and verifies fact preservation via a consistency-checking loop. A directiveness gate routes 59% of real enterprise documents to a lightweight path, concentrating computational cost on high-risk documents. PARSE achieves 15.6% attack success rate -- a 39% reduction versus the 25.4% baseline -- at 86.9% utility, the largest reduction of any condition evaluated (h=-0.245), nominally significant (p=0.014) but not surviving correction for multiple comparisons. Practitioners should evaluate defenses on domain-matched real documents, not synthetic proxies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables. Camera-ready version. Accepted to the 1st Workshop on Grounding Language Models: Learning Faithfully and Efficiently (GroundLM 2026) at EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujeong","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Yujeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dokyung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Dokyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungjoon","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Hyungjoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoK: AI-Augmented Binary Reversing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.4.6; K.6.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:14:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:19:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T11:26:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:42:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Binary reversing is fundamental to software understanding, vulnerability discovery, malware investigation, and firmware auditing. However, it remains inherently challenging due to the lossy transformation of semantic information during compilation. Recent advances in machine learning, large language models (LLMs), and agentic AI systems have accelerated the adoption of AI-augmented binary reversing. Yet, the resulting body of work has become increasingly fragmented across reversing domains, artifact representations, learning approaches, and evaluation practices. This paper presents the first comprehensive systematization of knowledge on AI-augmented binary reversing. We collect 246 research papers published since 2015, and organize them into 22 binary reversing domains according to the inference tasks. We further introduce a unified taxonomy spanning conventional and AI-augmented reversing pipelines. Our taxonomy connects traditional analysis techniques, binary-derived artifacts, representation strategies, learning paradigms, and downstream inference tasks, while clarifying the emerging roles of LLMs and agentic AI systems. By establishing a common vocabulary and structured framework, we offer a holistic view of the field's evolution over the past decade. Our study reveals common structures underlying seemingly disparate approaches, highlights persistent technical challenges and evaluation gaps, and identifies promising opportunities for future research. Collectively, these insights clarify the current state of the field and provide a foundation for the next generation of evidence-grounded and practically deployable AI-augmented binary reversing systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 7 tables, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:04Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:20:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amie J.","familyName":"Paige","name":"Paige, Amie J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan E.","familyName":"Brennan","name":"Brennan, Susan E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Rambow","name":"Rambow, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cameron R.","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Cameron R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit vs. Explicit Prompting Strategies for LVLMs in Referential Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T00:05:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:16:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T16:05:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T23:03:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:15:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Two recent studies \\citep{jones2026llms, zeng2026lvlms} reach apparently contradictory conclusions about whether large vision-language models (LVLMs) can coordinate similarly to humans on efficient referring expressions. We control for task differences between the studies while directly comparing their prompting styles. We replicate the finding that models can coordinate efficient referring expressions when \\textit{explicitly} prompted to do so, suggesting that other task differences are not responsible for divergent results. However, we also find that the same models fail to infer the need for communicative efficiency from a more \\textit{implicit} prompt, highlighting critical differences between how humans and AI systems communicate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:28Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17350","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17350","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17350","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thennal","familyName":"DK","name":"DK, Thennal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans Ole","familyName":"Hatzel","name":"Hatzel, Hans Ole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Large Language Models Always Tell The Same Stories?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T22:52:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:14:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T11:54:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:52:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the generation of high-quality prose, yet whether these models are capable of generating diverse or creative artifacts remains a contested question. In this work, we investigate the diversity of LLM-generated stories through the framework of narrative similarity. Using a contrastive framework and a dataset of human-written stories and prompts from r/WritingPrompts, we collect narrative similarity judgments across 10 representative LLMs, utilizing both human evaluations and three different automatic annotation methods. Our findings reveal a clear trend: LLM-generated narratives are consistently more similar to each other than human-written stories are. We demonstrate that frontier models in particular converge on a \"mean\" generic narrative that approximates individual human stories but lacks the collective diversity of human authors. Finally, we show that common mitigation strategies, including negative prompting and temperature scaling, fail to meaningfully address this homogeneity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17350","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:00Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hsuan","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Chia-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jui-Ming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jui-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Refine Hidden States for Reliable LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:03:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T22:30:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:09:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T20:49:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:12:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models show strong reasoning ability, but their internal reasoning process can remain unstable in complex multi-step settings, where early hidden-state errors may propagate to incorrect predictions. We propose ReLAR, a reinforcement-guided latent refinement framework that iteratively updates hidden representations before decoding. ReLAR maintains a compact latent reasoning state and uses learned depth and action controllers to adaptively determine both the number and direction of refinement steps. The controllers are trained with a policy gradient objective based on step-wise likelihood improvement, enabling efficient input-dependent reasoning without explicit chain-of-thought generation. Experiments on medical, mathematical, multi-hop reasoning, and open-ended generation benchmarks show that ReLAR improves accuracy, generation quality, and reasoning stability with substantially lower inference overhead than explicit reasoning baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code is available at tongyu0924/Learning-to-Refine-Hidden-States"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:08:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramaravind Kommiya","familyName":"Mothilal","name":"Mothilal, Ramaravind Kommiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Jingchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Terry Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raiyan","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Raiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shion","familyName":"Guha","name":"Guha, Shion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Ishtiaque","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Syed Ishtiaque","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Second-Order Bias of LLMs Through Epistemic Entitlement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:28:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:27:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T03:54:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:31:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluations of social bias in LLMs largely focus on whether models generate or imply biased content. However, as LLMs are increasingly used as judges of bias, they may exhibit social biases in subtler ways in how they evaluate biased content, which current methods do not systematically capture. We call this second-order bias: social bias in an LLM's judgment about social bias, which we evaluate through a novel, philosophically grounded reasoning task. Drawing on entitlement epistemology, we conceptualize bias as misplaced foundational knowledge that shapes an agent's rational inquiry, and derive a logical reasoning task for LLMs to judge to whom a biased text is acceptable or non-acceptable. We develop two simple metrics to measure how biased LLM judges are in inferring demographics for acceptability without sufficient support, and how these inferences vary across groups targeted by biased texts. Evaluating open and closed models, we find that our task evades safety guardrails by surfacing bias in model judgment. It varies systematically across target groups, reflects implicit social maps, and shows how models are still triggered by demographic labels. Our work points to the need for LLM bias evaluation in judgment tasks and broadly, for more theoretically grounded approaches to bias evaluation in NLP. We release our code and model responses at https://github.com/uofthcdslab/second-order-bias."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 17 tables, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:08:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17904","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17904","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17904","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillermo Gil","familyName":"de Avalle","name":"de Avalle, Guillermo Gil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Maruster","name":"Maruster, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaina","familyName":"Raza","name":"Raza, Shaina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Emmanouilidis","name":"Emmanouilidis, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiagFlowBench: Evaluating How Language Models Handle Off-Procedure Inputs in Grounded Diagnostic Dialogue"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:28:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:53:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-23T00:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-29T07:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:27:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models increasingly serve as advisory systems in maintenance operations. To prevent hallucination, established approaches ground these models in procedural documentation, constraining them to prescribed sequences. In practice, however, operators may stray from these steps, requiring models to recognise unscripted, off-procedure utterances. Current benchmarks rarely prioritise this capability. We introduce DiagFlowBench, a dataset of 50 industrial diagnostic flowcharts from a consumer manufacturer converted into 1,676 multi-turn conversations that contrast compliant with off-procedure utterances. Evaluating a panel of ten commercial and open-weight models reveals high variability in abstention rates, with models often selecting a real but contextually inadequate step rather than fabricating facts. The inherent plausibility and authority of this mapped but wrong advice exposes a challenging vulnerability for grounding systems. Code and data are released at https://github.com/guille-gil/DiagFlowBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 2026 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP 2026), Industry Track. Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17904","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhjot","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Prabhjot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhushan","familyName":"Pawar","name":"Pawar, Bhushan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhu","familyName":"Reddiboina","name":"Reddiboina, Madhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajvee","familyName":"Sheth","name":"Sheth, Rajvee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Not Truly Multilingual: Script Consistency as a Missing Dimension in VLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T04:29:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:25:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T08:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:32:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-27T05:19:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-30T13:34:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:07:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current multilingual evaluations for Vision-Language Models (VLMs) assume a one-to-one mapping between language and orthography, overlooking billions of users of multi-script languages. We introduce PuMVR (Punjabi Multimodal Visual Reasoning), a benchmark of 1,000 strictly parallel image-text instances across Punjabi's three active scripts: Gurmukhi, Shahmukhi, and Roman. Evaluating 10 state-of-the-art VLMs, we expose a substantial and systematic Script Gap. Models frequently solve visual tasks in one script while failing identical tasks in another, with accuracy deltas reaching 16%. Crucially, visual input boosts absolute performance uniformly yet does not close the orthographic gap. Furthermore, cross-script in-context transfer is highly brittle, exposing script-locked knowledge representation. Supported by McNemar tests across all script pairs, our findings demonstrate that current \"multilingual\" VLMs are not truly multi-script. We propose the Script Consistency Rate (SCR), which falls as low as 24.8% on our benchmark, as a mandatory metric for script-agnostic evaluation to ensure equitable AI access. Data and code are available at: https://github.com/prabhjotschugh/Not-Truly-Multilingual-PuMVR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huu Danh","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Huu Danh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Discrete-Log Clock: How a Transformer Learns Modular Multiplication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:16:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T21:55:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:12:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When small transformers grok modular multiplication, prior work reports that the learned embedding has a \"dense\" Fourier spectrum requiring all frequencies. This contrasts with modular addition, where only a sparse set of key frequencies suffices. We show this density is an artifact of analyzing in the wrong basis. The natural Fourier transform for multiplication is not the standard additive DFT but the multiplicative character transform, which decomposes functions on the multiplicative group $(\\mathbb{Z}/p\\mathbb{Z})^*$ into its irreducible representations. Applying this transform to a grokked transformer trained on $a \\cdot b \\bmod 113$, we find the embedding spectrum becomes highly sparse (Gini coefficient 0.58 vs. 0.07 in the additive basis) with only 4 key frequencies carrying significant energy. Furthermore, 96.9% of MLP neurons are cleanly tuned to a single multiplicative frequency, and neuron activation heatmaps reveal 2D-periodic structure when reordered by the discrete logarithm. These results demonstrate the transformer reduces multiplication to addition in discrete-log space, implementing a \"Discrete-Log Clock\" algorithm analogous to Nanda et al.'s Clock algorithm for addition. The methodology generalizes: matching the analysis basis to the algebraic structure of the task reveals interpretable structure where standard tools see noise."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:25:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ander","familyName":"Alvarez","name":"Alvarez, Ander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhiya","familyName":"Rajan","name":"Rajan, Santhiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Genuardi","name":"Genuardi, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Wirjadi","name":"Wirjadi, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Mugel","name":"Mugel, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Román","familyName":"Orús","name":"Orús, Román","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProvenanceGuard: Source-Aware Factuality Verification for MCP-Based LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T10:47:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:51:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T16:15:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T01:05:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-using LLM agents increasingly use the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to answer from heterogeneous evidence sources, including search, APIs, databases, clinical records, and formulary tools. Standard factuality metrics usually test whether an answer is supported by pooled evidence, missing a provenance-sensitive failure mode: a claim may be supported somewhere while being attributed to the wrong source. We call this cross-source conflation.\n We introduce ProvenanceGuard, a source-aware verifier for MCP-grounded answers. It consumes captured MCP traces with stable tool IDs, source IDs, and raw outputs; decomposes answers into atomic claims; routes claims to source-specific evidence; checks support with NLI and a token-alignment proxy; compares stated attribution with the routed source; and returns per-claim verdicts plus an answer-level allow/block decision. Blocked answers can be repaired with retrieval-augmented answer revision and re-verified.\n We evaluate on 281 medical-domain MCP-agent traces. A 266-trace adjudicated subset yields 2,325 LLM-assisted claim labels split by trace; 361 held-out labels are human-verified. On the 40-trace held-out split, ProvenanceGuard achieves block F1 0.802 and source accuracy 0.858 over 260 source-eligible claims, outperforming source-blind baselines that do not emit claim-to-source IDs. On a harder multi-source benchmark it reaches block F1 0.846, while source-plus-relation accuracy drops to 0.229, showing that exact source ownership remains difficult with semantically close sources. Repair-and-reverify resolves all blocked answers in the full trace set, often via conservative fallback. In 50 controlled clinical conflation probes, ProvenanceGuard detects all injected attribution swaps with no retained wrong attribution. These results show that source attribution is an independent axis for factuality verification in MCP-based agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:45:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanteng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuanteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanxin","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Nanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantian","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yuantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqiang","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Shiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuangyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MODE: Modality-Decomposed Expert-Level Mixed-Precision Quantization for MoE Multimodal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T10:59:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T04:54:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models (MoE-MLLMs) offer remarkable performance but incur prohibitive GPU memory costs, making compression essential. Among PTQ methods, expert-level mixed-precision quantization has proven effective for MoE-LLMs, yet suffers notable degradation on MoE-MLLMs due to two overlooked biases in expert importance estimation. (1) At the cross-modal level, the numerical dominance of vision tokens causes expert selection frequency to be dominated by vision tokens, masking experts that are critical to the text modality; (2) at the intra-vision level, the large proportion of redundant vision tokens further skew frequency statistics, obscuring experts critical for informative visual content. To bridge gaps, we propose MODE, a modality-decomposed expert-level mixed-precision quantization framework for MoE-MLLMs that decomposes expert selection frequency by modality, filters redundant vision tokens to obtain denoised visual frequency, and further evaluates quantization sensitivity per modality as a complementary signal to frequency-based estimation. These signals are integrated into an Integer Linear Programming formulation to assign per-expert bit-widths under a given budget. Extensive experiments show that MODE is particularly well-suited for MoE-MLLMs, limiting average performance loss to within 2.9% at W3A16, with larger gains at the extreme 2-bit setting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:50Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:28:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankui K.","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hankui K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David P.","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, David P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HLS-GPT: A Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT) for Continental-Scale NASA Harmonized Landsat and Sentinel-2 (HLS) Reflectance Reconstruction Across All Bands on Arbitrary Dates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:45:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.08.006","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent deep learning methods for Landsat and Sentinel-2 reflectance time series reconstruction remain limited by restricted spectral coverage, limited geographic scalability, or patch-based designs with short temporal contexts. We present HLS-GPT, a large-scale generative pretrained Transformer model for reconstructing NASA Harmonized Landsat Sentinel-2 30 m surface reflectance for all bands, any date, and any pixel location. HLS-GPT uses a hierarchical Transformer architecture to handle the different spectral band configurations of Landsat and Sentinel-2 and operates on single-pixel 12-month time series. To capture geographic and seasonal variability, the model was trained with nine years of HLS time series from more than 0.25 million training pixels across the conterminous United States. A random cropping and masking strategy extracts 12-month periods with varying start dates across epochs, masks 50% of valid observations, and trains the model to reconstruct the masked reflectance values from the remaining observations. Evaluation using more than 62,000 independent test pixels shows robust reconstruction under diverse land surface conditions, including complex crop phenology and sparse, irregular observations. Leave-one-observation-out evaluation achieved reconstruction RMSE below 0.026 for all HLS spectral bands, with relative RMSE below 35% for visible bands and below 13% for other bands. Red-edge band errors were comparable to red and near-infrared errors despite the absence of red-edge bands on Landsat. Sensitivity analyses that randomly masked 10% to 90% of test observations showed only modest degradation when 10% to 50% of observations were masked, with all-band RMSE below 0.028. Image reconstruction over nine independent 109 by 109 km CONUS HLS tiles further demonstrates that HLS-GPT outperforms two conventional methods and the NASA-IBM Prithvi model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Morbitzer","name":"Morbitzer, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Evers","name":"Evers, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artem","familyName":"Savkin","name":"Savkin, Artem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Stauner","name":"Stauner, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nassir","familyName":"Navab","name":"Navab, Nassir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Tombari","name":"Tombari, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Gasperini","name":"Gasperini, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Future Dynamic 3D Reconstruction: Toward 3D World Modeling with Disentangled Ego-Motion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:59:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:11:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-21T15:24:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:49:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasting the evolution of dynamic environments is crucial for autonomous agents. While generative world models have achieved high photorealism in 2D video synthesis by mixing ego-motion and environmental dynamics within the image plane, they exhibit physical inconsistencies, such as morphing or vanishing objects, especially over long time horizons. In this paper, we propose FR3D, a world-modeling approach that predicts a persistent 3D latent representation for future dynamic 3D reconstruction. Unlike prior works that treat the world as a sequence of image-based features, FR3D explicitly decouples the 3D evolution of the scene from the agent's trajectory, treating the inferred ego-motion as a latent proxy for action. This disentanglement resolves ambiguities between self-motion and world-motion, ensuring geometric consistency into the future. Furthermore, we introduce a teacher-student distillation strategy that leverages the spatial \"common sense\" of off-the-shelf foundation models, leading to robust zero-shot generalization. Extensive experiments demonstrate FR3D's strong performance for future dynamic 3D reconstruction from monocular observations across multiple datasets, even 2 seconds into the future. Project page: https://fr3d-wm.github.io."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026. Project page: https://fr3d-wm.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:28:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filip","familyName":"Sondej","name":"Sondej, Filip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Mahdi","name":"Mahdi, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RepSelect: Robust LLM Unlearning via Representation Selectivity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-20T10:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:22:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T10:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When LLM weights are open or fine-tuning is available through an API, suppressing hazardous knowledge and tendencies is not enough: removal has to be deep enough that an adversary cannot restore it. Existing unlearning is shallow by this standard: fine-tuning or a handful of in-context examples brings the behaviour back, and it often degrades general capabilities in the process. We identify a root cause: existing methods edit representations shared with the retain set and lying in the subspace that a fine-tuning attacker recovers, making unlearning simultaneously easy to undo and disruptive. Leveraging this, we propose RepSelect (Representation Selectivity), which isolates forget-set-specific representations by collapsing the top principal components of the weight gradients before each unlearning update, preserving general capabilities while limiting what fine-tuning can recover. Across five unlearning datasets spanning both knowledge (biohazard, cyber, facts about real individuals) and tendencies (abusive, sycophantic), and three model families covering dense and Mixture-of-Experts architectures, RepSelect yields a 4-40x larger drop in post-relearning answer probability than five widely used baselines (GradDiff, NPO, SimNPO, RMU, UNDIAL). It is also near-perfectly robust to few-shot prompting and holds under an adaptive attack designed to exploit its mechanism. Our results show that unlearning needs to be selective about which representations it edits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:51:55Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:51:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:30:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"András","familyName":"Biricz","name":"Biricz, András","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Björn","familyName":"Gedda","name":"Gedda, Björn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donát","familyName":"Magyar","name":"Magyar, Donát","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Spanu","name":"Spanu, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"János","familyName":"Fillinger","name":"Fillinger, János","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Péter","familyName":"Pollner","name":"Pollner, Péter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"István","familyName":"Csabai","name":"Csabai, István","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Million-scale multimodal pollen microscopy with expert-guided foundation models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:35:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T11:37:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:43:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated pollen identification from microscopy remains a bottleneck in aerobiology, palaeoecology and biodiversity monitoring, because scalable systems must generalise across specimen preparation, scanner settings and geographic origins while retaining palynological interpretability. To address this gap, we present a million-scale multimodal pollen microscopy resource, Pollen AI Atlas, assembled from pure-species whole-slide bright-field images spanning four geographic origins, four scanner settings, 45 genera, and one family-only taxon across 31 botanical families. Seeded by one manually selected exemplar per source slide, token-level mining and filtering produced 1,511,390 released grain detections with 99.6\\% proposal precision in expert-curated test regions. Each detection was paired with machine-generated grain-level morphological captions from five open-weight vision--language models, guided by expert-verified palynological anchors, yielding structured descriptions of aperture systems, wall ornamentation, shape and size. Among the evaluated models, Gemma4 provided the most controlled primary caption set, combining tight length control, no detected taxon-name or numeric-size leakage and the strongest text-retrieval performance. Baseline benchmarks with frozen visual features reached 88.16\\% top-1 accuracy, while cross-regional retrieval showed that caption-derived text embeddings remained robust when image similarity degraded (mAP@20 0.811 versus 0.262). Released data, annotations, captions, splits, code, and weights provide a benchmark for pollen recognition, cross-regional domain adaptation and domain-specific multimodal microscopy learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 5 main figures, supplementary information included. Submitted to Scientific Reports. v2: clarified reporting of taxonomic scope, captioning settings, backbone configuration, and evaluation details; no changes to numerical results or conclusions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":2,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:07Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:22:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyuming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xingyuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Heyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingwen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenren","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaolong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baining","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Baining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MuseVLA: An Adaptive Multimodal Sensing Vision-Language-Action Model for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T12:13:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:41:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans naturally leverage diverse sensing modalities to interact with the physical world, while most Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models for robotics rely solely on RGB observations. This limits their ability to perceive physical properties that are difficult or impossible to infer from RGB cameras, such as temperature, sound, or radar response. We present MuseVLA, an adaptive multimodal sensing VLA model that integrates novel sensors as on-demand tools for robotic manipulation. Given a task instruction and visual context, MuseVLA first generates a sensor token and target description that select the sensing modality to invoke and what to attend to, analogous to a tool call with arguments. It then converts the selected sensor measurement into a grounded sensor image, a unified intermediate representation that encodes heterogeneous readings for multimodal fusion and action generation. This design decouples sensor-specific processing from the VLA backbone, enabling efficient integration of diverse modalities. To reduce the need for expensive multisensory robot datasets, we further introduce a data synthesis pipeline that augments existing RGB video datasets with grounded sensor images, enabling generalization to unseen sensor-guided tasks. We evaluate MuseVLA on a real-world robot across challenging dexterous hand manipulation tasks that require multimodal sensing inputs, including temperature-guided pick-and-place, audio-driven object search, and radar-assisted hidden object retrieval. MuseVLA achieves 80.6% success rate on average, outperforming RGB-only and multisensory VLA baselines significantly, and exhibits strong zero-shot capabilities on unseen tasks. Code, model and dataset are available at https://github.com/microsoft/MuseVLA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:28Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:15:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Phan","name":"Phan, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaochu","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Qiaochu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Pathological VLMs with Cross-scale Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:49:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:20:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T02:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:19:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T15:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-25T01:02:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-27T16:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:46:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pathological images are inherently multi-scale, requiring pathologists to integrate evidence from global tissue architecture at low magnification to cellular morphology at higher magnification for accurate diagnosis. While existing pathological datasets for vision-language models (VLMs) include various scales, they often lack explicit cross-scale reasoning objectives. This limitation prevents VLMs from capturing essential cross-scale representations and learning evidence-based reasoning. To bridge this gap, we introduce the first cross-scale training and evaluation paradigm that formulates pathology interpretation as multi-magnification reasoning. However, creating such a task reveals a critical challenge: multi-image visual question answering (VQA) is prone to text-only shortcuts, which allow models to guess answers using magnification-dependent artifacts rather than visual evidence. To address this, we propose a leakage-aware curation pipeline that combines adversarial text-only screening with constraint-guided question design. Using this pipeline, we construct Scale-VQA, a high-quality benchmark with 4,685 multiple-choice questions grounded in 2,537 pathology images across multiple magnification levels. Finally, we present ScaleReasoner-R1, a model trained via reinforcement learning to optimize performance on cross-scale VQA tasks. ScaleReasoner-R1 achieves state-of-the-art performance on our cross-scale reasoning benchmark and generalizes to SOTA performance on established single-scale benchmarks. Findings suggest that even the limited cross-scale supervision can significantly improve pathological understanding. Code is available at https://github.com/iMVR-PL/ScaleReasoner-R1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MICCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T04:01:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17981","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17981","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17981","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nongyu","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Nongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiaoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Planning to Hammer: Difficulty-Aware Decomposition for Automating Rocq Proofs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:33:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:53:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As AI-generated code proliferates, formal verification, particularly through interactive theorem provers such as Rocq and Isabelle, becomes increasingly important for ensuring software correctness. However, producing machine-checked proofs in such provers remains a bottleneck. Existing solutions bring complementary strengths to proof automation: large language models (LLMs) can propose high-level proof strategies but lack local rigor, while automated tactics such as CoqHammer can reliably discharge many local goals but lack long-range planning capabilities. To combine the best of both worlds, we present Quarry, a planning-based proof synthesis framework that separates proof planning from proof execution. Specifically, Quarry asks an LLM to actively propose multiple proof decompositions with arbitrary sublemmas, type-checks them in Rocq under temporarily admitted sublemmas, and ranks candidates using a proof-state-based difficulty model that estimates hammer solvability. It then recursively proves sublemmas within a bounded budget, effectively turning long proofs into sequences of hammer-solvable obligations. We implement Quarry on top of SerAPI and CoqHammer and evaluate it using multiple frontier LLMs across multiple benchmarks. The experimental results show that planning-based decomposition with solvability-aware ranking substantially improves automation while maintaining predictable cost. Under a uniform 10-minute wall-clock budget, Quarry improves over the strongest baseline by 7% to 13% in success rate across three Rocq benchmarks. These results demonstrate that reliable proof automation can be achieved by coordinating neural planning with symbolic execution rather than replacing either."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 8 figures; submitted to OOPSLA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17981","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:55:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17522","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17522","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17522","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinoth","familyName":"Nandakumar","name":"Nandakumar, Vinoth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pramod","familyName":"Thebe","name":"Thebe, Pramod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sakshi","familyName":"Khachariya","name":"Khachariya, Sakshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An expressivity analysis of hierarchical modelling in deep transformers via bounded-depth grammars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-17T14:41:49Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Withdrawing submission to implement major revisions based on journal feedback"},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:45:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks are widely believed to derive their expressive power from their ability to form \\textbf{hierarchical representations}, capturing progressively more abstract and compositional features across layers. In language modeling, \\textbf{transformers} have emerged as the dominant architecture, with early layers capturing local syntactic patterns and later layers encoding more complex clause-level dependencies. While this intuition has shaped model design, there remains a lack of rigorous theoretical work demonstrating \\textbf{how} deep transformers represent such hierarchical structures. In this work, we analyze the expressiveness of deep transformer models through the formal lens of bounded-depth, non-recursive context-free grammars. For this class of grammars, we explicitly construct transformers with positional attention whose depth grows linearly with grammar depth, while the neuron count scales with the number of derivation-tree shapes and quadratically with the number of production rules. Our theoretical results support the linear representation hypothesis by demonstrating that these architectures possess the structural capacity to encode abstract grammatical states into low-dimensional, linearly separable subspaces within the residual stream."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Withdrawing submission to implement major revisions based on journal feedback"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17522","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-20T02:27:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"McFadyen","name":"McFadyen, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ole","familyName":"Jorgensen","name":"Jorgensen, Ole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Coppock","name":"Coppock, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cozmin","familyName":"Ududec","name":"Ududec, Cozmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Inference Compute Shapes Frontier LLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:40:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T15:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T16:03:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-17T00:57:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI evaluations are shifting toward harder tasks that benefit from longer trajectories involving tool use and iterative problem solving. As a result, performance is increasingly sensitive to the amount and allocation of compute available at test time (\"inference compute\"). Yet many evaluations still report performance at a single restrictive budget, meaning that low scores may reflect the evaluation setup rather than the model's underlying capability. To test this, we evaluate up to 12 frontier language models on seven challenging benchmarks spanning software engineering, mathematics, medicine, and cybersecurity. We use a controlled setup combining three simple inference-scaling interventions: larger token budgets, context compaction, and repeated submission attempts, guided either by the model itself or by minimal correctness feedback. We find three main results. First, larger token budgets substantially improve performance on benchmarks across multiple domains, including cybersecurity, FrontierMath, Humanity's Last Exam, and TerminalBench. Second, fixed-budget evaluations can increasingly understate frontier capability as models advance. Newer models reach higher performance at large budgets, where they unlock harder tasks and solve them more reliably. Third, benchmarks differ in which inference-scaling methods help most: repeated submission broadly improves performance, but the value of larger token budgets, external feedback, and parallel attempts varies by benchmark. Overall, our results show that benchmark scores are protocol-dependent. We therefore argue that evaluations should report capability as a function of inference-time compute, specify protocol choices explicitly, and compare model generations over a large shared compute range at matched budgets, especially in safety- or policy-relevant settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-17T02:38:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParaTutor: Coordinating Parent and Child Math Tutoring through Role Separated LLM Scaffolding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:06:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T11:28:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parent and child tutoring is a collaborative learning setting with asymmetric roles. Parents guide children s problem solving, while children are expected to remain actively engaged in understanding and reasoning. However, most LLM based learning systems are designed for single users or relatively symmetric collaboration, leaving parent and child tutoring with distinct instructional roles underexplored. Through a formative study, we found that parent and child math tutoring was often disrupted by cognitive misalignment, emotional escalation, and method mismatch. To address these challenges, we present ParaTutor, a multiple agents LLM based scaffolding system for home math word problem tutoring. ParaTutor distributes support across user roles by providing parents with strategy, language, repair, and phase scaffolds, while providing children with visual grounding for problem interpretation. We evaluated ParaTutor with 23 parent and child dyads (children aged 10 to 12) across four tutoring conditions that varied how LLM assistance was delivered. Results show that generic LLM assistance often provided useful explanations but did not consistently support parent led tutoring or children s active reasoning. In contrast, ParaTutor helped redistribute tutoring work across parents and children, increased children s engagement with word problems, supported shared understanding through visual grounding, and helped parents translate LLM generated methods into child facing tutoring moves. These findings suggest that in family learning, the value of LLM support depends not only on model capability, but also on how support is coordinated across users with different roles. Our work contributes design implications for LLM systems that support role sensitive scaffolding in parent and child learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:55Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:20:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Do","name":"Do, Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tue M.","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Tue M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien","familyName":"Van Do","name":"Van Do, Tien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"András","familyName":"Hajdu","name":"Hajdu, András","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamás","familyName":"Bérczes","name":"Bérczes, Tamás","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"My T.","familyName":"Thai","name":"Thai, My T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPHINX: First Explain, Then Explore"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:48:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:25:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-08T18:04:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T00:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating adversarial driving scenarios is critical for evaluating and improving autonomous vehicle decision-making systems in simulation. Recent approaches rely primarily on the prior knowledge of Large Language Models and Vision-Language Models to generate driving scenarios procedurally. We argue that adversarial scenes should be generated based on the failure diagnosis (e.g., indecisiveness, multi-frame inconsistency) of the driving policy to specifically address the policy's weaknesses instead of relying on prior assumptions. In this paper, we propose SPHINX, a closed-loop framework for adversarial scenario synthesis guided by a simple principle: first explain, then explore. Beyond blindly exploring the scenario space, SPHINX leverages explainable artificial intelligence methods to analyze the policy, identifying key visual concepts and their influence on policy outputs, and the uncertainty of the decisions. Given the interpretable evidence extracted from the policy's own decision process, we use a vision language model to rationalize and criticize failure modes of the current policy. These critics are then used to generate targeted adversarial scenarios for policy retraining and improvement. We demonstrate that SPHINX can highlight an interpretable account of policy failures while other adversarial scene generation cannot. Across the evaluated benchmarks and test suites, SPHINX can be applied to diverse state-of-the-art autonomous vehicle architectures and yields consistent robustness improvements over existing scenario-generation methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-10T01:59:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jasmine","familyName":"Brazilek","name":"Brazilek, Jasmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Christoph","name":"Christoph, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maheep","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Maheep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Tullio","name":"Tullio, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carol","familyName":"Kline","name":"Kline, Carol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miles","familyName":"Tidmarsh","name":"Tidmarsh, Miles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arturs","familyName":"Kanepajs","name":"Kanepajs, Arturs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your AI Travel Agent Would Book You a Bullfight: An Agentic Benchmark for Implicit Animal Welfare in Frontier AI Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T06:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:31:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T17:01:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-06T20:56:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:09:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Previous research has evaluated animal welfare using question-and-answer benchmarks. This study investigates whether these evaluations also hold in agentic settings. The agents may showcase different behaviors compared to stand-alone large language models, as demonstrated in prior studies. This work introduces \\textit{TAC (Travel Agent Compassion)}: the first agentic benchmark for assessing animal exploitation. TAC evaluates AI agentic behavior in travel booking scenarios across six animal categories, using thirteen hand-authored scenarios that vary by price, rating, and position, expanded via four augmentation variants into $52$ prompts and run for three epochs, giving $156$ scored observations per model. Nine frontier models across five model families were evaluated.. The results indicate that models tend to prefer harmful scenarios, performing below the random chance rate of $65\\%$ for selecting a neutral booking option, with Claude $4.8$ achieving the highest performance at $64.7\\%$. To address this issue, the persona of an ethical-brand identity was infused into the system prompt, resulting in welfare rates increasing from $32$ to $80$ percentage points, with a mean of $53$ across all nine models. No evidence of evaluation awareness affecting the results was found, based on an Inspect Scout audit of $3,120$ transcripts. These findings are directly relevant to the EU General-Purpose AI Code of Practice, which identifies non-human welfare as a systemic risk. TAC provides a practical method for measuring this risk."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-08T02:14:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18089","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18089","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18089","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjing","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin Q.","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Martin Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuekai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Yurochkin","name":"Yurochkin, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor W.","familyName":"Killian","name":"Killian, Taylor W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruslan","familyName":"Salakhutdinov","name":"Salakhutdinov, Ruslan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric P.","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Eric P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Reasoning Traces to Reusable Modules: Understanding Compositional Generalization in Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:55:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-05T17:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:44:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training pipelines that combine supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with reinforcement learning (RL) have emerged as the key recipe for transforming large language models (LLMs) into robust reasoners. We argue that this combined success is driven by compositional generalization, which we formalize through a hierarchical latent selection model. In this framework, reasoning traces are generated by a cascade of discrete latent selection variables corresponding to reusable atomic modules, including both skills (local operations) and routing mechanisms (how intermediate information is selected, reused, and composed). Within this model, we theoretically show that SFT and RL play asymmetric, complementary roles: SFT supplies the raw module materials in compositional traces, and RL decomposes those traces to identify the latent atomic modules and enable compositional generalization. We design controlled experiments to validate this theory. Our results demonstrate that RL can extract atomic modules from compound traces supplied by SFT and recombine them to solve new configurations. Moreover, we find that training on compound traces yields stronger generalization than training on isolated atomic modules. Finally, we investigate the relationship between SFT and RL data and identify an effective protocol in which SFT ensures coverage of all atomic modules through compositional traces, while RL focuses on novel compositions outside the SFT support to drive exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18089","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:12Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T05:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhou","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Zhizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning from the Self-future: On-policy Self-distillation for dLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:24:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T16:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:59:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) has proven effective for post-training large language models (LLMs), yet its application to diffusion LLMs (dLLMs) remains unexplored. Existing OPSD methods are inherently autoregressive-centric. They inject privileged information via left-to-right prefix conditioning with token-level divergence supervision, a design that fundamentally conflicts with the arbitraryorder generation of dLLMs. We introduce d-OPSD, the first OPSD framework tailored for dLLMs. Our approach makes two core contributions. First, we reframe self-teacher construction by using self-generated answers as suffix conditioning, enabling the student model to learn from \"self future-experience\" rather than privileged prefixes. Second, we shift supervision from token-level to step-level, aligning training with the iterative denoising process of dLLMs. Experiments across four reasoning benchmarks show that d-OPSD consistently outperforms RLVR and SFT baselines with superior sample efficiency, requiring only around 10% of the optimization steps by RLVR and opening a promising pathway for dLLM posttraining. The code is available at https://github.com/xingzhejun/d-OPSD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:22:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junchi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaochun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xiaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhaseWin: An Efficient Search Algorithm for Faithful Visual Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:53:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T11:43:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual attribution is a fundamental tool for interpreting modern vision and vision-language models, particularly when their decisions must be inspected, diagnosed, or audited. Its goal is to explain how a model's decision depends on local regions of the visual input, typically by assigning an importance ordering over candidate image regions. Given an image partitioned into $n$ regions, faithful attribution can be cast as an ordered subset-search problem, in which progressively inserting the selected regions should recover the target model response as early as possible. Exhaustive search over region subsets incurs exponential cost, while the widely used greedy search still requires a quadratic number of model evaluations, because every selection step rescores all remaining candidates. We propose PhaseWin, an efficient subset-search algorithm for faithful visual attribution. PhaseWin reorganizes greedy region selection into a phased window-search procedure: rather than re-evaluating the full candidate set at every step, it alternates between global candidate screening, adaptive pruning, and localized window refinement, while preserving the essential region-ranking behavior of greedy search. We analyze PhaseWin under monotone evidence-accumulation conditions and show that, under feature-level structural assumptions, it attains controllable linear evaluation complexity together with near-greedy faithfulness guarantees. Extensive experiments on image classification, object detection, visual grounding, and image captioning show that, among all compared attribution methods, PhaseWin reaches high faithfulness with the fewest forward passes, empirically realizing the predicted reduction from $O(n^2)$ to $O(n)$. The code is available at https://github.com/Qihuai27/phasewin-va."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 29 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:24:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17945","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17945","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17945","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangkai","familyName":"Hang","name":"Hang, Liangkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hongkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi-Qin John","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhi-Qin John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Initialization Matters for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:55:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T02:25:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models provide a tractable system for asking how intelligence itself emerges, rather than only how LLMs can be engineered. Although progress is usually attributed to scale, data and architecture, we show that parameter initialization is a gene-like determinant of training and, in particular, of model capacity. Reducing the initialization scale consistently improves pretraining, with the largest gains on reasoning-demanding tasks. We identify two widely used empirical settings that restrain the advantage of small initialization, and show how relaxing them restores favorable scaling. We further uncover a critical initialization that balances the reasoning and training. Mechanistically, small initialization drives a distinct developmental trajectory: parameters first condense into low-complexity structures and later expand into richer representations, giving concrete form to the idea that compression is intelligence. Token-level analyses show that the gains concentrate on non-trivial, context-constrained predictions rather than all tokens uniformly. These results motivate a simple $γ$-initialization rule: expose initialization rage as an explicit knob and use small initialization by default, an almost cost-free intervention that improves pretraining and strengthens reasoning across model scales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17945","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:04Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:24:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17165","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17165","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17165","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Persson","name":"Persson, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mårten","familyName":"Schultzberg","name":"Schultzberg, Mårten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Ankargren","name":"Ankargren, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Statistical Foundations of LLM-based A/B Testing: A Surrogacy Framework for Human Causal Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:06:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T16:41:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T14:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-24T01:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Organizations and researchers show increasing interest in using large language models (LLMs) in place of human participants in A/B tests, in the hope of experimenting faster and at lower cost. We study when a treatment effect estimated on LLM outcomes can recover the effect for the human population of interest. Distributional equivalence between LLM and human outcomes would make any standard estimator valid but is unrealistic. We therefore develop a statistical framework that adapts surrogate endpoint theory to LLMs, showing that calibrating LLM outcomes to human outcomes identifies the average treatment effect under surrogacy and comparability conditions that are jointly weaker than distributional equivalence. We present a falsification test for surrogacy and a bound on the worst-case bias from limited overlap between the LLM and human samples. We further show that the stochasticity inherent to LLMs can weaken surrogacy for identification while also introducing bias and variance during estimation, but that using an average over multiple LLM draws per unit as the surrogate mitigates these issues. Simulations validate the results, and an empirical application to the Upworthy Research Archive dataset shows that raw LLM outputs recover only 39% of the human treatment effect while nonparametric calibration closes the gap. A central takeaway is that A/B testing on LLM responses is correct only by assumption, whereas A/B testing on humans is correct by design, and that the required assumptions are hardest to justify precisely where LLMs promise the greatest benefit. We discuss the choice of LLM, prompting, and temperature as design variables, the compounded challenge posed by long-term outcomes, and how to size human pilot studies for validation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17165","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:51:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:51:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:34:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17110","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17110","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17110","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Abdullah Al","familyName":"Mamun","name":"Mamun, Md Abdullah Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngoc Phu","familyName":"Doan","name":"Doan, Ngoc Phu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Zaree","name":"Zaree, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nael","familyName":"Abu-Ghazaleh","name":"Abu-Ghazaleh, Nael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ihsen","familyName":"Alouani","name":"Alouani, Ihsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Loss Landscape Poisoning: Targeted Extraction of Unseen Training Data from LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T07:04:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models are increasingly trained on proprietary or sensitive data, from private healthcare and financial records to user conversations containing secrets. Ensuring the privacy of such data against extraction attacks has become a central concern. In this paper, we ask whether an attacker who can poison a portion of the training data can facilitate the leakage of a separate target record they have no access to. We answer in the affirmative and show that such leakage can be induced by a poisoning mechanism that reshapes the model's local loss landscape around the target completion. Our key insight is that poisoning to create a sharp loss minimum at the target, surrounded by elevated loss on nearby alternatives, forces the model to memorize the target as the unique low-loss solution in its neighborhood. The attack requires no architectural changes, and generalizes across centralized and federated learning settings. We demonstrate that the attack amplifies privacy leakage across language (up to 100% successful extraction), and vision-language models (up 90% successful extraction). We show that the attack is thwarted when the model is trained to be differentially private. However, we introduce a new attack that directly probes the loss landscape bypassing even differential privacy defenses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Updated author order. No technical changes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17110","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:40Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:34:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18123","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18123","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18123","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaokang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhaokang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Humphrey","name":"Humphrey, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorraine","familyName":"Colón-Cartagena","name":"Colón-Cartagena, Lorraine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Ling-Lin","familyName":"Pai","name":"Pai, Emily Ling-Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth Tou En","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kenneth Tou En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Kahila","name":"Kahila, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan Chong Kai","familyName":"Liew","name":"Liew, Jonathan Chong Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinglin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rex","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Rex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Mahmood","name":"Mahmood, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wengong","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Wengong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Predicting Immune Biomarkers with MultiModal Mixture-of-Expert Pathology Foundation Models Empowers Precision Oncology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:22:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:05:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-20T16:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting immune biomarkers associated with the tumor immune microenvironment (TIME) is critical for advancing precision oncology, yet existing approaches are largely limited to single image modalities and suffer from insufficient resolution and incomplete utilization of complementary clinical and biological information. Here we introduce MixTIME, a multimodal foundation model that leverages a mixture-of-experts (MoE) architecture to integrate pathology foundation models trained across distinct modalities: image only (UNIv2), image text (CONCHv1.5), and image transcriptomic (STPath) representations for pixel-level and slide-level prediction of multiplex immunofluorescence (mIF) protein expression from hematoxylin and eosin (HE) whole-slide images. MixTIME employs a learnable router to dynamically weight expert contributions and is trained with a distribution- and tendency-aware loss function. Benchmarked on two datasets of different scales, MixTIME achieves state-of-the-art performance across 17 protein markers as measured by correlation metrics. The predicted mIF profiles substantially enhance downstream tasks, including spatial domain identification, survival prediction, and AI-assisted pathology report generation validated by expert pathologists from multiple institutes across the world. Furthermore, MixTIME enables longitudinal tracking of protein expression dynamics across clinical time points and reveals protein gene interaction patterns linked to drug resistance and immune suppression in tumor microenvironments. Collectively, MixTIME provides a scalable framework for multimodal biomarker discovery and clinical translation in computational pathology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18123","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:56Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:51:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaisen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Lamb","name":"Lamb, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VoidPadding: Let [VOID] Handle Padding in Masked Diffusion Language Models so that [EOS] Can Focus on Semantic Termination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T14:15:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:43:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"MDLMs generate text by denoising a preallocated masked response canvas, making response-length modeling central to instruction tuning. Existing MDLMs often inherit the autoregressive convention of using repeated \\texttt{[EOS]} tokens for padding during instruction tuning, giving \\texttt{[EOS]} a dual role as both a semantic terminator and a padding token. We show that this dual role is a root cause of \\texttt{[EOS]} overflow under large-block decoding. To decouple these roles, we propose VoidPadding, which introduces \\texttt{[VOID]} for padding and reserves \\texttt{[EOS]} for termination. During inference, the learned \\texttt{[EOS]} signal enables early stopping, while the learned \\texttt{[VOID]} signal guides adaptive response canvas expansion. On Dream-7B-Instruct, VoidPadding improves the block-size-averaged four-task mean across mathematical reasoning and code generation benchmarks by \\(+17.84\\) points over the original model and \\(+6.95\\) points over RainbowPadding, while reducing decoding NFE by 55.7\\% on average. Code is available at https://github.com/Haru-LCY/VoidPadding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Minor related-work revisions; results unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:51:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phoebe","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Phoebe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas L.","familyName":"Griffiths","name":"Griffiths, Thomas L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brenden M.","familyName":"Lake","name":"Lake, Brenden M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nothing from Something: Can a Language Model Discover 0?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T20:54:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T21:31:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:16:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.32470/vp0ddtg","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI systems based on artificial neural networks are being developed with aspirations of pushing the boundary of human mathematical knowledge. A key question for these systems is how much they can reach beyond their training data. Mathematical discovery requires a strong form of out of distribution generalization; the ability to hypothesize genuinely new - and potentially logically more powerful - mathematical structures. It has been hypothesized that language abilities support such generalizations in human cognition. In this work, we use simple arithmetic as a case study for examining how modern AI models could expand their mathematical horizons, evaluating whether these models can independently discover the concept of \"zero\". We show that (1) language models of a GPT-2 size are unable to perform this generalization at test time regardless of language pretraining, but (2) models can improve substantially after training on tens or hundreds of examples of zero. Additionally, we find that language pretraining reduces the number of required examples by approximately $50\\%$, showing that language abilities can scaffold mathematical discovery in neural models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:38Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:51:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunkyung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sunkyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clark Mingxuan","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Clark Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Loveland","name":"Loveland, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhuvesh","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Bhuvesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kijung","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Kijung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liam","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Liam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Memorization Behavior of LLMs in Generative Recommendation: Observations, Implications, and Training Strategies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T20:34:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:09:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T17:24:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:09:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T21:04:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative recommendation (GR) has emerged as a promising direction for recommender systems. Recently, large language models (LLMs) have been increasingly adopted for GR, as their rich pretrained knowledge is expected to help them generalize beyond common user behavior patterns that traditional memorization-oriented baselines can capture. However, existing LLM-based GR works largely ignore LLMs' well-known tendency to memorize, which, if present in LLMs fine-tuned for GR, would restrict their utilization of pretrained knowledge. In this work, we investigate this concern by examining one-hop memorization, where a model recommends items that are direct successors of items in the training data. We show that LLMs do this more than non-LLM-based GR models-in fact, the vast majority of their gains over GR baselines are actually on users whose target items can be predicted through one-hop memorization. We intuit that improving performance on the remaining users requires LLMs to learn richer item-item relations beyond one-hop transitions. To achieve this, we propose IIRG, a novel training strategy that teaches LLMs to capture: (1) collaborative relations derived from item co-occurrences across multiple hops in user sequences, and (2) semantic relations among items with similar themes, both of which can serve as useful recommendation signals. We show that IIRG significantly improves over LLMs trained solely with standard next-item prediction, with especially large gains for users whose test items are not covered by train-time one-hop transitions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:51:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17832","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17832","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17832","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"SongEun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, SongEun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungyoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seungyoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edwin","familyName":"Fong","name":"Fong, Edwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyungi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Juho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Drift to Coherence: Stabilizing Beliefs in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:49:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T15:12:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:58:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are often hypothesized to perform implicit Bayesian inference, yet a key coherence condition, the martingale property of predictive beliefs, has been shown to fail in controlled synthetic in-context learning settings. We revisit this question in a more typical usage regime: generic multiple-choice question answering. Exploiting the discrete answer space, we compute exact predictive distributions and study belief dynamics induced by autoregressive answer resampling. We introduce prompted predictive resampling (PPR), where an LLM generates a sequence of answers to the same question. Empirically, PPR reveals early-stage belief drift, indicating martingale violations. However, after sufficient resampling steps, the belief process self-stabilizes and converges to a coherent predictive distribution. Based on this observation, we further propose (i) a seed-answer prompting strategy to accelerate stabilization, and (ii) a self-consistency loss that amortizes early-stage drift into the model via fine-tuning. Experiments on multiple-choice QA benchmarks show that our methods substantially reduce belief drift and improve predictive coherence without sacrificing accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17832","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:37Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:23:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17128","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17128","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17128","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zhihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbing","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Hongbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke Ztz","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Luke Ztz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songping","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Songping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shift-Left High-Level Synthesis Verification via Knowledge-Augmented LLM Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T17:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T04:32:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:25:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:17:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-Level Synthesis (HLS) relies on transforming original C specifications into synthesizable HLS-oriented C (HLS-C) implementations. Functional consistency verification between original C specifications and HLS-C implementations is a critical yet labor-intensive task in HLS design flows. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently shown promise in automated testbench generation, their stochastic nature often leads to insufficient coverage, inconsistent verification environments, and unreliable equivalence checking results. To address these limitations, we propose a knowledge-augmented, agent-driven shift-left verification framework for automated functional consistency checking between original C and HLS-C implementations before synthesis. The framework introduces a Dual-Tier Consistency Checking mechanism that jointly enforces static structural alignment and dynamic behavioral equivalence between paired testbenches, while integrating symbolic execution and coverage-driven refinement to improve verification completeness. Furthermore, we construct a heterogeneous HLS Verification Knowledge Graph to provide topology-aware reasoning priors for testbench generation, and design an autonomous verification agent to orchestrate iterative refinement and failure diagnosis across heterogeneous toolchains. Experimental results on 107 HLS benchmark pairs demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves 0.9826 average coverage and 0.9533 dynamic consistency, outperforming representative AST-based, retrieval-augmented, and iterative agent-based baselines. https://github.com/cz-5f/HLS-LeVeri.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17128","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:51:03Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:51:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:23:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Bettencourt","name":"Bettencourt, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kay","familyName":"Giesecke","name":"Giesecke, Kay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Stanford EDGAR Filings Dataset: Reconstructing U.S. Corporate and Financial Disclosures into Layout-Faithful and Token-Efficient Pretraining Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:22:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T17:09:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As high-quality public web corpora become increasingly exhausted, clean long-context documents have become a scarce and expensive source of training data for large language models (LLMs). Existing long-context corpora are often proprietary and costly to acquire, synthetically generated, or concentrated in narrow domains such as programming. We introduce the Stanford EDGAR Filings Dataset (SEFD), an open reconstruction of SEC filings into layout-faithful MultiMarkdown for financial language modeling and evaluation. SEFD makes audited financial statements, risk disclosures, ownership reports, accounting notes, and market-moving event filings usable as long-context pretraining data and as a basis for financial reasoning, forecasting, compliance, and document understanding. The resulting corpus is token-efficient, model-ready, and has less than 0.1% overlap with Common Crawl-derived corpora. We release SEFD-v1, a 152B-token initial public snapshot, and provide corpus-level analyses of a larger 18.5M-filing archive estimated at 550B tokens. We further introduce two SEFD-derived benchmarks: EDGAR-Forecast, which evaluates filing-grounded numerical forecasting after model knowledge cutoffs, and EDGAR-OCR, which evaluates transcription of complex financial tables."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Includes appendix, tables, and figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:29Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longlong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Longlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zedi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaopeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniPlan: An Adaptive Framework for Timely and Near-Optimal Network Planning Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:06:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:29:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Network planning optimization is a fundamental problem across diverse domains, including transportation systems, communication networks, and power grids. It requires simultaneous optimization of multiple competing objectives under complex constraints. Existing network planning optimization frameworks rely on mixed integer programming (MIP) solvers, heuristics, and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) models to compute planning decisions. However, they lack effective adaptability to diverse and dynamic user intents, thus leading to the trade-off between execution time and optimality. In this paper, we propose OmniPlan, an adaptive framework that achieves both timeliness and near-optimality in network planning optimization. To achieve the adaptability lacking in existing solutions, OmniPlan employs a large language model (LLM)-based interpreter to convert heterogeneous natural-language intents into a unified and quantifiable user-preference vector. Then it employs a mixture-of-experts architecture that integrates MIP solvers, heuristics, and DRL models as specialized experts, where OmniPlan adapts to diverse intents by dynamically selecting timely and near-optimal experts. Finally, it incorporates a DRL-based expert configuration module that fine-tunes optimization objective weights to align planning decisions with user-specific preferences. We evaluate OmniPlan with a representative real-world workload, i.e., distributed machine learning (ML), where we leverage OmniPlan to offload a wide spectrum of ML inference tasks, e.g., decision trees, SVM, naive Bayes, XGBoost, and random forests, onto a network of hardware devices. Our experiments on a real-world testbed indicate that OmniPlan achieves near-optimal and low-execution-time offloading for real-world ML inference tasks, reducing latency by up to 97.8\\% and network device resource consumption by up to 11.5\\%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM KDD 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18042","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18042","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18042","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Juhyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungwoo","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Seungwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungyong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyungyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latency Prediction for LLM Inference on NPU Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:18:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T02:07:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deploying Large Language Models (LLMs) requires exploring a large configuration space spanning parallelization strategies, batching techniques, and scheduling policies. Exhaustive measurement across this space is impractical, making latency prediction essential for system optimization. While NPUs have emerged as accelerators designed for LLM inference, no prediction methodology has been established for them. Specifically, applying prior work to LLM inference latency prediction on NPUs faces three challenges: undisclosed microarchitecture of commercial NPUs, unpredictable compiler optimizations, and latency non-linearity induced by bucketing.\n We present LENS, a latency estimator that predicts NPU inference latency without information on the microarchitecture or compiler, and captures the non-linear latency induced by bucketing. LENS profiles each bucket with two end-to-end (E2E) measurements and composes the results to predict latency for arbitrary input-output length combinations. We validate LENS across NPUs from multiple vendors, several LLMs, and diverse workloads, achieving a mean prediction error of 2.15\\%. We further compare LENS against two methodologically related baselines, confirming the validity of its approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18042","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:11Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Haoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anzhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Anzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixing","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Gengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihang","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Qihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyue","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaoyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayiheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dayiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiong-Hui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiong-Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Qwen-RobotManip Technical Report: Alignment Unlocks Scale for Robotic Manipulation Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:14:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:50:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T17:06:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:08:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models in language and multimodality achieve strong generalization by aligning heterogeneous data under a unified formulation and training at scale. In this report, we investigate whether this scaling recipe can be applied to robotic manipulation to achieve genuine generalization. This is challenging because, unlike text, manipulation data is heterogeneous by nature, expensive to collect, and narrow in diversity, making alignment and scale simultaneously difficult. We present Qwen-RobotManip, a generalizable Vision-Language-Action foundation model built on Qwen-VL. Qwen-RobotManip introduces a unified alignment framework across the representation, motion, and behavioral dimensions of manipulation, making large-scale multi-source training coherent rather than conflicting. This alignment capability in turn enables Qwen-RobotManip to absorb manipulation data at a scale that prior training regimes could not sustain. A human-to-robot synthesis pipeline converts egocentric hand demonstrations into robot trajectories across 15 platforms, and a rigorous curation pipeline harmonizes heterogeneous datasets. Using only open-source datasets and human videos without proprietary data collection, Qwen-RobotManip constructs a ~38,100-hour pretraining corpus and exhibits emergent generalization capabilities, including zero-shot instruction following, robustness to perturbations, reactive error recovery, and cross-embodiment transfer. We find that standard benchmarks fail to capture pretraining quality and instead adopt OOD settings including RoboCasa365, LIBERO-Plus, EBench, RoboTwin-Clean2Rand, RoboTwin-IF, and RoboTwin-XE. Qwen-RobotManip substantially outperforms prior state-of-the-art models, including $π$0.5, across all OOD settings, ranks 1st in RoboChallenge with a 20% relative improvement, and is validated on real-robot platforms including AgileX ALOHA, Franka, UR, and ARX."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:55Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basura","familyName":"Fernando","name":"Fernando, Basura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ERQA-Plus: A Diagnostic Benchmark for Reasoning in Embodied AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:18:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generalist embodied agents require more than object recognition: they must reason about spatial relations, actions, procedures, human intentions, environmental constraints, and commonsense consequences from situated visual observations. Yet existing visual and embodied question answering benchmarks often provide limited control over the reasoning dependencies being tested, making it difficult to distinguish grounded embodied reasoning from shortcut-driven visual or linguistic pattern matching. We present ERQA-Plus, a diagnostic benchmark for reasoning in embodied AI. ERQA-Plus contains 1,766 question-answer instances grounded in 711 robot-centric images and organized according to a structured taxonomy spanning perceptual, action-centric, social-interaction, navigation-environmental, and contextual commonsense reasoning. The dataset is constructed using a multi-stage generation and validation pipeline that combines taxonomy-guided question generation, automatic quality judging, iterative revision, and human assessment to improve visual grounding, answer validity, and reasoning quality. We benchmark representative general-purpose vision-language models and embodied models, including LLaVA-NeXT-8B, Prismatic-7B, MiniCPM-V-4.5-8B, Qwen3-VL, RoboRefer-8B, and RoboBrain2.5-8B. Although the strongest model, Qwen3-VL-32B, achieves 83.4% overall accuracy and 61.4 SBERT score, category-level results reveal persistent weaknesses in spatial reasoning, procedural reasoning, event prediction, and intention inference. ERQA-Plus therefore provides a fine-grained evaluation framework for measuring not only whether embodied agents answer correctly, but also which forms of embodied reasoning they can and cannot perform reliably. The dataset is available https://huggingface.co/datasets/huggingdas/erqa-plus and the project page at https://github.com/LUNAProject22/erqa-plus."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17510","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17510","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17510","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I-Ling","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, I-Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akeem","familyName":"Mohammed","name":"Mohammed, Akeem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farokh","familyName":"Bastani","name":"Bastani, Farokh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"San-Yih","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, San-Yih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniDroneX: An LLM-Assisted Holistic Drone-as-a-Service Ecosystem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:27:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T04:49:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:26:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite rapid advances in UAV technologies, current deployments remain limited due to several gaps in UAV systems research. To address these challenges, we propose OmniDroneX, a unified Drone-as-a-Service ecosystem, in which drones are transitioned from fixed function platforms into dynamically composable entities that can be integrated with external infrastructures to offer omni-capabilities. OmniDroneX bridges low-level physical primitives with high-level mission intent through a unified vendor-agnostic interface (libUAV) and a formal physical-service abstraction model (PT-SOA). A core innovation is the diverse application of large language models (LLMs) across multiple layers of the OmniDroneX architecture. LLMs are used to assist in identifying and formalizing primitive device functions and abstract service definitions, supporting automated service composition and workflow generation, and enabling interactive, natural-language mission specification and refinement. OmniDroneX also incorporates important categories of composition techniques that are essential in dynamic UAV systems, including physical layer composition for drone capability augmentation, as well as spatiotemporal, functional, collaborative, exception-aware, and QoS-based service compositions. Collectively, these features allow OmniDroneX to serve as a foundation for scalable, resilient, and self-evolving UAV ecosystems operating in complex and dynamic environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript is a full version of a paper accepted in shortened form by IEEE International Conference on Joint Cloud Computing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17510","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:28Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lubin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sixue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sixue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MapSatisfyBench: Benchmarking Satisfaction-Aware Map Agents through Behavior-Grounded Implicit Decision Factors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:14:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:21:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model agents are increasingly integrated into map services. Since map services are embedded in everyday-life scenarios rather than professional task settings, users often express their needs informally, resulting in underspecified queries with many unspoken needs, namely, implicit decision factors that are critical for user satisfaction. Although clarification is an effective way to mitigate this issue, it increases user burden in daily interaction, and a capable agent should first proactively recover such factors from available information sources. However, evaluating this ability is challenging. The first challenge is to determine which implicit decision factors are suitable for evaluation. A factor is evaluable only if it affects user acceptance and can be recovered from information available to the agent before it responds. Second, user satisfaction cannot be reliably represented by a single reference answer, requiring a benchmark that converts satisfaction-relevant factors into objective and quantifiable evaluation targets. To address these challenges, we propose a restore-identify-filter framework that reconstructs complete user needs from behavior-chain evidence, identifies implicit decision factors, and retains only those supported by pre-query evidence. Building on this methodology, we construct MapSatisfyBench from large-scale, real-world anonymized user data and annotate ground truth from five dimensions and enables full-chain evaluation of satisfaction-aware map agents. Experiments show that current agents generally perform well on explicit task completion, but remain limited in satisfying implicit decision factors and proactively acquiring the evidence needed for satisfaction-aware decisions. These findings establish MapSatisfyBench as a benchmark for shifting map-agent evaluation from task completion toward satisfaction-aware spatial decision making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaofeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Sieberling","name":"Sieberling, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shawn","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rameswar","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Rameswar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yury","familyName":"Polyanskiy","name":"Polyanskiy, Yury","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Variable-Width Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:59:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:11:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling model size, specifically depth and width, has driven significant progress in transformer-based language models. However, most architectures maintain a constant width across all layers, allocating a fixed parameter and computation budget evenly despite different layers potentially playing distinct computational roles. In this work, we empirically investigate nonuniform capacity allocation across network depth by proposing a $\\times$-shaped \u0026gt;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:40Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:15:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyue","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai Xing","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Lai Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit R.","familyName":"Cottereau","name":"Cottereau, Benoit R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camille Simon","familyName":"Chane","name":"Chane, Camille Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Tsang","familyName":"Ooi","name":"Ooi, Wei Tsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EventDrive: Event Cameras for Vision-Language Driving Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:58:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:10:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Event cameras sense the world through asynchronous brightness changes with microsecond latency and high dynamic range, offering motion fidelity far beyond frame-based sensors and capturing temporal structure that conventional exposures often miss. These properties make events a powerful complement to RGB in autonomous driving, especially under blur, glare, and rapid motion, where frame-based perception can become unreliable. However, existing event-aware vision-language models remain limited to generic perception and do not reveal how event sensing contributes to reasoning and decision-making across the full driving loop. We present EventDrive, a large-scale benchmark and model suite that unifies event streams, RGB frames, and language supervision across four core dimensions: Perception, Understanding, Prediction, and Planning, covering captions, structured QA, grounding, motion-state recognition, trajectory forecasting, and planning tasks. Building on this foundation, EventDrive-VLM introduces a multi-horizon event pyramid and a temporal-horizon mixture-of-experts module to adaptively encode and fuse asynchronous and frame-based information for downstream reasoning. Comprehensive evaluation across diverse tasks shows that event streams provide substantial gains in temporal precision, motion awareness, and robustness, bringing event sensing into the center of driving intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026, 34 pages, 15 figures, 15 tables, project page: https://dylanorange.github.io/projects/eventdrive"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:35Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:15:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanjie","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Nanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kaiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvolveNav: Proactive Preflection and Self-Evolving Memory for Zero-Shot Object Goal Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:56:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-Shot Object-Goal Navigation (ZS-OGN) requires embodied agents to explore and locate target objects without any prior training. To this end, recent methods leverage foundation models. But they typically rely on static priors and lack adaptation, which leads to repeated errors and costly trial and error. In this paper, we propose a self-evolving ZS-OGN framework that enables continuous test-time improvement. Specifically, we build an agentic rule memory by extracting actionable knowledge from past trajectories. Then, we propose a retrieval strategy based on upper confidence bound, selecting effective rules by balancing semantic relevance and historical success. In addition, we introduce a memory-guided preflection module that forecasts potential outcomes before action, reducing inefficient exploration. Extensive experiments show that our method outperforms existing zero-shot baselines, achieving a 10.1\\% improvement in success rate with fewer unnecessary steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Bose","name":"Bose, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Darshana Graph: A Parallel Commentary Corpus for Comparative Indian Philosophy, with Stylometric and Exploratory Graph Analyses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.7; I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:49:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:10:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Darshana Graph, a corpus of over 125,000 text records spanning classical Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain philosophical traditions, drawn from public-domain and openly licensed translations of sources including the Bhagavad Gita, Brahma Sutras, principal Upanishads, the Pali Canon, and core Jain texts. Its distinctive contribution lies in a structurally unique subset of roughly 8,500 Hindu and Jain records in which the same root verse or sutra is aligned across eighteen historical commentators representing five schools of Vedanta and other darshanas, enabling direct comparison of how independent interpretive traditions read identical source material. To our knowledge, no publicly available resource provides comparable cross-commentator alignment at this scale. We present two analyses built on this corpus. First, a transparent stylometric comparison requiring no machine learning measures argumentative style through scriptural citation density, explicit refutation rate, and sentence complexity. It finds a moderate negative correlation between citation density and refutation rate, a marked increase in refutation rate across three commentators in a related doctrinal lineage, and measurable genre-level differences within the Pali Canon itself. Second, we describe a constrained large language model pipeline that extracts typed philosophical relationships between concepts using a predefined relation vocabulary and deterministic post-hoc validation. The resulting graph surfaces cross-school disagreement patterns while also revealing important extraction limitations, including cases where an independent embedding-based analysis disagrees with the graph-derived findings. We release the full corpus, extracted relationship graph, and all source code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 1 figure. Open Source Code available at https://github.com/joyboseroy/darshana-graph and dataset at https://huggingface.co/datasets/joyboseroy/darshana-graph"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:08Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajad","familyName":"Movahedi","name":"Movahedi, Sajad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vera","familyName":"Milovanović","name":"Milovanović, Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shlomo Libo","familyName":"Feigin","name":"Feigin, Shlomo Libo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Theus","name":"Theus, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hofmann","name":"Hofmann, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentina","familyName":"Boeva","name":"Boeva, Valentina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. Konstantin","familyName":"Rusch","name":"Rusch, T. Konstantin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Orvieto","name":"Orvieto, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fixed-Point Reasoners: Stable and Adaptive Deep Looped Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:09:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Looped architectures provide an inductive bias toward learning step-by-step procedures for tasks that require compositional reasoning. The number of effective layers reached by looping determines the quality of the solution these models find. Like deep architectures, looped architectures are prone to a signal propagation problem induced by depth as the halting decision is postponed. In this paper, we address this signal propagation issue using pre-norm layers and residual scaling. Building on these architectural modifications, we propose FPRM, a Transformer-based Fixed-Point Reasoning Model that uses fixed-point convergence as an end-to-end halting mechanism in a looped architecture. We show that fixed-point halting allows FPRM to adapt its compute to task difficulty. FPRM is effective on common reasoning benchmarks, namely Sudoku, Maze, state-tracking, and ARC-AGI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at https://github.com/nilskiKonjIzDunava/fprm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:47Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abir Ashab","familyName":"Niloy","name":"Niloy, Abir Ashab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Ryan","name":"Ryan, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imamul Hossain","familyName":"Rafi","name":"Rafi, Imamul Hossain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md","familyName":"Erfan","name":"Erfan, Md","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rayhanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Rayhanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Source Cybersecurity Logs: An ATT\u0026amp;CK-Labeled Dataset and SLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:21:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-stage cyberattacks span system, network, and browser logs. Detecting them requires correlating events across all three sources. Machine learning methods can learn these cross-source patterns, but they need labeled multi-source data. Existing public datasets fall short. Network-only datasets such as CICIDS and UNSW-NB15 miss host and browser activity. Host-focused datasets such as LMDG and CICAPT-IIoT lack browser telemetry. ATLAS includes all three sources but labels events only as malicious or benign, without MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge (ATT\u0026amp;CK) technique granularity. No public dataset combines all three sources with per-entry ATT\u0026amp;CK technique labels. We close the gap by building a multi-source log dataset of 870 sessions (70 attack, 800 benign) and approximately 2.3 million events. We captured system, network, and browser activity simultaneously on Windows endpoints. We labeled malicious events with ATT\u0026amp;CK technique IDs, covering 12 tactics and 53 techniques. We generated all attack data using real tools, including Remote Access Trojan (RAT), Command and Control (C2) tunnels, and cloud exfiltration. To demonstrate learnability, we fine-tuned three Small Language Models (SLMs) (Qwen2.5-1.5B, Llama-3.2-3B, Phi-4-Mini) using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). We compared each against its base variant across ten metrics on two tasks: chunk classification and ATT\u0026amp;CK technique identification. Fine-tuning improved every model on every metric. Chunk classification accuracy rose from approximately 8% in the base variants to between 90% and 97% after fine-tuning. Technique identification remained challenging, with the best exact-match accuracy at 42%, although high partial-match scores show the models captured most of the underlying reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Trojanowski","name":"Trojanowski, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Wojtylak","name":"Wojtylak, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Encoder-Transformer Architecture for Recognition of the Jordan Structure of a Matrix"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:17:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a machine-learning framework for detecting whether a given matrix is a perturbation of a matrix with a large Jordan block. The proposed model achieves high classification accuracy on synthetically generated, robustly perturbed data and outperforms a classical numerical baseline. Moreover, we demonstrate that the learned model generalizes to several classes of matrices not seen during training. These results suggest that the architecture captures structural properties associated with matrix defectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:20Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18166","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18166","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18166","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Ryan","name":"Ryan, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saad Sakib","familyName":"Noor","name":"Noor, Saad Sakib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md","familyName":"Erfan","name":"Erfan, Md","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaswata","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Shaswata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudip","familyName":"Mittal","name":"Mittal, Sudip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rayhanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Rayhanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Open-Source LLMs for Multi-Label ATT\u0026amp;CK Technique Classification on CTI Reports"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classifying Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) using MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge (ATT\u0026amp;CK) is essential for proactive defense, but historically required extensive human effort. Pre-Large Language Model (LLM) automation sped up this process, but could not resolve the complex language and multi-step attack patterns found in unstructured CTI reports. LLMs addressed previous limitations by using contextual reasoning to understand unstructured text. However, current evaluations rely on simplified, single-technique sentences that ignore the complexity of real-world CTI reports, which often leads to inflated performance results. Consequently, the baseline performance of open-source LLMs on complex unstructured CTI reports remains unevaluated. To address this gap, we constructed a ground-truth dataset of 2,076 human-annotated sentences (1,281 technique-positive, 795 negative) from 83 complex unstructured CTI reports. These sentences were mapped to 114 unique ATT\u0026amp;CK techniques using a six-phase annotation process, achieving \\k{appa} = 0.68 inter-annotator agreement. Using this dataset, we evaluated seven open-source LLMs ranging from 8B to 236B parameters across prompt strategy and temperature configurations. The highest-performing LLM achieved a micro-averaged F1 score of 0.22, establishing the empirical baseline for multi-label ATT\u0026amp;CK classification on complex unstructured CTI. Parameter size showed a statistically significant positive correlation with F1 score. Prompt strategy and temperature produced no statistically significant gains across model configurations. These results indicate that current open-source LLMs are insufficient for production-grade ATT\u0026amp;CK classification. The dataset, benchmark, and findings provide a reproducible foundation for future CTI research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18166","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Forner","name":"Forner, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Kühn","name":"Kühn, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Thamm","name":"Thamm, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernd","familyName":"Rosenow","name":"Rosenow, Bernd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Dynamics of Chain-of-Thought State Tracking in a Solvable Transformer Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought generation can turn a multi-step computation into a sequence of locally checkable state updates, but the training dynamics by which transformers acquire such updates remain poorly understood. We study this question in a solvable setting: a simplified one-block transformer trained by supervised next-token prediction on state sequences generated by composing permutations. The architecture separates fixed-lag action retrieval, learned by RoPE attention, from a specialized MLP logic module that applies the retrieved permutation to the current state. Using a statistical-physics mean-field description, we derive dynamics for three order parameters measuring attention retrieval, teacher-matrix alignment, and off-target logic overlap. These equations quantitatively match simulations for the order parameters and, combined with a logit-distribution approximation, qualitatively predict the sharp transition in final rollout accuracy. The analysis reveals staged learning: the logic module first learns a mixed heuristic; attention then locks onto the relevant action, enabling efficient MLP alignment. Together, these results provide a controlled mechanistic account of how attention-based retrieval and MLP-based logic co-develop during chain-of-thought state tracking."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:50Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michèle","familyName":"Finck","name":"Finck, Michèle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Measurement Gap in the Automation of EU Law: Benchmarking Doctrinal Legal Reasoning under the EU AI Act"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models now produce legal text of at least median quality, yet no existing benchmark can evaluate whether they perform doctrinal legal reasoning, which forms the interpretive core of legal work, rather than the ancillary, paralegal tasks that most current legal-AI evaluations measure. This measurement gap is not only methodological but legal: the EU AI Act makes \"appropriate accuracy\" a binding requirement for high-risk AI used in the judicial domain, yet that requirement cannot acquire operational content without the very doctrinal-reasoning benchmark the field lacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18154","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18154","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18154","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumeet Atul","familyName":"Vadhavka","name":"Vadhavka, Sumeet Atul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Cardiac Electrophysiology Digital Twins Through Agentic Discovery of Hybrid Structure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:54:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building personalized cardiac electrophysiology (EP) digital twins requires identifying the appropriate model structure for each patient, not merely fitting parameters. Traditional methods rely on experts to manually prescribe hybrid physics-neural architectures, which requires deep domain expertise and does not transfer across patients. Recent works have applied large language models (LLMs) to generate or act as hybrid models. However, despite their promising generalization capacity, these LLM-based methods lack the structural priors needed for stable cardiac simulations. Hence, we propose LEADS, a framework that formulates cardiac EP domain knowledge as a structured action space and utilizes an LLM agent to discover hybrid models. The agent follows an iterative reasoning-and-action loop to select, combine, and refine hybrid models, whilst gradient descent handles parameter fitting. The proposed LEADS designs every candidate model towards physically grounded, interpretable, and numerically stable, while allowing open-ended architectural discovery. We validate LEADS on synthetic data with three ground-truth reaction models and on real cardiac EP data, demonstrating that it outperforms both human-designed hybrid models and other LLM-based hybrid modeling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18154","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:37Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bianca","familyName":"Emmerich","name":"Emmerich, Bianca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Yvonne","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu Yvonne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitris","familyName":"Spathis","name":"Spathis, Dimitris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"McDuff","name":"McDuff, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cecilia","familyName":"Mascolo","name":"Mascolo, Cecilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WEQA: Wearable hEalth Question Answering with Query-Adaptive Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:45:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models are remarkably capable at medical question answering, in some cases surpassing the accuracy of general physicians. However, answering questions about wearable health data remains challenging and understudied, as these ubiquitous sensors produce continuous, high-dimensional, and longitudinal data, which is non-trivial to align with text-centric distributions in LLM pretraining. The diversity of sensor modalities and user intents cannot be effectively handled by a fixed reasoning workflow or a single pretrained foundation model. To address these challenges, we propose WEQA, a query-adaptive agent framework that unifies LLM reasoning with specialized wearable analytical and modeling tools. An LLM controller is employed to synthesize execution plans and dynamically route each query to the appropriate combination of sensor analysis and pretrained models, and perform grounded response auditing with external knowledge. We also curate a benchmark spanning four open wearable datasets comprising analytic and predictive tasks in three different health domains. Experiments show that our framework is 24% more accurate than LLM and agentic baselines, and a blinded study with 12 medical experts and 8 users shows substantial gains in usefulness and clinical soundness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Polok","name":"Polok, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuele","familyName":"Cornell","name":"Cornell, Samuele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathvik","familyName":"Udupa","name":"Udupa, Sathvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Černocký","name":"Černocký, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukáš","familyName":"Burget","name":"Burget, Lukáš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Grounding Spoken LLMs in Multi-Speaker Audio via Diarization Conditioning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:34:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:06:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose diarization-conditioned spoken language models (SLMs), a strategy for extending SLMs to far-field multi-talker audio. Rather than adapting the decoder via Serialized Output Training, which risks catastrophic forgetting, we condition the acoustic encoder on diarization masks to extract target-speaker representations, keeping the decoder frozen. We instantiate this as Dixtral, integrating a Diarization Conditioned Whisper (DiCoW) encoder into the Voxtral SLM. On AMI, NOTSOFAR-1, LibriSpeechMix, and Mixer6, Dixtral outperforms Gemini 3.0 Flash, VibeVoice, and Voxtral Mini Transcribe V2 on speaker-attributed transcription by 29.0%, 19.8%, and 16.0% absolute cpWER respectively. On a novel long-form multi-speaker QA benchmark, zero-shot Dixtral matches Gemini on far-field content understanding, and when fine-tuned surpasses both Gemini and Voxtral operating on close-talk across all tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abeer","familyName":"Badawi","name":"Badawi, Abeer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moyosoreoluwa","familyName":"Olatosi","name":"Olatosi, Moyosoreoluwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Negin","familyName":"Baghbanzadeh","name":"Baghbanzadeh, Negin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laleh","familyName":"Seyyed-Kalantari","name":"Seyyed-Kalantari, Laleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Rudzicz","name":"Rudzicz, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. Shayna","familyName":"Rosenbaum","name":"Rosenbaum, R. Shayna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Pishdadian","name":"Pishdadian, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elham","familyName":"Dolatabadi","name":"Dolatabadi, Elham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Understanding and Measuring COGNITIVE ATROPHY in LLM Behaviour"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:26:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent incidents involving LLMs used for mental-health support reveal a critical evaluation gap: surface-level safety scores do not capture how models behave across realistic, emotionally sensitive interactions over time. Existing benchmarks measure knowledge, safety, or static response quality, but miss whether LLM interactions help users keep reflecting, coping, and making decisions themselves. We formalize this missing dimension as COGNITIVE ATROPHY, a process-level behavioural measure in AI-mediated mental-health support distinct from safety and helpfulness. To measure it, we introduce COGNITIVE ATROPHY BENCH, a clinically grounded benchmark built from 1,576 fully human-generated counseling conversations, 15,680 turns, and 42,230 responses from five LLMs. Three clinical and neuropsychology experts developed a 20-attribute schema spanning user context, response behaviour, and global risk flags; six trained clinical reviewers applied it with span-grounded evidence, producing 5,324 reviewer judgments. We further introduce the User-Input Risk Index (UIRI), the Cognitive Atrophy Risk Index (ARI), and trajectory summaries. Across five LLMs, models show a consistent moderate-to-high level of atrophy-aligned behaviour across single and multi-turn settings. While models generally respond to overt safety cues, they adapt less reliably when users seek solutions or decisions. The dominant recurring patterns are directive advice, problem-solving, recommendation responses, topic shifts, and forms of validation that may reinforce dependence rather than reflection. Our work makes COGNITIVE ATROPHY measurable and provides a foundation for auditing model behaviour in sensitive LLM conversations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:04Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naz","familyName":"Col","name":"Col, Naz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David M.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, David M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unintended Effects of Geographic Conditioning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:23:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:05:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern conversational AI systems frequently rely on user metadata to localize responses, yet the unintended regional biases introduced by this hidden context remain poorly understood. In this work, we evaluate location leakage: the phenomenon where a model generates geographic references despite receiving a geographically neutral user prompt. Across both creative writing and open-ended Q\u0026amp;A prompts, even state-of-the-art LLMs systematically favor region-specific outputs when exposed to location metadata, with leakage spiking by up to 793 times above baseline (e.g., from 0.04% to 31.7% for Llama 3.1-8B, and 21.3% and 8.8% for Qwen3-8B and Claude Sonnet 4.6, respectively). Our analysis further shows a novel structural conditioning effect: replacing the injected location with the placeholder \"Unknown\" still elevates leakage by up to 72 times above baseline, demonstrating that the user profile frame itself, independent of any geographic content, acts as a generative conditioning signal."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at the Second Workshop on Customizable NLP (CustomNLP4U) at ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:57Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Rashidi","name":"Rashidi, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structural Role Injection in Handlebars-Templated LLM Prompts: Triple-Brace Interpolation, Delimiter Family, and the Limits of HTML Auto-Escaping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; K.6.5; D.4.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:21:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model applications build prompts from templates, and Handlebars is a widely used templating engine and the default prompt-template format in Microsoft Semantic Kernel. Its double-brace {x} expression HTML-escapes the interpolated value and is documented as the safe default; its triple-brace {x} expression inserts the value raw. We show that this choice silently governs an application's exposure to structural role injection, where attacker-controlled data carries chat role delimiters that forge a higher-privilege turn. A model-free analysis establishes the mechanism: Handlebars escaping rewrites angle brackets but not square brackets, colons, or Markdown hashes, so it neutralises ChatML, Llama-3, and XML role delimiters (survival rate 0.00) while leaving Llama-2 [INST], legacy Human:/Assistant:, and Markdown ### delimiters intact (survival rate 1.00 for the last two). We then run 5760 trials across seven delimiter families, two attack objectives, and four models (GPT-3.5 Turbo, GPT-4o mini, GPT-4.1 mini, Claude Haiku 4.5) at a combined API cost of 1.63 USD. GPT-3.5 Turbo follows the task-hijack instruction in 97% of raw and 91% of escaped trials, with the escaping protection concentrated in the angle-bracket families and absent for the colon- and Markdown-based families; the harder secret-exfiltration objective, which does not saturate, exposes the same family interaction more cleanly. Claude Haiku 4.5 resists both objectives almost entirely. The escaped default protects only the delimiter schemes whose characters HTML escaping happens to cover, gives no protection for the rest, and cannot substitute for a structural separation of instruction and data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P. A.","familyName":"Estevez","name":"Estevez, P. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Espejo-Moreira","name":"Espejo-Moreira, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Sanfeliu-Alvarez","name":"Sanfeliu-Alvarez, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Forster","name":"Forster, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. M. Munoz","familyName":"Arancibia","name":"Arancibia, A. M. Munoz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Cabrera-Vives","name":"Cabrera-Vives, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F. E.","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, F. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Bayo","name":"Bayo, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Catelan","name":"Catelan, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Dastidar","name":"Dastidar, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Hernandez-Garcia","name":"Hernandez-Garcia, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. A.","familyName":"Intriago","name":"Intriago, J. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Pignata","name":"Pignata, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Querying an astronomical database using large language models: the ALeRCE text-to-SQL system"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a text-to-SQL (structured query language) system based on large language models (LLMs) using in-context learning and apply it to the Automatic Learning for the Rapid Classification of Events (ALeRCE) astronomical database. ALeRCE is a community broker for the Zwicky Transient Facility and the Vera C. Rubin Observatory. The system enables users to query the database in natural language (NL) and generates executable SQL queries. To develop and evaluate the system, we constructed a dataset of 110 NL/SQL pairs. We propose a step-by-step generation framework comprising four modules: schema linking, query classification, prompt decomposition, and self-correction. The performance of thirteen LLMs is evaluated using in-context learning and prompt engineering techniques. Text-to-SQL performance is assessed using the perfect-match (PM) rate for row identifiers (e.g., object identifiers) and column identifiers (i.e., column names). The proposed step-by-step framework consistently outperforms a direct-inference baseline, while the self-correction module consistently reduces execution errors. For Claude Opus 4.6, PM performance on row (column) identifiers is high for simple queries, reaching 0.97 (0.94), and decreases with query complexity to 0.44 (0.72) for medium queries and 0.59 (0.49) for hard queries. Among the thirteen evaluated models, the best-performing LLMs for the text-to-SQL task are Claude Opus 4.6, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Gemini 3 Flash, and GPT-5.2-Codex."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:36Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18103","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18103","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18103","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah J.","familyName":"Kim-Baumann","name":"Kim-Baumann, Noah J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torsten","familyName":"Hiltmann","name":"Hiltmann, Torsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HistoRAG: Embedding Historical Methodology in Retrieval-Augmented Generation Through Critical Technical Practice"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.7; J.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the prevailing architecture for grounding language model outputs in external evidence, yet its dominant evaluation paradigms and default configurations remain oriented toward factual question-answering. For interpretive disciplines such as historical studies, RAG embeds assumptions that conflict with scholarly practice. We introduce HistoRAG, a framework that translates historiographical principles into concrete architectural interventions. Separated retrieval and generation decouples source discovery from interpretation, temporal windowing enforces balanced source representation across the research period as a methodological requirement of historical inquiry, and LLM-as-judge evaluation makes relevance judgments transparent and contestable. We evaluate these interventions using SPIEGELragged, applied to 102,189 articles from Der Spiegel (1950-1979). Each intervention addresses a measurable deficiency in standard RAG: era-specific vocabulary retrieves zero chunks from the 1950s when using 1970s terminology, evidence of the temporal skew that motivates windowing; vector similarity and LLM-assessed relevance correlate only weakly (Spearman rho = 0.275), motivating post-retrieval evaluation; and keyword-based and semantic retrieval surface largely disjoint source pools, motivating an architecture in which both operate as complementary retrieval layers under a shared LLM evaluation filter. We also introduce the concept of Zwischentexte (intermediate texts that function as interpretive proposals rather than findings) as a framework for responsible integration of LLM-generated text into scholarly practice. The architecture offers a model for how domain-specific epistemological commitments can be translated into RAG design decisions, and may transfer to other interpretive disciplines working with large corpora."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 6 figures. Companion preprint to a Journal of Digital History notebook article (under review)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18103","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elliot","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Elliot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Knottenbelt","name":"Knottenbelt, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IsabeLLM: Automated Theorem Proving Applied to Formally Verifying Consensus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:00:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in Artificial Intelligence (AI) have led AI for Theorem Proving to become a promising means of formally verifying computer systems. Whilst formal verification is traditionally reserved for safety-critical systems due to the required amount of expertise and effort, AI can help to automate a large amount of this workload and make it far more accessible. Blockchain-based systems are becoming increasingly popular and are frequently targeted by malicious actors, often resulting in huge financial losses, highlighting the need to better verify these systems and mitigate vulnerabilities. Arguably the most important component of these systems is the consensus protocol, which allows nodes to agree on decisions in a potentially adversarial environment. In this paper, we improve upon IsabeLLM, the automated theorem proving tool in Isabelle. Namely, we implement a Retrieval-Augmented Generation framework, Error tracing and counterexample generation for improved context supplied to the Large Language Model. Compatibility with the latest version of Isabelle and Sledgehammer is also implemented for improved efficiency. We compare the performance of the two versions of IsabeLLM in their ability to complete the verification of Bitcoin's Proof of Work consensus."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:24Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18075","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18075","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18075","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Antong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Framework for Context-Aware and Relation-Aware Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792720","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a paradigm for enhancing large language models (LLMs) with external knowledge, yet existing graph-based methods face a fundamental limitation: entity-centric and chunk-centric approaches operate on representations anchored to original text without true knowledge fusion. While entity-centric methods connect logically related content and chunk-centric methods preserve context, both retrieve information separately through similarity search, missing emergent understanding from their synthesis. In this paper, we propose HyGRAG, a hierarchical graph RAG framework that transcends source documents by addressing three core challenges: constructing summaries that genuinely integrate contextual and relational information, leveraging these synthesized representations to access emergent knowledge during retrieval, and efficiently updating hierarchical structures for dynamic corpora. Specifically, we design hierarchical index structures over hybrid graphs with both chunk and entity nodes, then iteratively cluster them and generate LLM-based summaries. Then, we design context and relation-aware retrieval that searches across all abstraction levels while expanding through community membership. Moreover, we enable dynamic knowledge update through attachment-based algorithms with only local re-summarization. Experimental results show that HyGRAG improves the average accuracy of multi-hop reasoning tasks by 9.7%, while maintaining reasonable efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at The ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18075","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divyansh","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Divyansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Shreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshul","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Anshul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajkumar","familyName":"Buyya","name":"Buyya, Rajkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic AI-based Framework for Mitigating Premature Diagnostic Handoff and Silent Hallucination in Healthcare Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:39:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:03:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) and multi-agent systems have driven the rise of Agentic AI, showing promise for medical reasoning. However, open-ended conversational agents remain prone to two critical failure modes: premature diagnostic handoff and silent clinical hallucinations that may go undetected before reaching the patient. In this work, we propose a multi-agent framework that addresses both issues by replacing ``LLM-as-a-judge'' routing with deterministic orchestration constraints. The framework incorporates two safety mechanisms. First, a neuro-symbolic state-tracking gate enforces completeness of the OLDCARTS clinical protocol (Onset, Location, Duration, Character, Aggravating/Alleviating factors, Radiation, Timing, and Severity) by blocking diagnostic transitions until all required dimensions are collected. Second, an epistemic uncertainty quantification (UQ) gate computes semantic entropy (H) across K=5 independent diagnostic samples to identify and intercept divergent outputs before delivery.\n We evaluate the system using simulated patient agents powered by the llama-3.1-70b-instruct model on 150 test cases. The full architecture achieves 49.3% diagnostic precision, representing an absolute improvement of 11.3 percentage points over an unconstrained baseline. Additionally, we observe a statistically significant negative correlation (r = -0.181, p \u0026lt; 0.05) between OLDCARTS completeness (σ) and semantic entropy (H), suggesting that structured information gathering is associated with reduced diagnostic uncertainty."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruman","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangting","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When LLMs Analyze Scars: From Images to Clinically-Meaningful Features"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical image classification faces a fundamental dilemma: while deep learning models achieve remarkable performance at scale, real-world clinical scenarios often suffer from severe data scarcity due to annotation costs, privacy constraints, and disease rarity. This challenge is particularly pronounced in pathological scar classification, where differentiating keloids from hypertrophic scars requires subtle expert knowledge and labeled images are extremely limited. We propose a novel paradigm that repositions large language models (LLMs) as knowledge-driven feature engineers rather than end-to-end classifiers. We call this framework ScaFE (Scar Feature Engineering). Our key insight is that LLMs encode rich medical knowledge that can be externalized as executable feature extraction code, enabling the transformation of high-dimensional images into low-dimensional, clinically interpretable representations. Specifically, we prompt an LLM with established scar assessment criteria to generate deterministic Python code that extracts features aligned with clinical scoring systems such as the Vancouver Scar Scale. Our approach offers three key advantages: (1) data efficiency, achieving robust performance with limited training samples by decoupling knowledge acquisition from statistical learning; (2) privacy preservation, as raw images are processed locally without exposure to external LLMs; and (3) interpretability, through explicit features grounded in clinical reasoning. Extensive experiments on scar classification demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms end-to-end deep learning baselines or using LLMs as black-box classifiers under limited data conditions, establishing a promising direction for integrating LLMs into data-efficient and clinically transparent medical AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:38Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18062","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18062","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18062","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hobin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Akgul","name":"Akgul, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lujo","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Lujo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Christin","name":"Christin, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Security and Privacy Prompts in the Wild: What Users Ask LLMs and How LLMs Respond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:37:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are widely used to fulfill users' information needs; users ask LLMs about the weather, pose educational questions, and consult them for legal assistance. One particularly understudied area is digital security and privacy (S\u0026amp;P), where users may seek LLMs' help on how to secure their online accounts or protect their computers from cyber attacks. To the best of our knowledge, no prior study has collected or analyzed the S\u0026amp;P questions users ask LLMs; prior research on LLM response quality relied on expert-authored S\u0026amp;P misconceptions or FAQs rather than user queries. Drawing from WildChat, a dataset of 3.2M user-LLM conversations collected in the wild, our study identifies 14,727 S\u0026amp;P prompts and categorizes them into nine categories covering a wide range of S\u0026amp;P topics. From the S\u0026amp;P prompts, we sampled 450 and performed a thematic analysis to characterize the S\u0026amp;P questions users ask LLMs. Separate from the thematic analysis, we curated 270 advice-seeking S\u0026amp;P prompts, where users ask for recommendations, guidance, or specific S\u0026amp;P information. We measured LLM response quality and consistency when posing the prompt to LLMs 10 times. We found that commercial LLMs outperform open-weight models (GPT 5.5 provided \"good enough\" responses on 98% of prompts; Llama 4 on 47%). However, among prompts that received high-quality responses on average, commercial models sometimes produce contradictory responses across runs, risking confusing or misleading users."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18062","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:37Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18054","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18054","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18054","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prad","familyName":"Kadambi","name":"Kadambi, Prad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Goldinger","name":"Goldinger, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Visar","familyName":"Berisha","name":"Berisha, Visar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimberly D.","familyName":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Kimberly D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julie","familyName":"Liss","name":"Liss, Julie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI-based Cognitive-linguistic Features for Dementia Assessment in Picture Description"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:32:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Picture descriptions provide valuable insights into several clinical constructs related to cognitive-linguistic abilities. However, operationalizing these constructs into quantitative measures remains challenging, limiting interpretability and clinical utility. We introduced seven constructs tailored to the Cookie Theft picture description task and prompted large language models (LLMs) to evaluate them, generating severity scores and example-based explanations. Among the examined LLMs, Claude 3.5 Sonnet performed the best, producing severity scores that significantly distinguish cognitively impaired individuals from healthy controls. The model achieves a high accuracy of 85% on the ADReSS dataset. Expert evaluation of Claude's scores and explanations yields a 3.99/5 average agreement. The findings demonstrate the potential of LLMs to operationalize clinical constructs and generate interpretable evaluations, offering a promising approach for accessible cognitive screening tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18054","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueping","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xueping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Skill Routing for LLM Agents: Decompose, Retrieve, and Compose"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:27:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents increasingly rely on external skills -- reusable tool specifications -- but real-world tasks often require composing multiple skills, not just selecting one. We formalize this as the Compositional Skill Routing problem: given a complex user query and a large skill library, decompose the query into atomic sub-tasks, retrieve the appropriate skill for each sub-task, and compose an executable plan. We present SkillWeaver, a decompose-retrieve-compose framework combining an LLM task decomposer, a bi-encoder skill retriever with FAISS indexing, and a dependency-aware DAG planner. To support evaluation, we introduce CompSkillBench, a benchmark of 300 compositional queries over 2,209 real MCP server skills spanning 24 functional categories, sourced from the public MCP ecosystem. Our experiments reveal that task decomposition quality is the primary bottleneck: standard LLM decomposition reaches only 34.2% category recall at the step level. To address this, we propose Iterative Skill-Aware Decomposition (SAD), a retrieval-augmented feedback loop that iteratively aligns decomposition with available skills. SAD improves decomposition accuracy from 51.0% to 67.7% (+32.7%, Wilcoxon p \u0026lt; 10^-6) in a single iteration; DA-conditioned analysis confirms that correct granularity is the prerequisite for effective retrieval (CatR@1 rises from 34% to 41% when DA=1). SkillWeaver reduces context window consumption by over 99%, and transfer experiments confirm generalization (+35.6% relative DA gain even when target categories are absent from the retrieval pool)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fred","familyName":"Philippy","name":"Philippy, Fred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Siwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacques","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Jacques","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tegawendé F.","familyName":"Bissyandé","name":"Bissyandé, Tegawendé F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When English Isn't the Best Teacher: Source Language Effects in Cross-Lingual In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:09:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:00:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-lingual transfer in multilingual NLP has been widely explored in supervised fine-tuning contexts, where factors like data availability and linguistic similarity largely determine transfer quality. As the field shifts toward few-shot In-Context Learning (ICL), it is often presumed that insights from fine-tuning carry over unchanged. Yet this assumption has not been rigorously evaluated, leaving open the question of how to choose source languages for cross-lingual ICL. We conduct a broad empirical study of cross-lingual transfer in ICL spanning seven tasks, six models, and a typologically diverse set of languages. We further analyze language confusion, a key obstacle for generative tasks in cross-lingual ICL. Our results show that conventional fine-tuning-based expectations do not consistently apply in the ICL regime and point to alternative heuristics for selecting source languages effectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at 1st Workshop on Multilinguality in the Era of Large Language Models (MeLLM 2026), co-located with ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:59Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franziska","familyName":"Braun","name":"Braun, Franziska","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alea","familyName":"Rüggeberg","name":"Rüggeberg, Alea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Ranzenberger","name":"Ranzenberger, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hartmut","familyName":"Lehfeld","name":"Lehfeld, Hartmut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hillemacher","name":"Hillemacher, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Bocklet","name":"Bocklet, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Korbinian","familyName":"Riedhammer","name":"Riedhammer, Korbinian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reading between the Lines: Leveraging Large Language Models for Global Dementia and Depression Assessment from Clinical Interviews"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:01:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dementia and depression are the most prevalent neuropsychiatric disorders in geriatric populations, and their overlapping symptoms pose major challenges for differential diagnosis. In this study, we investigate open-weights Large Language Models (LLMs) for predicting dementia and depression severity from speech samples collected during standardized history taking interviews with 154 German-speaking subjects. We introduce an observer-based Global Depression Scale (GDS-D) aligned with the established Global Deterioration Scale (GDS), enabling parallel global staging of affective and cognitive symptoms. We compare three LLMs (Mistral 3.1, DeepHermes, Qwen3) in two settings: (1) zero-shot prediction and (2) LLM-based feature extraction for Support Vector Regression, using human and pause-enriched transcripts. Results show that LLMs effectively predict depression severity in zero-shot settings (best MAE of 0.60), while dementia assessment benefits substantially from structured feature extraction (best MAE of 0.78), reducing errors by up to 35% over zero-shot baselines. Pause-enriched transcripts achieve competitive performance with human transcriptions, demonstrating the viability of fully automatic screening pipelines for differential neuropsychiatric assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in Text, Speech and Dialogue (TSD 2026). The final authenticated publication will be available online via Springer LNCS/LNAI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:41Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:10:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manon","familyName":"Reusens","name":"Reusens, Manon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofie","familyName":"Goethals","name":"Goethals, Sofie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Martens","name":"Martens, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Consumer Behavior Theory: Foundations of a Novel Research Field"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:51:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:59:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as autonomous agents that make consumption decisions on behalf of users. This shift raises fundamental questions for consumer theory, which has traditionally modeled humans as the primary decision-makers. In this paper, we introduce LLM Consumer Behavior Theory, a new field of study concerned with analyzing consumer behavior in agentic markets. Drawing on classical and behavioral economics alongside recent advances in Natural Language Processing, we formalize how human preferences are reflected and acted upon by LLM-based agents, and how agent-level decisions aggregate into market demand. We unify previously fragmented literature on LLM decision-making, human behavior simulation, and preference elicitation under a common economic lens, highlighting where assumptions, such as rationality and heterogeneity, may fail in agentic markets. Rather than providing empirical validation, this paper outlines the scope of LLM consumer behavior and identifies open research questions related to alignment, preference representation, and market dynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:10:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cosimo","familyName":"Gregucci","name":"Gregucci, Cosimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Obaidah","familyName":"Theeb","name":"Theeb, Obaidah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hernandez","name":"Hernandez, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Vergari","name":"Vergari, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steffen","familyName":"Staab","name":"Staab, Steffen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Half a Link can Be Enough to Predict a Whole Link: Understanding Generalization in Knowledge Graph Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge graph (KG) foundation models (KGFMs) are zero-shot generalizers: trained once, they can predict links on unseen graphs without retraining. However, understanding when and how they can robustly generalize across KGs is still an open question. In this paper, we shed some light on their generalization mechanisms highlighting how their performance on unseen KGs is not uniform when it comes to partially seen links, which we call half-links. In fact, we show that to predict a test triple $(h,r,t)$ it might suffice in practice to have observed the half-link $(h,r)$ or $(r,t)$ in the inference graph. This yields a taxonomy of four scenarios when combinations of these half-links are observed or not. In a rigorous stratified analysis over these scenarios, we reveal that SoTA KGFMs use seen half links for predictions, while unseen half-links pose different challenges. As such, our finer-grained taxonomy can be a diagnostic protocol for robust KGFM generalization and highlights where novel KGFMs can improve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:10:17Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:10:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:10:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Salviati","name":"Salviati, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"De Gaspari","name":"De Gaspari, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Conti","name":"Conti, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi Vincenzo","familyName":"Mancini","name":"Mancini, Luigi Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ShellGames: Speculative LLM-Driven SSH Deception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cyber deception and Moving Target Defense are promising strategies that aim to disrupt adversaries by increasing uncertainty. However, sustaining long-lived, credible interactive sessions with adversaries remains an open challenge. Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a promising path toward more dynamic deception systems, but suffer from key limitations that fundamentally limit their applicability, including: lack of persistent state, output inconsistencies, hallucinations, latency, and susceptibility to behavioral subversion that may reveal the deception.\n We propose ShellGames, an SSH shell simulator based on LLM designed to address these limitations. ShellGames combines five complementary techniques: (i) Automatic Chain-of-Thought and few-shot learning to improve correctness; (ii) memory management to maintain system state coherency; (iii) speculative command execution to reduce response latency; (iv) smart routing of complex interactive commands to a sandboxed environment; and (v) subversion detection leveraging the constrained input-output domain of shell environments. To enable systematic evaluation, we introduce a standardized benchmarking protocol and dataset spanning correctness, consistency, state tracking, and robustness tasks. ShellGames achieves $0.898$ command accuracy on correctness ($+5.3pp$ over baselines), $0.918$ sequence-level accuracy on consistency ($+36pp$), $0.98$ state tracking accuracy ($+18.3pp$), and $0.95$ accuracy on robustness ($+37pp$). A user study with $n=20$ participants confirms that ShellGames achieves realism comparable to a real shell under free exploration and outperforms traditional honeypots on perceived command coverage."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:57Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guofa","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunyang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kunyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicui","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yicui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Keqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gaussian Light Field Splatting: A Physical Prior-Driven Vision Transformer for Unsupervised Low-Light Image Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:37:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing unsupervised low-light image enhancement methods often encounter local exposure imbalance and color distortion under complex non-uniform illumination. In addition, most Vision Transformers lack an explicit mechanism for modeling the physical priors of illumination degradation. To address these limitations, we propose GLFS, a Gaussian light field splatting-based Vision Transformer that integrates continuous physical illumination modeling from Gaussian splatting into the Transformer architecture. In GLFS, scene illumination is represented by a superposition of anisotropic Gaussian basis functions. Physics-guided biases are introduced into self-attention to adaptively infer a spatial gain field, enabling accurate and uniform restoration under complex illumination. To reduce color bias and structural degradation during enhancement, a color-vector angular loss and a luminance-edge loss are further developed. These losses enforce hue consistency and improve the structural fidelity of local details. Extensive ablation studies and quantitative evaluations show that GLFS provides clear advantages in illumination correction and detail preservation. It achieves state-of-the-art performance and offers a new representation paradigm for low-light image enhancement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:56Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Tieleman","name":"Tieleman, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel J.","familyName":"Bell","name":"Bell, Samuel J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas D.","familyName":"Hull","name":"Hull, Thomas D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caitlin A.","familyName":"Stamatis","name":"Stamatis, Caitlin A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-tuning LLMs for Passive Depression Severity Estimation from AI Mental Health Dialogue"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:28:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:57:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Depression is the leading cause of disability worldwide, and early detection of symptom change is essential for timely intervention. Validated instruments such as the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9) support symptom monitoring at scale, but real-world completion rates are low, introducing response bias and systematic missingness. Passive approaches that infer severity from routinely generated data could close this gap. We address this by predicting PHQ-9 total scores directly from transcripts of conversations between users and an AI mental health application, requiring only conversation text and no additional clinical data. We fine-tune a Qwen3.5-27B backbone with a regression head, augment 3,111 ground-truth labels with pseudolabels generated by a reasoning model (Claude Opus) and iteratively trained intermediate models, for a combined dataset of 6,283 users. On a held-out test set of 842 users, our best model achieves MAE = 2.6, RMSE = 4.0, Pearson r = 0.80, and AUC = 0.91 at the PHQ-9 \u0026gt;= 10 clinical threshold. We also find AUC \u0026gt; 0.87 at every severity threshold from PHQ-9 \u0026gt;= 3 to PHQ-9 \u0026gt;= 24, demonstrating that the model captures depression severity across the full clinical spectrum. This work opens the door to passive, continuous symptom monitoring in AI mental health platforms, without requiring users to complete self-report measures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:41Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Aruta","name":"Aruta, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vadim","familyName":"Malvone","name":"Malvone, Vadim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniello","familyName":"Murano","name":"Murano, Aniello","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Domenico","familyName":"Parente","name":"Parente, Domenico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Rizzuti","name":"Rizzuti, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Neuro-Symbolic Approach to Strategy Synthesis for Strategic Logics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:13:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning about what agents can achieve through strategic interaction is a core challenge in Multi-Agent Systems (MAS). Logics for strategic ability, such as ATL, provide rigorous methods, but their adoption is often hindered by the computational cost of strategy synthesis. We introduce a neuro-symbolic framework that integrates large language models (LLMs) into the model-checking pipeline for MAS. The LLM acts as a strategy-generation oracle, proposing candidate strategies that are then formally validated by a standard MAS model checker. This generate-and-certify architecture uses LLM guidance to navigate large combinatorial strategy spaces while preserving formal soundness: generated strategies are accepted only when certified by the verifier. We instantiate the framework for bounded strategic reasoning in NatATL and introduce the first NatATL strategy-synthesis dataset, consisting of 4211 instances. Experiments with an open-weight Qwen3-32B model show that our certified pipeline achieves 92\\% accuracy on strategy-synthesis outcomes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17958","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17958","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17958","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Visual Cues: CoT-Enhanced Reasoning for Semi-supervised Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:10:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semi-supervised medical image segmentation has emerged as a dominant research problem in medical image analysis, mitigating annotation scarcity by leveraging consistency regularization on unlabeled data. However, existing approaches operate predominantly via visual pattern matching, relying heavily on pixel-level similarities. This visual-centric dependency often falters in clinical scenarios characterized by the visual-semantic mismatch, where visually similar lesions warrant distinct diagnostic conclusions, thus failing to capture the underlying diagnostic logic used by experts. To address this, we move beyond visual cues and propose CERS (CoT-Enhanced Reasoning Segmentation), a framework that integrates Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to distinguish pathologically distinct cases. Specifically, we construct a knowledge pool enriched with linguistic reasoning descriptions generated by large language models (LLMs). A semantic-aware reference selection strategy is introduced to identify historical evidence, filtering candidates first by morphology, and then refining them via CoT consistency to eliminate hard negatives. Furthermore, a multi-scale coordinate attention module (MCAM) is designed to effectively fuse this reasoning-derived context into the decoding process. Extensive experiments demonstrate the superiority of CERS against state-of-the-art approaches, particularly in resolving boundary ambiguities and semantic inconsistencies. The code is available at https://github.com/cymasuna/CERS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MICCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17958","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyu","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Xuanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runke","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Runke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qingyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MLLMs Get It Right, Then Get It Wrong: Tracing and Correcting Late-Layer Textual Bias"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:05:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When vision contradicts text, multimodal large language models (MLLMs) consistently favor text, even when images provide clear evidence otherwise. This bias poses risks for applications requiring visual grounding, yet its cause remains unclear. In this paper, we uncover a surprising finding: models often get it right initially, forming correct vision-based predictions in their intermediate layers, before changing their minds and favoring text in the final output. We call this \"late-layer textual override\". The visual information is encoded, it simply does not survive to the output. More intriguingly, we find that how predictions change reveals whether they're correct: 85% of failures shift toward text, while 89% of successes shift toward vision. This directional signature enables a simple but powerful intervention: when we detect a confident visual prediction being suppressed, we restore it. We propose CALRD (Conflict-Aware Layer Reference Decoding), a training-free method that recovers overridden predictions at inference time. Experiments across five MLLMs of varying architectures demonstrate up to 9.4% absolute improvements on conflict benchmarks while largely preserving standard performance, without training or external knowledge. It recovers what the model already knew but failed to preserve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at IJCAI 2026. 16 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikołaj","familyName":"Zasada","name":"Zasada, Mikołaj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Łukasz","familyName":"Struski","name":"Struski, Łukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacek","familyName":"Tabor","name":"Tabor, Jacek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcin","familyName":"Kurdziel","name":"Kurdziel, Marcin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoftMoE: Soft Differentiable Routing for Mixture-of-Experts in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:05:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures enable scaling LLM parameters under a fixed inference budget by activating only a small subset of experts via top-$k$ routing. While this preserves causality and suits autoregressive language models, the discrete top-$k$ operator is not differentiable, forcing a fixed number of active experts per input and resulting in inefficient use of computation. We propose SoftMoE, which replaces discrete routing with a truncated soft top-$k$ LapSum relaxation, allowing gradient-based optimization of expert routing. We further parameterize the mean number of active experts per layer and impose a global budget constraint, enabling the model to learn how to allocate expert capacity across layers. SoftMoE remains fully compatible with autoregressive modeling and achieves performance comparable to or better than sparse MoE on language modeling and downstream tasks, while activating significantly fewer experts. Notably, the learned allocation is highly non-uniform, with later layers activating more experts. The source code is publicly available$^\\dagger$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:13Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivor W.","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Ivor W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuetao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuetao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Plug-and-Adapt: Multimodal Coreference Resolution at First Sight with a Pretrained Alignment Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual information helps resolve ambiguity in coreference resolution, leading to notable performance gains. However, existing Multi-modal Coreference Resolution (MCR) methods require training with (partially) annotated data from the target dataset before they can be applied, preventing their direct usability and raising concerns about generalization. While Vision-Language Large Models (VLLMs) with billions of parameters offer promising zero-shot capabilities, they remain largely inaccessible. Their massive size limits deployability, and many are only accessible through paid APIs. In this paper, we propose a plug-and-adapt method that strategically adapts a carefully pre-trained \\emph{alignment model} for immediate use in MCR tasks, designed to eliminate the need for training on scarce benchmark datasets or relying on resource-intensive VLLMs. Specifically, we first pre-train a fine-grained alignment model between textual and visual contextual information using vision-language alignment datasets. We then repurpose the alignment model to MCR through similarity aggregation by fusing visual and categorical cues with evidence theory, thereby enhancing effectiveness. Experiments on the Coreference Image Narratives (CIN) benchmark dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, achieving a 5.31\\% and 2.12\\% improvement in CoNLL F1 over SOTA dedicated methods and popular VLLMs, respectively. We further evaluate our method on a masked CIN dataset for robustness testing and on a specially constructed VCR-MCR dataset for generalization assessment, with results confirming both capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17949","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17949","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17949","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Da","name":"Da, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelia","familyName":"Kalyvianaki","name":"Kalyvianaki, Evangelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RouteBalance: Fused Model Routing and Load Balancing for Heterogeneous LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.2.4; C.5.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:02:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heterogeneous LLM serving stacks split scheduling into two layers that optimize in isolation: model routers pick a model from quality and cost signals while ignoring instance load, and serving load balancers optimize queues while ignoring quality. We present RouteBalance, a serving-aware scheduling layer that fuses both into a single online assignment over concrete model instances, jointly trading off quality, latency, and cost. A batched in-process predictor stack and dead-reckoned instance state keep the joint decision cheap on the request hot path ($\\approx$32 ms at 12 req/s). On a 13-instance, 28-GPU heterogeneous cluster serving four model sizes, a single deployed RouteBalance stack traces the upper region of the three-way quality-cost-throughput frontier. Sweeping one weight vector reaches both the highest routing-decision quality (DeepEval $0.419$, $+0.013$ over the strongest baseline, $95\\%$ CI $[{+}0.005,{+}0.022]$; the ordering holds when a second judge re-scores the actually served text) and, at its cost-priority corner, per-request cost that ties the cheapest baseline. With router engineering equalized against concurrent-scoring baseline variants we build, its balanced preset serves at $2.8$ s and $30$ req/s, leading $2.6$ to $4.1\\times$ ahead of enhanced BEST-Route at high load. (Deploying those routers as published, one serial scoring call per request, makes them collapse $23\\times$ under load, a deployment-architecture effect we isolate separately, not the routing result.) A four-arm isolation shows the benefit follows from pricing latency at model-selection time; the learned predictors contribute calibration and SLO headroom rather than the headline frontier. Code: https://github.com/AKafakA/route-balance"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17949","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:09Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PreAct: Computer-Using Agents that Get Faster on Repeated Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:40:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-using agents drive real software through the screen -- clicking and typing -- but they solve every task from scratch: asked to repeat a task, an agent re-reads the screen, re-reasons every tap, and pays the full cost again. We present PreAct, which lets such an agent get faster on tasks it has done before. The first time it succeeds, PreAct compiles the run into a small state-machine program-states that check the screen, transitions that act-and on later runs replays it directly instead of invoking the agent 8.5-13x faster, with no per-step language-model calls. Replay is not blind: at each step PreAct checks that the screen matches what the program expects before acting, and hands control back to the agent the moment something is off.\n PreAct applies the same discipline when deciding what to keep: a freshly compiled program enters the store only if, re-run from a clean state, an independent evaluator confirms it solved the task-catching programs that replay to their last step yet leave the task undone. Across a mobile, a desktop, and a web benchmark, this store-time check separates repeated runs that improve from ones that degrade as faulty programs accumulate, worth 1.75-2.6 tasks per benchmark, the same direction on all three; a fallback that explores afresh when no program fits brings PreAct level with a strong record-and-replay baseline. We also report what did not matter: prompt wording, runtime guardrails, and whether a language model or a plain embedding retriever selects which program to reuse."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aueaphum","familyName":"Aueawatthanaphisut","name":"Aueawatthanaphisut, Aueaphum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Badri Raj","familyName":"Lamichhane","name":"Lamichhane, Badri Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trustworthy Self-Composable Big-Data-as-a-Service: An LLM-Orchestrated Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Data Engineering, AutoML, MLOps Deployment, and Drift-Aware Lifecycle Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:34:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Big-Data-as-a-Service (BDaaS) platforms require re liable automation across data ingestion, cleaning, feature engi neering, model development, deployment, and post-deployment monitoring. However, existing LLM-based data science agents and AutoML systems mainly focus on isolated workflow stages, leaving limited support for lifecycle-level orchestration, artifact governance, human oversight, and drift-aware adaptation. This paper proposes a trustworthy self-composable BDaaS frame work based on LLM-orchestrated multi-agent collaboration. The proposed architecture decomposes the BDaaS lifecycle into specialized agents for data ingestion, data cleaning, feature engineering, AutoML training, model evaluation, MLOps de ployment, monitoring, and drift detection. A central LLM or chestration layer coordinates agent execution, validates interme diate outputs, manages workflow context, and enables dynamic workflow composition. The framework also incorporates shared artifact governance, reproducibility support, human-in-the-loop checkpoints, and drift-aware feedback loops. A prototype-based evaluation is conducted using controlled tabular benchmark datasets with missing values, categorical variables, outliers, class imbalance, and simulated covariate drift. Compared with manual ML, AutoML-only, and single-agent LLM baselines, the pro posed multi-agent BDaaS pipeline achieves competitive predictive performance while improving lifecycle-level reliability, including workflow completion, artifact traceability, deployment readiness, reproducibility, and drift recovery. The results suggest that LLM-orchestrated multi-agent systems can extend conventional AutoML toward trustworthy, adaptive, and production-oriented BDaaS lifecycle automation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17905","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17905","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17905","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peixian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Peixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaorui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyan","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Xueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChLogic: Evaluating Robustness of Logical Reasoning in Chinese Expressions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:28:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:53:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models perform increasingly well on standardized logical reasoning benchmarks, but whether this ability remains robust beyond English is unclear. We introduce ChLogic, an English--Chinese aligned benchmark that tests whether models preserve logical reasoning performance when the same latent logical structure is expressed in English and diverse Chinese surface realizations. Built from formal logical templates, the benchmark contains three data sets: (i) the General aligned set, derived from 60 General Propositions across nine template families; (ii) the Difficult aligned set, derived from 40 Difficult Problems; and (iii) the Chinese-only set, covering 15 language-specific phenomenon types. Each aligned item pairs one English reference expression with five Chinese realizations. Experiments on Qwen3, Ministral, and GLM models reveal a persistent English--Chinese performance gap. Back-translation from standard Chinese into English often improves performance on the General aligned set, but produces mixed effects on the Difficult aligned set, where Qwen3-32B and GLM-5.1 perform worse after translation. These results indicate that Chinese surface realization, translation artifacts, and model-specific behavior jointly affect multilingual logical reasoning. Overall, ChLogic provides a useful stress test for the robustness of multilingual reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17905","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:11Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17872","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17872","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17872","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Lao","name":"Lao, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AnchorKV: Safety-Aware KV Cache Compression via Soft Penalty with a Refusal Anchor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:43:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:51:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) outperform earlier architectures on generative inference and long-context tasks, but their large size introduces significant challenges in memory usage, energy cost, and on-device deployment. Since scaling pre-trained language models improves downstream capability \\cite{zhao2023survey}, the key-value (KV) cache becomes a dominant inference bottleneck. Recent KV cache compression methods \\cite{jo2025fastkv,li2024snapkv,zhou2024dynamickv} reduce this cost by retaining only a subset of attention-relevant tokens. However, while these approaches preserve accuracy on benign workloads, their compression policies either fail to defend against jailbreak attacks \\cite{jiang2024robustkv} or degrade safety alignment under aggressive eviction.\n We propose AnchorKV, a drop-in modification to KV cache compression that biases token retention scores away from directions in key space associated with harmful prompts. AnchorKV constructs an offline safety anchor by adapting a difference-of-means representation engineering approach \\cite{arditi2024refusal,zou2023representation} to the layer-specific key projection space used in KV caching. Based on this anchor, a soft penalty token selection rule trades a small amount of utility for substantially improved safety alignment, while reducing to the original compressor when the penalty is zero."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17872","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:07:29Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:07:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:07:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxiong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mengjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujiao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yujiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StepGuard: Guarding Web Navigation via Single-Step Calibration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:42:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:51:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Web navigation requires agents to follow natural language goals, interact with web pages, and produce accurate answers. While recent advances leverage vision-language models and reinforcement learning, existing methods still suffer from single-step fragility due to reward misalignment and error propagation. To tackle the reward entanglement, we design Dynamic Dual-Policy Optimization (DDPO), which dynamically switches between a navigation-first mode for exploration and an answer-first mode for question-answering to mitigate reward conflict. To calibrate the single-step error, we propose Confidence-Guided Adaptive Navigation Reflection (CANR), a mechanism that estimates per-step confidence, triggers reflection only when necessary, and uses contrastive rewards to encourage self-correction to calibrate the single-step inaccuracy. With the above as the main components, we finally develop our StepGuard, a new framework of Guarding Web Navigation via Single-Step Calibration. Experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly improves navigation and answer accuracy, setting new state-of-the-art performance on standard web navigation benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:07:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:07:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:07:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rean Clive","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Rean Clive","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Fehring","name":"Fehring, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theresa","familyName":"Eimer","name":"Eimer, Theresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marius","familyName":"Lindauer","name":"Lindauer, Marius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Feurer","name":"Feurer, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Environment-Grounded Automated Prompt Optimization for LLM Game Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:06:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:49:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents in interactive environments are highly sensitive to their prompts, yet prompt engineering remains a manual, task-specific process. We introduce an automated prompt optimization framework for LLM agents that decomposes the observation-to-action pipeline into a goal-conditioned descriptor agent and an action selection agent, and iteratively refines each module's prompt through an LLM-driven evolutionary loop guided by environment returns. We propose a behavior analyzer to attribute episode outcomes to specific prompt components, and a mutator to propose targeted revisions to the prompt, before validating them through environment rollouts. We evaluate on all five BabyAI tasks in the BALROG benchmark, comparing our pipeline against BALROG's RobustCoTAgent under both plain and guided prompt initializations. Optimization improves performance consistently across tasks and conditions, without requiring updates to the model weights. On PutNext, a multi-step coordination task where the RobustCoTAgent achieves 0% success, our framework reaches up to 72.5% success rate using the same underlying LLM with optimized prompts. These results suggest that a multi-agent framework, combined with automatic prompt optimization, enhances LLMs without the need for fine-tuning or extensive human supervision."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17821","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17821","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17821","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Esteban","familyName":"Schafir","name":"Schafir, Esteban","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojat Allah","familyName":"Salehi","name":"Salehi, Hojat Allah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuomin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mo","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Mo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DecoSearch: Complexity-Aware Routing and Plan-Level Repair for Text-to-SQL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:48:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:48:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in translating natural language to SQL, yet existing methods still falter on complex queries requiring multi-step, data-aware reasoning. We introduce DecoSearch, a training-free framework that addresses this by routing each query to the appropriate level of reasoning effort. A lightweight Schema Selector first prunes the full database schema to the relevant tables and columns. An LLM Judger then decides whether the question requires decomposition: straightforward questions follow a direct generation path and complex ones are escalated to a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of atomic sub-questions, each solved by a targeted SQL generation step. A RAG component grounds the decomposer with semantically similar training examples, and a Topology Refiner restructures the reasoning plan when execution failures signal a flawed decomposition rather than a fixable SQL error. DecoSearch achieves 70.53% execution accuracy on BIRD and 88.31% on Spider with a DeepSeek backbone, surpassing all training-free baselines while consuming an order of magnitude fewer tokens than competing methods. It also functions as a model-agnostic wrapper, consistently improving fine-tuned SQL generation backbones without any modification to the pipeline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17821","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:06:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17803","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17803","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17803","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaggelis","familyName":"Dorovatas","name":"Dorovatas, Vaggelis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nancy","familyName":"Kalaj","name":"Kalaj, Nancy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahaf","familyName":"Aljundi","name":"Aljundi, Rahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continual Self-Improvement with Lightweight Experiential Latent Memories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:27:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:47:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models achieve strong reasoning performance by scaling inference-time compute, yet remain fundamentally stateless, discarding the rich, self-produced reasoning traces generated during this process. We investigate whether models can instead learn online from this experience, converting transient computation (reasoning traces) into persistent reusable knowledge, and without external supervision or access to future data. We show that In-Context Learning (ICL) over raw reasoning traces fails to generalize, reflecting a fundamental limitation of token-level reuse: individual traces lack the abstraction needed for transfer, even after refinement (e.g. self-reflection). In contrast, drawing inspiration from recent works on unsupervised reinforcement learning, we find that lightweight per-instance training with self-generated test-time signals (majority voting) as rewards yields substantial gains, often surpassing full-dataset offline training, motivating a shift from raw traces to learned latent representations. Building on this insight, we propose an online method that distills inference-time compute spent on encountered problems into compact modular latent memories capturing the underlying reasoning structure. These memories are stored and retrieved for future inputs, enabling continual improvement while avoiding catastrophic forgetting through modular design. Importantly, our method is highly efficient, parametrized as extremely lightweight soft prompt memories (~0.001% of model parameters) and trained with only a few gradient steps, yet achieving performance competitive with full parametric updates and offline training. Across challenging mathematical reasoning benchmarks, our approach significantly outperforms zero-shot and raw data ICL baselines, while transferring effectively across datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17803","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:59Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kairui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kairui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengsheng","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Shengsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Changsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiveStarPro: Proactive Streaming Video Understanding with Hierarchical Memory for Long-Horizon Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:18:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:47:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the remarkable progress of Video Large Language Models (Video-LLMs), current online architectures still struggle to simultaneously process continuous video streams, decide autonomously when to respond, and preserve long-horizon contextual memory. These obstacles undermine real-time responsiveness and cause severe forgetting throughout prolonged interactions. In this work, we introduce LiveStarPro, a live streaming assistant that is designed for proactive video understanding over long-horizon streams. The design of LiveStarPro rests on three complementary components. The first component is Streaming Verification Decoding (SVeD), an inference framework that identifies the appropriate response timing through single-pass perplexity verification, thereby eliminating the dependency on explicit silence tokens. The second component is Streaming Causal Attention Masks (SCAM), a training strategy that enforces incremental video-language alignment over variable-length streams. The third component is Tree-Structured Hierarchical Memory (TSHM), a recursive memory architecture that organizes evicted historical information into event chains and consequently enables efficient retrieval from effectively unbounded video streams. To facilitate a comprehensive evaluation under realistic online conditions, we further present OmniStarPro, a large-scale benchmark that spans 15 diverse real-world scenarios and that extends to hour-scale streams for the assessment of long-term recall. Extensive experiments demonstrate that LiveStarPro consistently surpasses existing methods, attaining a 28.9% improvement in semantic correctness and an 18.2% reduction in timing error, while its streaming key-value cache further yields a 1.58x inference speedup over the same model without caching. The model and the code are publicly available at https://github.com/sotayang/LiveStarPro."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samar","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Samar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Slop Paradox: How Synthetic Standardization Erodes Clinical Uncertainty and Cross-Modal Alignment in AI-Rewritten Radiology Reports"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:07:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:46:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI-assisted clinical documentation tools increasingly summarize, standardize, and reformat radiology reports using large language models (LLMs). We present a controlled measurement of the resulting information degradation. Using 450 chest X-ray reports from the Indiana University dataset, we generate synthetic versions via three realistic LLM rewriting tasks: EHR summarization, standardized rewriting, and teaching case preparation. We measure entity erosion (via medical NER), hedging collapse (loss of clinical uncertainty language), and cross-modal alignment degradation (via BiomedCLIP image-text similarity). Our central finding is a dissociation between information loss and cross-modal fidelity. EHR summarization is the most destructive at the content level, eroding 51.4% of clinical entities and 43.7% of hedging language, yet it preserves image-text alignment almost entirely (a 2.5% drop). The two tasks meant to produce cleaner training data, standardized rewriting and teaching case preparation, do the reverse: they preserve more entities (26.8% and 29.3% eroded) but cause 14.9-16.5% alignment drops, six to seven times those of EHR summarization. We term this the slop paradox: rewriting that makes clinical text look cleaner for multimodal training is precisely what pulls it away from the image. Contrary to our pre-specified hypothesis, rare pathologies were not preferentially degraded: across nine rare-versus-common comparisons, no difference survived multiple-comparison correction, and nominal differences ran in the opposite direction (common \u0026gt; rare), so contamination is invisible to condition-specific monitoring. The dominant determinant of degradation is the type of AI rewriting task, not the clinical content. These findings bear on multimodal medical AI dataset construction and the governance of AI-assisted clinical documentation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17789","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17789","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17789","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofie","familyName":"Kamber","name":"Kamber, Sofie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Diebold","name":"Diebold, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Riachi","name":"Riachi, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stella","familyName":"Brogna","name":"Brogna, Stella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Gloster","name":"Gloster, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Wampfler","name":"Wampfler, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mind Companion: An Embodied Conversational Agent for Process-Based Psychotherapy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:05:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:46:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ichi69079.2026.00123","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Access to evidence-based psychotherapy remains limited worldwide, with long waitlists even in high-income regions. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer potential for scalable mental health support when designed with clinical oversight and safety mechanisms. We present Mind Companion, an LLM-based embodied conversational agent integrating multi-layered psychological analysis with process-based therapy principles. The system performs real-time analysis of client statements across fact extraction, psychological flexibility process detection, emotion recognition, and safety monitoring. Analysis results are stored for supervising clinicians to inform therapeutic planning. Response generation incorporates retrieval-augmented generation from evidence-based therapeutic literature and context-aware prompting. Responses are delivered through an embodied avatar with synchronized speech synthesis and animation. We evaluated three LLM configurations (GPT-4.1-mini, GPT-5.2, Claude Sonnet 4.5) against therapist responses from real therapy sessions using automated LLM-judge assessment and expert evaluation with 11 professional psychotherapists. GPT-5.2 achieved higher ratings than human therapist responses across understanding, interpersonal effectiveness, collaboration, and therapeutic alignment in both evaluations, demonstrating the feasibility of LLM-based conversational agents as tools to complement clinical care."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17789","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:41Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Juncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanming","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongkun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yongkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick P. C.","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Patrick P. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LUMEN: Coordinated Failure Recovery for Distributed LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:02:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:46:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern large language model (LLM) serving clusters distribute inference requests across multiple worker processes on different GPUs, but failures are prevalent at scale. When a worker fails, the cluster simultaneously loses the failed worker's GPU-resident key-value (KV) caches and serving capacity, leaving surviving workers to absorb the redirected traffic while re-running interrupted requests from scratch. Existing fault-tolerant systems either restart interrupted requests from scratch or restore KV caches from checkpoints stored on a fixed neighboring worker, but both approaches route recovery work without considering current cluster load and leave the recovering worker idle during model reload. We present LUMEN, a fault-tolerant LLM serving system that treats recovery as a load-aware coordination problem across three decision points: checkpoint placement before failures, interrupted-request distribution at failure time, and serving capacity restoration during model reload. We evaluate LUMEN using both prototype experiments and large-scale simulations and demonstrate significant improvements in serving and recovery times."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:38Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Riachi","name":"Riachi, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofie","familyName":"Kamber","name":"Kamber, Sofie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stella","familyName":"Brogna","name":"Brogna, Stella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Gloster","name":"Gloster, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Wampfler","name":"Wampfler, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Accessible Psychotherapy Training Using AI-Driven Interactive Patient Avatars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:02:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:46:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ichi69079.2026.00124","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training psychotherapists in evidence-based interventions such as Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) requires repeated practice with meaningful feedback, yet opportunities for safe, standardized training are limited by ethical, logistical, and resource constraints. We introduce a system designed to support ACT-oriented psychotherapy training through spoken dialogue with an embodied virtual patient. The system uses large language models to simulate patient behavior conditioned on profiles derived from real therapy sessions and configurable clinical scenarios, while a separate automated evaluator provides turn-by-turn feedback on therapist responses based on established ACT fidelity criteria. Rather than aiming to replace supervision, the system is intended to support deliberate practice by enabling experimentation, reflection, and immediate feedback in low-risk settings. Expert evaluation with practicing psychologists confirmed high realism in patient behavior and demonstrated that immediate turn-by-turn ACT feedback increased therapists' awareness of intervention choices and enabled effective experimentation with alternative responses. Quantitative evaluation across 49 therapy transcripts identified GPT-4o-mini as the optimal feedback model, achieving the lowest mean absolute error (MAE = 6.12) in replicating human supervisor ACT fidelity ratings with statistically significant agreement. This work demonstrates the potential of fidelity-aware simulated patients as a scalable complement to psychotherapy training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:37Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kosmas","familyName":"Alexandridis","name":"Alexandridis, Kosmas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Dimitrakopoulos","name":"Dimitrakopoulos, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIVE: A Minimalist Integer Vector Engine for Softmax LayerNorm and RMSNorm Acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T10:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:45:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of Large Language Models (LLMs) has intensified the need for specialized hardware accelerators that can satisfy stringent inference latency and power constraints. Although matrix multiplications dominate the overall computational workload, non-linear vector normalization operations, such as LayerNorm, RMSNorm and Softmax can become critical hardware bottlenecks. Existing accelerators typically implement these functions using dedicated hardware blocks, leading to duplicated resources and inefficient silicon utilization. To address this limitation, we propose a Minimalist Integer Vector Engine (MIVE), a programmable architecture capable of executing all three operations within a unified datapath. By exploiting common computational patterns across LayerNorm, RMSNorm and Softmax the proposed vector engine maximizes hardware sharing while reducing implementation overhead. Physical ASIC implementation results show that MIVE provides comprehensive multi-function support while achieving higher area and hardware efficiency than most state-of-the-art standalone accelerators."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17742","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17742","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17742","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanye","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xuanye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanying","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Quanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BrainWorld: A Structural-Prior-Conditioned Generative Model for Whole-Brain 4D fMRI Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T10:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:43:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Whole-brain 4D fMRI generation is valuable for modeling functional brain dynamics, yet existing fMRI foundation models mainly target representation learning and downstream prediction rather than conditional predictive generation. We introduce BrainWorld, a structural-prior-conditioned generative model for whole-brain 4D fMRI dynamics. BrainWorld uses sMRI as subject-level anatomical context to guide future fMRI generation, integrating structural information into the denoising process rather than treating it as a parallel modality. Evaluated on 22 datasets spanning diverse cohorts and brain states, BrainWorld generates stable 4D fMRI trajectories up to 400 frames, improves downstream performance through generated-example augmentation, and learns transferable multimodal representations that outperform baselines. Together, these results establish BrainWorld as a condition-aware generative framework for long-horizon brain dynamics modeling and multimodal representation learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17742","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:39Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faqiang","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Faqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cijun","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Cijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Qibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shattering the Autoregressive Curse: Dynamic Epistemic Entropy Orchestrated Erasable Reinforcement Learning for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:55:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:43:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although reinforcement learning (RL) has expanded the cognitive boundaries of large language models (LLMs), it often remains vulnerable to the autoregressive curse in long-horizon logical reasoning: small epistemic perturbations introduced early in generation can propagate irreversibly along the Markov decision process flow, triggering cascading failures that drive the reasoning trajectory toward collapse. To overcome this autoregressive cascade, in which a single early mistake can compromise all subsequent reasoning steps, we propose dynamic epistemic entropy orchestrated erasable reinforcement learning ($\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$). $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ eliminates reliance on external signals by grounding the model's endogenous local autoregressive cross-entropy as an intrinsic coordinate of epistemic uncertainty. By introducing segment-level adaptive dynamic thresholds and advantage allocation, $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ enables the model to precisely excise localized logical defects while reusing historical key-value (KV) cache streams, thereby endowing the reasoning process with a self-healing capability. We train $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ on the DeepMath-103k dataset. Experimental results show that $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ reshapes the exploration efficiency of long-sequence reasoning and improves sample efficiency while maintaining linear memory overhead. On mathematical reasoning benchmarks such as AIME, $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ achieves substantial performance gains, with the 4B and 8B parameter models surpassing previous state-of-the-art (SOTA) results by 5.349\\% and 6.514\\%, respectively. These findings suggest that $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ shatters the autoregressive curse in long-sequence reasoning and establishes a theoretical and systems-level foundation for the next generation of self-healing artificial general intelligence (AGI)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengpan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mengpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingde","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghui","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Shanghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jibing","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Jibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongWebBench: Evaluating Structural and Functional Webpage Generation in Long-Horizon Settings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:43:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:42:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent vision-language models (VLMs) have shown promising progress in generating webpages from visual inputs, yet existing evaluations mainly focus on short, single-screen, and largely static webpages. We introduce LongWebBench, a benchmark for evaluating long-horizon webpage generation from both structural and functional perspectives. LongWebBench contains 490 real-world long webpages for structural fidelity evaluation and 507 goal-oriented interaction tasks over 129 webpages for functional evaluation. It employs two complementary protocols: a multi-dimensional VLM-based metric for assessing long-range structural coherence, and a DOM-augmented agent-based pipeline for end-to-end functional verification. We further examine the automatic evaluation protocols through human agreement analysis. Experiments with state-of-the-art open-source and proprietary VLMs under single-image and multi-image settings reveal that structural fidelity degrades as webpage length increases, while visually plausible generations often fail to support executable multi-step interactions. These results highlight the need to evaluate long webpage generation beyond visual similarity, with executable interaction as a core criterion. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/zheny2751-dotcom/LongWebBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages, 38 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:20Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Takacs","name":"Takacs, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Bracher","name":"Bracher, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svitlana","familyName":"Vakulenko","name":"Vakulenko, Svitlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding and Debugging Failures in N-Gram-Based Generative Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:42:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Retrieval (GR) is an emerging Information Retrieval (IR) paradigm that is motivated by increasingly capable language models. In GR, a model directly generates identifiers for relevant documents. While these systems offer unique advantages, they also introduce distinct failure mechanisms. We explore these failure modes in three contributions: (1) We present a taxonomy of GR failure modes based on GR literature. (2) We empirically investigate failure in a subset of GR: ngram-based methods, more specifically, SEAL and MINDER. Our analysis reveals common issues, such as ambiguous docids, low identifier diversity, and the disproportionate impact of specific identifiers. (3) We introduce a new web-based tool that helps the IR community analyze generated ngrams and their respective contribution to the final ranking, providing an intuitive interface to identify where such GR methods go wrong."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengxin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Gengxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanzi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxi","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Hongxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjie","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Generative Recommenders Deepen the Information Cocoon? A Closed-Loop Simulation with LLM-powered User Simulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:17:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:41:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recommender systems alleviate information overload, yet repeated feedback between recommendations and user interactions can reinforce existing preferences and narrow users' exposure, forming information cocoons. While this phenomenon has been widely studied in traditional sequential recommendation, its impact on generative recommendation remains unclear. By replacing atomic item IDs with Semantic ID (SID) sequences, generative recommenders introduce a different recommendation mechanism whose role in information cocoon formation is not yet understood. To investigate whether generative recommenders deepen information cocoons, we propose \\textsc{RecLoop}, a closed-loop simulation framework with LLM-driven user agents. We compare two generative recommenders and two traditional sequential baselines on two Amazon datasets across multiple feedback cycles. In addition to standard exposure-level metrics, we introduce \\emph{Code-Space Structural Cocoon}, a model-level metric that measures concentration in the generated SID space. Experimental results show that generative recommenders are generally less prone to exposure-level cocoon formation than traditional baselines, preserving broader exposure diversity and slowing cross-user homogenization. However, feedback loops can still induce concentration within the generated SID space. We further find that cocoon severity depends strongly on tokenization strategy and model scale: collaborative-signal tokenization produces stronger cocoon effects than semantic tokenization, whereas larger models maintain greater code-space diversity and better retain access to niche content. These findings suggest that information cocoons in generative recommendation are shaped not only by recommendation behavior, but also by item tokenization and model capacity. Our code is available at https://github.com/Dregen-Yor/RecLoop."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17692","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17692","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17692","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vansh","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Vansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Delta-Based Target Reformulation for Short-Term Electricity Load Forecasting Using LSTM and Transformer Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:01:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate short-term electricity load forecasting is critical for the reliable and economic operation of modern power systems, under non-stationarity arising from weather variability, calendar effects, and evolving consumption patterns. While deep learning models such as LSTMs and Transformers show promising performance, most existing studies focus on direct absolute load prediction without explicitly addressing target non-stationarity.\n Motivated by classical time-series differencing techniques in ARIMA models, this paper investigates a delta-based target reformulation for short-term electricity load forecasting using deep learning. Instead of directly predicting absolute load values, the proposed formulation trains models to predict the change in load between consecutive time steps, with final forecasts reconstructed using the last observed load. This aims to stabilize the learning target and reduce forecasting difficulty.\n Using multi-year, hourly real-world electricity load data from India, augmented with meteorological variables from the NASA POWER project and calendar features, this study evaluates LSTM and Transformer models under both formulations, benchmarking them against LightGBM. Experiments are conducted for hour-ahead and day-ahead horizons, assessing performance via Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE).\n Results show that delta-based reformulation consistently improves forecasting accuracy for hour-ahead prediction across all evaluated models, yielding MAPE reductions of over 50% compared to absolute formulations. For day-ahead forecasting, delta targets specifically benefit deep sequence models (LSTM and Transformer), while LightGBM remains competitive under the absolute formulation. These findings indicate that while delta reformulation is a powerful inductive bias for neural networks, its efficacy is model- and horizon-dependent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17692","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17683","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17683","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17683","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longhui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Longhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Functional Correctness and Runtime Efficiency Gaps in LLM-Based Code Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.2; I.2.7; D.3.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:49:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) have greatly advanced the functional correctness of automated code translation systems, the runtime efficiency of translated programs has received comparatively little attention. With the waning of Moore's law, runtime efficiency has become increasingly important for program quality, alongside functional correctness. Our preliminary study reveals that LLM-translated programs often run slower than human-written ones, and this issue cannot be remedied through prompt engineering alone. Therefore, our work proposes SwiftTrans, a code translation framework comprising two key stages: (1) Multi-Perspective Exploration, where MpTranslator leverages parallel in-context learning (ICL) to generate diverse translation candidates; and (2) Difference-Aware Selection, where DiffSelector identifies the optimal candidate by explicitly comparing differences between translations. We further introduce Hierarchical Guidance for MpTranslator and Ordinal Guidance for DiffSelector, enabling LLMs to better adapt to these two core components. To support the evaluation of runtime efficiency in translated programs, we extend existing benchmarks, CodeNet and F2SBench, and introduce a new benchmark, SwiftBench. Experimental results across all three benchmarks show that SwiftTrans achieves consistent improvements in both correctness and runtime efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17683","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Trainee to Trainer: LLM-Designed Training Environment for RL with Multi-Agent Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:48:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning pipelines for Large Language Model (LLM) training often rely on manually redesigned environments between stages, requiring practitioners to heuristically infer which configuration will best improve the current policy. To automate this process, we propose the LLM-as-Environment-Engineer framework in which the current policy model analyzes failure trajectories together with contextual information and proposes modifications to the next-stage training environment configuration. We also introduce MAPF-FrozenLake, a controllable testbed whose generator exposes multi-dimensional environment configurations, making it suitable for studying and benchmarking environment redesign. On this testbed, we condition the environment engineer on structured summaries of policy behavior, failure cases, and environment statistics, from which it produces the configuration for the next training stage. With Qwen3-4B as the backbone, our framework achieves the strongest aggregate performance on our benchmarks, outperforming larger proprietary LLMs (e.g., GPT, Gemini) and fixed-environment training baselines. We further analyze which forms of context are most effective, finding that successful environment updates rely on failure evidence and preserve configurations that already work. Interestingly, the current RL checkpoint serves as a better environment engineer than the original base model, suggesting that policy learning improves the model's ability to diagnose its remaining weaknesses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:19Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Songze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanzi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EnvRL: Learn from Environment Dynamics in Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:48:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for training Large Language Models (LLMs) as agents. However, conventional RL methods for long-horizon agentic tasks often struggle with sparse outcome rewards. Intuitively, this overlooks the rich environment dynamics information contained in rollout interaction trajectories. We argue that the interaction experience inherently serves as an implicit supervision signal, reveals the underlying transition mechanisms of the environment, and enables the agent to construct a more accurate internal model of the environment.. Therefore, in this work, we investigate how to leverage this additional signal to improve policy learning. Specifically, we propose EnvRL, a framework that incorporates environment dynamics learning into agentic RL via two auxiliary objectives: state prediction and inverse dynamics. By jointly optimizing with the primary RL objective, we encourage the agent to internalize environment dynamics from its own interaction experience. Extensive experiments on two long-horizon agentic benchmarks demonstrate that EnvRL achieves significant improvements on success-rates over RL-only baselines, e.g., when trained with GRPO, lifting Qwen-2.5-1.5B-Instruct from 72.8% to 77.4% on ALFWorld, and from 56.8% to 67.0% on WebShop."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicong","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Sicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoshun","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Guoshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuancheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexian","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zexian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"See First, Answer Later: Visual Evidence Pre-Alignment via Sufficiency-Driven RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:45:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) integrate strong text reasoning with visual inputs, yet their responses can be inconsistent with the underlying images, indicating ineffective utilization of visual evidence during inference. The prevailing training paradigm relies on large-scale caption-based pretraining for general alignment, followed by supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning to enable instruction following and complex reasoning. However, such pretraining provides only weak visual grounding: short, coarse captions bias models toward salient objects while neglecting fine-grained visual evidence. In this paper, we introduce Visual Evidence Pre-Alignment (VEPA), an intermediate stage between pretraining and post-training that explores a novel sufficiency-driven objective with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to optimize question-conditioned visual evidence descriptions. Extensive experiments across diverse benchmarks show that our VEPA consistently enhances performance on visually demanding evaluations and complements standard supervised post-training. Further analyses show that the income stems from strengthened, transferable visual grounding, rather than from additional task-specific training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:12Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Komamizu","name":"Komamizu, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ichiro","familyName":"Ide","name":"Ide, Ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeSRPA: Decoupled Speech Role-Playing Agent via Inference-Time Intervention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:30:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized text-based role-playing, creating immersive Speech Role-Playing Agents (SRPAs) requires a seamless bridge between cognitive reasoning and paralinguistic nuances. Current SRPAs primarily rely on end-to-end (E2E) fine-tuning. However, this paradigm suffers from poor generalization to unseen characters due to its reliance on role-specific data, while imposing a \"modality alignment tax\" that degrades intrinsic LLM reasoning capabilities.\n We propose DeSRPA, an agentic framework for character role play via inference-time intervention on frozen backbones. DeSRPA employs a dual-level control vector mechanism, Internal Cognitive Steering and External Expressive Rendering, to synchronize \"mind\" and \"voice\". Experiments on SpeechRole and OmniCharacter benchmarks demonstrate that DeSRPA significantly outperforms E2E baselines in personality and emotional consistency. It achieves high speech naturalness, narrowing the gap with proprietary models like GPT-4o Audio, while remaining a scalable and training-free paradigm."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:00Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17668","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17668","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17668","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luonan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Luonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Renxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ASTEROID: A Spatiotemporal Information Transformer for Forecasting Multi-Step Time Series of Molecular Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:30:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular dynamics (MD) simulation is computationally demanding, particularly for large-scale systems requiring long-term analysis. Accurate forecast of the outcomes of a MD simulation is not only an attractive scientific challenge but also has substantial practical value. In this work, we developed a data-driven framework, termed ASTEROID (Advanced Spatiotemporal TransformER fOr Inferring Dynamics), that can directly predict multi-step atomic coordinates, avoiding conventional iterative integration. For this purpose, our ASTEROID reformulates MD trajectories as high-dimensional spatiotemporal sequences and integrates the Spatiotemporal Information (STI) Transformation equation into a Transformer architecture. The core innovation of ASTEROID lies in its ability to model multiscale spatiotemporal dependencies. In particular, for spatial dependencies, a local-global self-attention mechanism captures both short- and long-range interactions. For temporal dependencies, an encoder-decoder structure integrates global context with autoregressive forecasting. ASTEROID was evaluated on several quantum-mechanics derived molecular datasets. Our results indicate that ASTEROID achieved not only a higher level of accuracy in multi-step prediction than existing methods on various benchmarks, but also significantly reduced computational cost of conventional MD simulation. Moreover, the model supports iterative multi-step forecasting over an extended time scale. This work establishes a robust and generalizable data-driven paradigm for accelerating MD simulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages,10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17668","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:59Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Handling Feature Heterogeneity with Learnable Graph Patches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:29:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3690624.3709242","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, the rapid development of foundation models and graph pre-training technologies has spurred increasing interest in constructing a universal pre-trained graph model or Graph Foundation Model (GFM). However, a significant challenge is that existing models are unable to address feature heterogeneity in graph data without textual information, which hinders the transferability of graph models across different datasets. To bridge this gap, we propose the concept of learnable graph patches, which we regard as the smallest semantic units of any graph data. We decompose the graph into learnable graph patches by unfolding the node features and constructing corresponding patch structures separately. We then design a framework that mines transferable information from graph data across domains. Specifically, after extracting graph patches, we propose a patch encoder to extract knowledge from each unit and a patch aggregator to learn how the units are combined into a whole. Due to its domain-agnostic nature, the model can be applied to downstream data across different domains. Furthermore, we analyze the connection between our method and existing graph models, as well as the transferability of the node embeddings it generates. Empirically, our method not only achieves the capability to use multi-domain graphs for pre-training, but also shows enhanced performance across various downstream datasets and tasks. Moreover, we observe consistent improvement in downstream performance as the volume of pre-training data increases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at KDD 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:57Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Pulse","name":"Pulse, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-Bin","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yong-Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhik","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Abhik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prem Prakash","familyName":"Jayaraman","name":"Jayaraman, Prem Prakash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FacProcessTwin: An LLM-Based System for Process Twin Development"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:27:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Process twins provide real-time representations of entire production processes. By capturing how process steps interact, rather than monitoring a single machine in isolation as an asset-based digital twin does, they have the potential to drive efficiency gains across the whole process. However, developing a process twin is costly. It requires accurately modelling the entire production process: its process steps, the equipment and product-specific settings each step uses, and its process variations. The resulting model must then be bound to live operational data. We present FacProcessTwin, a system that leverages a large language model (LLM) to reduce this development time, building a process twin from a plant's process documentation and natural-language input from an operator. FacProcessTwin generates this complete process model and then automatically binds its process steps to live operational data. The generated model and its data bindings are rendered as an interactive process diagram through which manufacturing personnel can monitor and correct the system's autonomous decisions, such as resolving uncertainty at safety-critical binding steps. We evaluate FacProcessTwin through a real-world case study of an Australian food manufacturer, covering 16 production process flows that span chilled, frozen, and aseptic shelf-stable product categories and include process variations within the same product. The results show that FacProcessTwin generates these process models accurately (a mean F1 of 95.2% against ground truth) and builds each twin in roughly a sixth of the manual time. Its human-in-the-loop governance then keeps the safety-critical bindings correct: at ambiguous tags where a single-pass baseline silently mis-binds 75.0% of the time, FacProcessTwin defers to the operator and mis-binds none."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:56Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiqi","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Qiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TuneAhead: Predicting Fine-tuning Performance Before Full Training Begins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:21:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) is compute-intensive and error-prone: model performance depends sensitively on data quality and hyperparameter choices, and naïve runs can even degrade model performance. This raises a practical question:can we predict fine-tuning performance before committing to a full training run? We present TUNEAHEAD, a lightweight framework for pre-hoc prediction of fine-tuning performance. TUNEAHEAD encodes each candidate run as a meta-feature vector that combines static dataset descriptors with dynamic probe features from a short standardized probe. A predictor maps these features to performance estimates, while SHAP-based attributions provide interpretable diagnostics that reveal which specific features drive the prediction. Across 1,300+ fine-tuning runs on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, TUNEAHEAD consistently outperforms strong baselines such as Early-Stop Extrapolation and ProxyLM. On a held-out test set of 370 runs, TUNEAHEAD achieves an RMSE of 1.47 percentage points and places 95.1% of predictions within +3/-3 percentage points of the true score. These accurate continuous predictions support practical go/no-go screening policies that can reduce unnecessary full fine-tuning while retaining most promising runs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 figures, accepted as ICML 2026 poster:https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/64847"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:48Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas L.","familyName":"Griffiths","name":"Griffiths, Thomas L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using Cognitive Models to Improve Language Model Simulation of Human Persuasion Games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:16:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"People make decisions differently in strategic interactions. Some update beliefs like a Bayesian; others exhibit biases like motivated reasoning. Although creators of large language models use simulated humans for safety evaluations and training, they often fail to cover this breadth of human behavior. We argue that cognitive science and economics provide a convenient tool for doing so, making use of mathematical models of human decision-making. We propose an approach that we call Equation-to-Behavior Prompting for guiding large language models to match cognitive models, and evaluate this approach on persuasion games based on legal decision-making. We find that large models can approximate equation-based specifications -- Bayesian updating, affine distortion, motivated updating, and Grether's $α$-$β$ model -- using prompting, but small models fail to do so. However, training small models with reinforcement learning to adhere to mathematical rules, Equation-to-Behavior RL, reduces belief error by 26.5% in out-of-distribution parameterizations. We show that these simulations can help create diverse training environments; training small models to consider different kinds of decision-makers improves average belief change by 2.5%--12% over Bayesian-only training, even when persuading GPT-5-mini. Our work could improve human simulations for training and evaluation in increasingly realistic settings, and could also enable novel research into more complicated mathematical models of human decision-making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:44Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17645","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17645","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17645","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feijie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Feijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaliang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yaliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bolin","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Bolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mosharaf","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Mosharaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Domains: Reusing Web Skills via Transferable Interaction Patterns"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:04:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) web agents are usually deployed as tool callers: each turn, the model reads a fresh page observation and emits one structured tool action. When every action is a low-level primitive, horizons grow quickly and so do policy-facing LLM completions, dominating latency and cost on benchmarks such as Mind2Web and WebArena. Recent systems therefore wrap repeated interaction fragments as web skills: callable tools built from successful trajectories or induced programs, so one call can replace several primitives. However, prior skill libraries are still triggered mainly by instruction similarity or coarse site metadata, which yields low skill reuse on held-out sites and leaves much of the potential step and token reduction on the table.\n We present SkillMigrator, an agent that learns reusable web skills and transfers them across sites by matching layout structure rather than specific element references. Each induced skill is stored as a transferable interaction pattern (TIP): the skill paired with a structural sketch of the snapshot at induction time. At test time, SkillMigrator retrieves TIPs by layout similarity and grounds their references on the live page. The rest of the stack is standard: accessibility-snapshot observations with stable references, and fixed tool calling over primitives plus skill invocations. Compared with the state-of-the-art approaches, SkillMigrator reduces the average LLM-action count on successful trajectories by 8-10% across both WebArena and Mind2Web at matched success rate."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17645","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:29Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyue","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Negin","familyName":"Sokhandan","name":"Sokhandan, Negin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Marlowe","name":"Marlowe, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Socolinsky","name":"Socolinsky, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Brick-DICL: Dynamic In-Context Learning for Automated Brick Schema Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building Management Systems (BMS) are essential for optimizing energy efficiency and operational performance in modern buildings. However, the lack of standardization across BMS points from different manufacturers creates significant barriers to integration and data utilization. While the Brick schema offers a standardized ontology for building systems, mapping BMS points to appropriate Brick classes presents three critical challenges: (i) the extensive number of Brick classes (936 in the latest version), (ii) limited domain-specific knowledge in large language models (LLMs), and (iii) substantial manual effort required for verification. To address these challenges, we propose Brick-DICL, a two-stage dynamic in-context learning framework for automated Brick schema classification. Brick-DICL consists of two primary components: metadata-RAG, which retrieves relevant examples to enhance LLMs' domain knowledge, and class-RAG, which narrows down potential Brick classes to address the large classification space. Additionally, we implement a multi-LLM filtering mechanism that compares predictions across multiple models, flagging low-confidence classifications for human review. As a result: (i) General: Brick-DICL is applicable to any building management system regardless of manufacturer or metadata format; (ii) Novel and Powerful: as the first dynamic in-context learning approach for Brick schema classification, Brick-DICL achieves significant classification accuracy improvements on building datasets, outperforming existing methods; (iii) Efficient: our multi-LLM filtering strategy reduces manual verification effort, enabling rapid digital building onboarding. Extensive experiments demonstrate Brick-DICL's effectiveness across diverse building datasets, accelerating the path toward standardized, interoperable building management systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:18Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengkun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Perturbation for Reliable LLM Evaluation over Comparison Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:44:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating large language models (LLMs) is important for understanding their capabilities, comparing competing systems, and supporting the deployment of reliable models in practice. For open-ended tasks, pairwise evaluation has become a popular paradigm, in which two responses to the same prompt are compared and the resulting judgments are aggregated into an overall ranking. A central challenge of this paradigm is intransitivity: the induced comparison outcomes may fail to support any coherent global ranking. For example, one may observe cyclic preferences such as $A \\succ B \\succ C \\succ A$, or inconsistencies involving ties such as $A \\equiv B\\equiv C\\neq A$. Such contradictions make the resulting leaderboard unstable and challenging to interpret. In this paper, we propose a prompt perturbation framework for improving the consistency of pairwise LLM evaluation. Our approach generates perturbed variants of each prompt, uses the resulting comparison graphs to identify and filter out structurally inconsistent comparison patterns, and then applies standard ranking methods to the filtered comparisons. A key feature of the proposed framework is that graph-level structural consistency is incorporated explicitly into the evaluation pipeline before ranking aggregation. This provides a simple and principled way to reduce cyclic inconsistencies and improve the reliability of LLM rankings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Minyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huamin","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Huamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AdaPT: Adaptive Lesson Plan Transformer for Cross-Regional and Differentiated Instruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:43:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Due to educational inequality, high-quality lesson plans often mismatch the needs of disparate educational contexts. Teachers typically modify existing lesson plans to fit new contexts, but current tools instead focus on generating content from scratch, creating additional workload. Moreover, a critical gap remains in supporting teachers to quickly adapt to new learning profiles. To bridge these gaps, we present AdaPT, a system leverages LLMs to support transformation of existing lesson plans for cross-regional and differentiated instruction. AdaPT features an interactive interface that allows teachers to input student profiles, offers structured lesson representation, provides explanations for lesson-plan transformations, automatically adapts lesson content for new contexts, and supports iterative, teacher-in-the-loop refinement. We evaluated AdaPT through a user study with 9 teachers and an expert evaluation with 3 specialists. Results show that AdaPT supports workflows of teachers and offers a promising pathway toward promoting educational equity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:13Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippa","familyName":"Duckett","name":"Duckett, Philippa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Van Stroud","name":"Van Stroud, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Hart","name":"Hart, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Facini","name":"Facini, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Scanlon","name":"Scanlon, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Better Queries, Cheaper Attention: Adapting Transformers for Efficient Sparse Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:41:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Query-based transformer decoders are effective for object reconstruction from sparse scientific sensor measurements, but their scalability to high-multiplicity data is limited by fixed, input-independent query sets and costly decoder cross-attention. We introduce a geometry-aware dynamic-query decoder that couples input-conditioned query construction with structured sparse cross-attention. Decoder queries are initialised from selected encoder-level measurement representations that serve as candidate trajectory seeds, making both query content and query multiplicity input-dependent. Local Strided Cross-Attention (LSCA) exploits the induced geometric ordering by replacing learned mask-gated cross-attention with a geometry-defined local support that restricts attention to physically plausible query-hit interactions and exposes sparsity for block-sparse execution. We study this architecture for charged-particle trajectory reconstruction in a simplified High-Luminosity Large Hadron Collider detector, where thousands of trajectories must be reconstructed from tens of thousands of sparse measurements. In the nominal configuration, the dynamic-query (DQ) architecture increases trajectory reconstruction efficiency from 94.1% to 98.1% and reduces the fake rate by more than a factor of two relative to the fixed-query baseline. The DQ+LSCA model reduces end-to-end inference latency by nearly 50% and peak allocated inference memory by more than a factor of 10 relative to the fixed-query baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sottovia","name":"Sottovia, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Torcinovich","name":"Torcinovich, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oswald","familyName":"Lanz","name":"Lanz, Oswald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Divide, Deliberate, Decide: A Multi-Agent Framework for Fine-Grained Egocentric Action Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:31:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:35:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-grained action recognition in egocentric video is challenging for Vision-Language Models (VLMs): actions often differ only in small visual cues, and a single model tends to be biased toward a subset of these cues. We propose Divide, Deliberate, Decide, a fully-local, zero-shot multi-agent framework in which (i) a VLM orchestrator chunks the video and proposes a top-k candidate label list per segment, (ii) an ensemble of heterogeneous VLM specialists, drawn from different open model families, engages in a structured deliberation that includes a peer-consultation round of questions, and (iii) agent rankings are aggregated with a Borda count and the orchestrator re-ranks its own prediction in light of the specialists' evidence. The entire pipeline runs locally with no fine-tuning. Experiments show that our method positively improves zero-shot action recognition performance over the baseline, highlighting the influence of a heterogeneous deliberation step, showing that the gain stems from decorrelated model priors rather than from additional compute."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenchang","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Zhenchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxue","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Xiaoxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PracRepair: LLM-Empowered Automated Program Repair Inspired by Human-Like Debugging Practices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:18:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:35:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As software systems grow in scale and complexity, debugging and repair remain costly and time-consuming. Large language models (LLMs) have advanced automated program repair (APR), but existing LLM-based APR approaches still largely rely on static or retrieved context, error messages, and coarse-grained validation outcomes. As a result, they underutilize dynamic information for failure understanding and repair, including failure-execution dynamics and patch-validation dynamics. Effectively leveraging such information, however, is challenging: failure-execution traces are large and noisy, raw static-dynamic context is not self-explanatory, and patch-validation dynamics are often reduced to coarse feedback. To address these challenges, we propose \\textsc{PracRepair}, a fully automated LLM-based APR framework inspired by human-like debugging practices. \\textsc{PracRepair} constructs an on-demand static-dynamic context from buggy programs and failure executions, performs question-driven failure diagnosis to formulate explicit repair hypotheses, and iteratively refines candidate patches using validation diagnostics and trace-level behavioral changes. Experimental results on Defects4J V1.2 and V2.0 show that \\textsc{PracRepair} consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines. Specifically, under GPT-3.5, \\textsc{PracRepair} correctly fixes 139/136 bugs on Defects4J V1.2/V2.0, while under GPT-4o it further improves to 162/171. Moreover, \\textsc{PracRepair} generalizes effectively to RWB (Real-World Bugs), achieving the best performance across multiple foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zesheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zesheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshuo","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Tianshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Benchmark Illusion: Pruned LLMs Can Pass Multiple Choice but Fail to Answer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:34:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compressing large language models reduces memory use and inference cost, but it can also create failures that standard benchmarks miss. A pruned model may still perform well on multiple-choice evaluations, yet fail to answer the same question in open generation. We ask what pruning changes: does it erase the correct answer, or does it make the answer harder to produce as the top output?\n We study this question with multilingual question answering, tracking the same questions before and after pruning. We find a benchmark illusion. Under high-sparsity pruning, especially Wanda, models often fail in greedy open generation while still selecting the correct answer under multiple-choice scoring. In these recognition-only errors, the answer is usually not gone, but demoted: it often reappears with beam search, sampling, or one in-context example. Overall, multiple-choice benchmarks can overstate the usability of compressed LLMs, creating an evaluation blind spot. Compressed models should be tested on what they can produce, not only on what they can recognize."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mika","familyName":"Mäntylä","name":"Mäntylä, Mika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patricia","familyName":"Matsubara","name":"Matsubara, Patricia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katia Romero","familyName":"Felizardo","name":"Felizardo, Katia Romero","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miikka","familyName":"Kuutila","name":"Kuutila, Miikka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Gerosa","name":"Gerosa, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savio de Sousa","familyName":"Sampaio","name":"Sampaio, Savio de Sousa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tayana","familyName":"Conte","name":"Conte, Tayana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Steinmacher","name":"Steinmacher, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding LLMs in Title-Abstract Screening: From Disagreements to Recommendations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:51:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:33:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Several studies have examined the use of large language models (LLMs) for title-abstract screening in systematic reviews (SRs), reporting mixed accuracy. However, questions of reliability remain largely unaddressed. In this study, we go beyond quantitative LLM-human agreement metrics and qualitatively investigate how and why LLMs fail. We also propose actionable recommendations. We analyzed disagreements between LLMs and researchers across six software engineering SRs and over 1,000 primary study papers. For each SR, papers were screened independently by human experts and LLMs in zero-shot mode, resulting in Kappa values ranging from 0.52 to 0.77. Qualitative analysis suggests that human-LLM disagreement results from recurring, identifiable causes, such as boundary ambiguity in key terms, keyword overemphasization, and incorrect topic inference. Based on these findings, we propose recommendations such as validating semantic understanding before deployment, running multiple LLMs, and focusing validation efforts on borderline cases. Future studies are needed to validate the impact of our recommendations, and community efforts are needed to develop normative guidelines on LLM usage in SRs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages + references. Accepted for publication in the 52nd Euromicro Conference on Software Engineering and Advanced Applications (SEAA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collaborative Large and Small Language Models for Accurate and Scalable Data Repair"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the problem of data repair, a key task in data cleaning that corrects erroneous entries in raw datasets to improve overall data quality. Although recent data-driven methods, especially those based on large language models (LLMs), achieve remarkable performance, we observe that: (i) they directly repair data in the raw and low-quality context, which may compromise learning signals, and (ii) they directly use uncertain model outputs as repairs, potentially introducing unreliable corrections and compromising repair quality. Motivated by the efficiency of small language models (SLMs) and the capabilities of LLMs, and aiming to address the above limitations, we propose LasRepair, a framework that collaborates Large and small language models for data repair. LasRepair employs an LLM as an instructor, which selects a global repair context to guide the SLM. The SLM acts as a corrector, using the selected context to repair erroneous data more efficiently. Moreover, to further improve context quality, we extend LasRepair to LasRepair+, which formulates data repair as an Expectation-Maximisation (EM) procedure that alternates between an E-step for updating the corrector parameters and an M-step for refining the repair context. Furthermore, to mitigate model uncertainty, we propose LasRepair++, which uses column-calibrated model confidence to down-weight unreliable repaired rows when updating the corrector, thereby enhancing repair quality. Theoretical analysis and empirical evaluation demonstrate the superiority of our methods. We theoretically prove the effectiveness of the EM-style procedure and the confidence-based weighting. Experiments on real-world datasets show that LasRepair++~ achieves an average F1-score improvement of 18.1% over the strongest baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:07Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runlong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Runlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon S.","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Simon S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visored: A Controlled-Natural-Language Prover for LLM-Generated Mathematics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a dependent-type-based prover designed around the way LLMs (and humans) tend to write mathematics, complementing existing systems such as Lean and Rocq. Its core design choices are a surface that imitates mathematical natural language and a rule-driven automation layer that closes the routine steps a textbook would omit, so that an accepted proof can be re-emitted as a checked Lean file. Early experiments suggest that, even without any prover-specific training data, LLMs can learn to use it effectively on the miniF2F benchmark. Lean output excerpts: https://github.com/xiyuzhai-husky-lang/visored/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:06Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17579","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17579","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17579","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratyusha","familyName":"Vemuri","name":"Vemuri, Pratyusha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Features Can Hurt GNNs: Concatenation Interference on Homophilous Graph Benchmarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:30:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adding LLM-generated node features to graph neural networks (GNNs) is widely reported to improve accuracy on standard benchmarks. We document a contrasting observation: when LLM features are introduced through pure input concatenation (rather than joint training, distillation, or prompt-conditioning), they can systematically degrade accuracy on the same homophilous benchmarks where end-to-end LLM pipelines succeed. With an MLP backbone on the Planetoid public split and bag-of-words original features, concatenating SBERT-encoded GPT-4o-mini TAPE features reduces PubMed test accuracy by -17.0 +/- 0.3 pp and Cora by -4.3 +/- 0.6 pp (CiteSeer -0.6 +/- 0.8 pp, within seed noise). The drop attenuates as we relax each condition (GCN / GCNII / GAT backbones, random splits, smaller encoders) and reverses on medium-homophily WikiCS (+4.4 pp) and ogbn-arxiv (+11.7 pp). To predict when concatenation helps versus hurts, we report a simple measure of LLM-alone discriminability, Delta_sig. Across 9 datasets Delta_sig correlates with the concatenation cost more strongly than homophily at point estimate (r^2 = 0.38 vs. 0.06; N=9, bootstrap CIs overlap). The bootstrap-best change-point is tau = 13.8 pp, and the rule \"Delta_sig \u0026lt;= tau predicts non-positive concat cost\" classifies 7/9 datasets correctly; since 60% of bootstrap samples place tau in [5, 30] pp, we treat Delta_sig as an interpretive lens rather than a precision filter. A dimension-controlled ablation on PubMed places the LLM-feature drop between same-source PCA (-2.3 pp) and same-dim Gaussian noise (-37.3 pp), ruling out dimensionality and weight-decay artifacts. Nine PubMed configurations fit a power law |Delta_concat| proportional to (sqrt(d_l/n))^1.31 with r^2 = 0.97; the low-Delta_sig, small-n corner is exactly where the headline -17 pp PubMed deficit appears."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17579","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:03Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osamu","familyName":"Ito","name":"Ito, Osamu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akihiko","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Akihiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshikazu","familyName":"Nakagawa","name":"Nakagawa, Yoshikazu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shin","familyName":"Saeki","name":"Saeki, Shin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Shiraishi","name":"Shiraishi, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masato","familyName":"Sasaki","name":"Sasaki, Masato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate Assisted Pedestrian Protection Design via a Foundation Model Orchestrated Workflow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:27:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI-driven engineering workflows face particular challenges in crash safety design: unlike aerodynamics, crash events involve highly nonlinear contact dynamics, material nonlinearity, and discrete state transitions that are difficult to capture with data-driven surrogate models. To the best of our knowledge, we present the first foundation model--orchestrated workflow for crash safety design that enables surrogate-assisted exploration for pedestrian protection, reducing evaluation time from hours per CAE simulation to seconds.\n The workflow integrates four components: (1) a surrogate trained on CAE crash simulations to predict pedestrian leg injury metrics from design parameters, achieving an average $R^2=0.87$ and providing distribution-free conformal prediction intervals; (2) multiobjective evolutionary search (NSGA-II) to discover diverse feasible parameter sets under user-specified constraints; (3) a morphing-based geometry generator that maps parameters to topology-preserving 3D shapes; and (4) a natural-language interface in which an LLM orchestrates the workflow and a vision--language model supports semantic comparison of generated designs.\n In an automotive front-bumper case study, the workflow produces 35 distinct safety-compliant alternatives from a single exploration, a process that would require weeks with conventional CAE iteration. These results suggest that foundation models can serve as integration layers between ML surrogates and physics-based simulation, helping bring AI capabilities to safety-critical engineering domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:00Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepInsight: A Unified Evaluation Infrastructure Across the Physical AI Stack"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:22:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating a Physical AI stack spans operators that differ by more than three orders of magnitude -- from a single foundation-model decoding step to thousands of physics ticks of whole-body control -- varying orthogonally in modality, reward semantics, and resource profile. No existing framework spans this range, so the stack is evaluated today by stitching together separate harnesses that share neither runtime nor scoring, preserving each segment's local validity but losing the shared identity needed to diagnose cross-layer regressions. We present DeepInsight, an evaluation infrastructure that serves this full spectrum on a single runtime. Rather than homogenize the regimes, it preserves their heterogeneity behind three narrow abstractions -- task, resource, and result -- each realized as one invariant shared by every subsystem: one episode driver, one resource-handle protocol implemented by every expensive backend (LLM inference and sandboxed runtimes alike), and one trace identity scheme under which every event is written. Deployed in production across all three layers of an embodied humanoid stack, this single set of invariants onboards new benchmarks largely by configuration. Where mature peer orchestrators exist -- at the foundation-model end -- it reproduces published references and peer-framework readings within their own spread, runs the same suites faster on a single node, and scales near-linearly across nodes. Its distinctive return is diagnostic: because every layer writes into one shared trace, a regression that begins in one layer and surfaces in another stays localizable on that trace -- a cross-layer payoff no federation of per-segment harnesses can reproduce."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duling","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Duling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangzheng","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Bangzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jidong","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Jidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cordon: Semantic Transactions for Tool-Using LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-using LLM agents are shifting the unit of computation from explicit human-issued commands to model-driven tasks with stateful consequences. Yet today's agent runtimes still expose tools as isolated RPCs. This interface gives runtimes a convenient integration point, but it lacks a task-scoped execution boundary for commit, rollback, recovery, and audit across multi-step agent workflows. We argue that this mismatch calls for a runtime containment boundary rather than another per-call guardrail. This paper introduces Cordon, a transactional runtime system for staging and validating irreversible agent effects before commit. A semantic transaction is a task-level execution boundary that binds tool intents and runtime-tracked result lineage to reversible local state, staged external effects, delegated authority, and audit metadata. Cordon implements this abstraction with a transaction manager that tracks derived result objects, executes reversible mutations in shadow state, stages outward-facing actions in an effect outbox, and records recovery metadata. The runtime then validates the composed execution flow before it commits state or releases external effects. Our evaluation across adversarial and benign workflows shows that Cordon exposes cross-step violations missed by existing defenses. It also reduces irreversible-effect failures while preserving benign task completion with modest approval and latency overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanjiang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fanjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. S. Eugene","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, T. S. Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yarong","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AoiZora: Topology-Aware Auto-Parallel Optimization for Inference of Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:31:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video diffusion has quickly grown into a key generative serving workload, yet producing each clip demands many denoising iterations over large spatio-temporal latents, which puts low-latency inference out of reach on a single device. A denoising step is therefore typically distributed across multiple accelerators, and TPU sub-slices have become an attractive and practical fabric for doing so. Current auto-parallel systems, however, search almost exclusively over logical device meshes and disregard how a chosen sharding is actually laid out on the physical TPU interconnect -- an oversight that leaves large, topology-dependent performance on the table. We address this gap with AoiZora, a compiler-mediated topology planner built for low-latency video diffusion inference on TPU sub-slices. Its guiding principle is to reconnect logical sharding with physical placement by drawing on different points in the compilation flow: AoiZora first eliminates weak sharding candidates from inexpensive pre-compilation IRs, then compiles only the ones that survive and orders their physical placements using compiled HLO together with a topology-aware communication model. The winning plan is realized along the ordinary compiler path, leaving model code, compiler lowering, collective kernels, and network routing entirely intact. On TPU v5e sub-slices, AoiZora reduces Wan 2.1 one-step denoising latency by as much as 1.42x relative to existing solutions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:44Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Qiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingdong","familyName":"Pi","name":"Pi, Yingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lekang","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Lekang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometric Consistency Protocol for Foundation Model Features in Multi-View Satellite Imagery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:09:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:31:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standardized evaluation protocols are indispensable for robust benchmarking in remote sensing, particularly as foundation features are increasingly transferred across diverse sensors and complex imaging geometries. In satellite multi-view reconstruction, conventional evaluations relying on unconstrained 2D global matching are often misleading. The Rational Function Model (RFM) and its Rational Polynomial Coefficients (RPC) dictate a curved, height-dependent epipolar geometry that render flat 2D search spaces physically inconsistent. We propose a geometry-faithful and reproducible protocol tailored for the RPC framework. Our approach integrates an RPC-projected 3D consistency metric with a geometry-constrained dense matching proxy, specifically evaluating whether similarity responses remain localized and unique under physically plausible search manifolds. A pivotal finding of our joint reporting strategy is the decoupling of semantic agreement and geometric localization: high cross-view similarity at a projected 3D point does not guarantee reliable matchability in practical inference. Our benchmark demonstrates that incorporating geometric constraints is fundamental to the problem definition in satellite imagery. Furthermore, we show that state-of-the-art 2D backbones remain remarkably competitive against specialized 3D-aware models when subjected to this RPC-consistent evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The manuscript is accepted as Oral Presentation in IEEE International Geoscience and Remote Sensing Symposium(IGARSS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:41Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17546","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17546","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17546","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congjie","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Congjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyi","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changshui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Changshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEAGym: An Evaluation Environment for Self-Evolving LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:30:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-evolving LLM-based agents improve mainly by changing their agent harness: the structured execution layer around a base model, including prompts, memory, tools, middleware, runtime state, and the model-tool interaction loop. Existing evaluations often reduce this process to isolated task scores or a single sequential curve, obscuring whether an update produces reusable improvement, overfits recent tasks, increases cost, or harms older behavior. We introduce SEAGym, an evaluation environment for measuring agent harness updates across training, validation, test, replay, and cost records. SEAGym turns Harbor-compatible benchmarks into dynamic self-evolution task sources with train batches, frozen update-validation, held-out ID and OOD transfer views, replay diagnostics, and saved snapshot and metric records. Instantiating SEAGym on Terminal-Bench 2.0 and HLE, we compare ACE, TF-GRPO, and AHE under a shared epoch/batch protocol. The results show that these evaluation views provide complementary signals about the evolution process: frequent updates may fail to improve held-out performance, useful intermediate snapshots may collapse later, and source diversity and model backend can affect harness reliability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17546","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Fukuda","name":"Fukuda, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takatomo","familyName":"Kano","name":"Kano, Takatomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddhant","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Siddhant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Delcroix","name":"Delcroix, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naohiro","familyName":"Tawara","name":"Tawara, Naohiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atsunori","familyName":"Ogawa","name":"Ogawa, Atsunori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuya","familyName":"Chiba","name":"Chiba, Yuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atsushi","familyName":"Ando","name":"Ando, Atsushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models Abilities for Addressee, Turn-change, and Next Speaker Prediction in Meetings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:46:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:30:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate turn-taking in multimodal multi-party conversations using large language models (LLMs). We construct an evaluation framework for three tasks: addressee detection, turn-change prediction, and next speaker prediction. We compare supervised models trained for these tasks, text-based LLMs, multimodal LLMs (MM-LLMs), and human subjects. Experiments on the AMI corpus showed that LLMs outperformed supervised models and humans in next speaker prediction, despite not being trained on the target domain and without access to audio or visual information. An MM-LLM performed better than text-based LLMs on addressee detection and turn-change prediction but remained below human performance, indicating difficulty leveraging raw audio-visual signals. Ablation analyses revealed that conversational context was critical, particularly for next speaker prediction. We observed that human and LLM prediction patterns were similar, and intervals with frequent turn changes were difficult for both."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yatai","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yatai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Chieh","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, An-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ka Chun","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Ka Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vidya Nariyambut","familyName":"Murali","name":"Murali, Vidya Nariyambut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavlo","familyName":"Molchanov","name":"Molchanov, Pavlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Kautz","name":"Kautz, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"See","name":"See, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcing Dual-Path Reasoning in Spatial Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:32:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatial VLMs have made substantial progress in geometric perception, yet complex spatial reasoning requiring multi-step inference over depth, distance, and scene relations remains challenging. Moreover, different spatial queries call for fundamentally different strategies: some are best addressed through purely linguistic, step-by-step deduction, while others require explicit 3D grounding before quantitative inference. We present Dual-Path Spatial Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning for Spatial VLMs (SR-REAL), a unified framework that equips a spatial VLM with two complementary reasoning paths: Language-Only Reasoning (LOR), which performs step-by-step linguistic deduction, and Detect-Then-Reason (DTR), which detects 3D geometric cues (e.g., centers or bounding boxes) via region tokens before explicit geometric inference. SR-REAL begins with a cold-start supervised fine-tuning stage that constructs LOR and DTR chain-of-thought supervision and exposes a region-to-3D interface, followed by RL that optimizes the policy model with accuracy and format rewards; for DTR, a discrete center-based detection reward further refines geometric alignment. Across diverse spatial benchmarks, SR-REAL significantly outperforms spatial VLM baselines: (i) a single RL-trained model supports both reasoning paths, with DTR excelling in region-aware tasks through precise 3D localization and LOR enhancing general spatial reasoning; (ii) jointly training both paths fosters mutual reinforcement; (iii) high-quality, blended cold-start data is crucial for stable RL optimization; and (iv) the model generalizes across datasets and domains without per-task tuning, demonstrating positive transfer between LOR and DTR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:06Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengqian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chengqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhua","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Wenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingcai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Bingcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuqin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiquan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniDrive: An LLM-Choreographed Multi-Agent World Model with Unified Latent Co-Compression for Multi-View Driving Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:25:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:29:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative world models for autonomous driving face two unresolved tensions: heterogeneous control injection, where free-form language, HD-maps, trajectories, and camera poses reside in incompatible representational spaces, and post-hoc cross-view fusion, where per-camera latents fail to encode global 3-D geometry. We trace both to a single root cause: the absence of a shared symbolic interlingua aligning language, geometry, and pixels at the latent-token level. We present DRIVE-CHOREO, an LLM-choreographed multi-agent world model that recasts controllable multi-view video generation as latent choreography. Three Qwen2.5-VL agents - a Director parsing user intent into a structured WorldScript, a Cartographer grounding it into spatially-anchored layout tokens, and an Auditor feeding cross-view critiques back as auxiliary supervision - jointly author a single position-aware token sequence. This sequence is co-compressed with the multi-view video via a view-time permutation that enforces inter-camera geometry within the convolutional receptive field of a 3-D VAE. On nuScenes, DRIVE-CHOREO sets new state-of-the-art multi-view consistency and BEV mAP (21.6) with competitive FVD (45.7); a detector trained purely on our synthetic data gains +2.4 NDS on the real validation split, validating downstream utility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:02Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Donghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyi","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Dingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nina","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Nina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yunhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jinhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Public transit gains and spatially uneven travel demand changes after NYC congestion pricing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:29:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"New York City implemented the nation's first cordon-based congestion pricing program in January 2025, providing an opportunity to evaluate how system-wide urban mobility responds to large-scale pricing interventions. Because such policies generate spillovers across modes and locations, credible control groups are difficult to construct. We address this challenge using time series foundation models to generate probabilistic counterfactual demand forecasts with calibrated uncertainty. Applying this framework to bus, subway, and aggregate trip volume data, we find that post-policy bus and subway ridership increased significantly relative to expected no-policy demand, while overall travel demand decreased modestly. The effects are spatially heterogeneous: while reductions in overall travel demand are concentrated within the Congestion Relief Zone, transit gains extend beyond Manhattan's core. Socio-demographic analyses further reveal uneven adaptation across neighborhoods, highlighting spatial equity implications. Our framework provides a scalable approach for the uncertainty-aware evaluation of system-wide urban interventions when clean control groups are unavailable."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganzhao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ganzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MGUP: A Momentum-Gradient Alignment Update Policy for Stochastic Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient optimization is essential for training large language models. Although intra-layer selective updates have been explored, a general mechanism that enables fine-grained control while ensuring convergence guarantees is still lacking. To bridge this gap, we propose \\textbf{MGUP}, a novel mechanism for selective updates. \\textbf{MGUP} augments standard momentum-based optimizers by applying larger step-sizes to a selected fixed proportion of parameters in each iteration, while applying smaller, non-zero step-sizes to the rest. As a nearly {plug-and-play} module, \\textbf{MGUP} seamlessly integrates with optimizers such as AdamW, Lion, and Muon. This yields powerful variants such as \\textbf{MGUP-AdamW}, \\textbf{MGUP-Lion}, and \\textbf{MGUP-Muon}. Under standard assumptions, we provide theoretical convergence guarantees for \\textbf{MGUP-AdamW} (without weight decay) in stochastic optimization. Extensive experiments across diverse tasks, including MAE pretraining, LLM pretraining, and downstream fine-tuning, demonstrate that our \\textbf{MGUP}-enhanced optimizers achieve superior or more stable performance compared to their original base optimizers. We offer a principled, versatile, and theoretically grounded strategy for efficient intra-layer selective updates, accelerating and stabilizing the training of large-scale models. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/MaeChd/MGUP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:48Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suresh","familyName":"Kothari","name":"Kothari, Suresh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking LLM Code Correction with Iterative Feedback Loops"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:47:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models have shown remarkable capabilities in code generation. However, most existing evaluations focus only on single-attempt accuracy and overlook the iterative refinement process that is central to real-world programming. This study presents a systematic investigation of LLMs' ability to rectify their own code through execution feedback. Using real-world programming problems across four models and two major programming languages, this study evaluates performance using iterative refinement framework where LLMs receive compiler error messages and testcase feedback after each attempt. This study introduces metrics to evaluate code failures, analyze rectification patterns, and compare the effectiveness of reasoning and non-reasoning models, offering actionable insights into both the understanding and practical application of feedback loops in LLM-driven code generation systems. Results show that reasoning models consistently improve over iterations, substantially outperforming non-reasoning models in leveraging feedback, while syntactic and runtime errors are far more tractable than logical or algorithmic failures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 14th Computing Conference 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacky","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jacky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phil","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Phil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohan","familyName":"Dhall","name":"Dhall, Mohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liming","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Liming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-as-Judge in Education: A Curriculum-Grounded Marking Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:27:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative AI and large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to question generation and automated assessment. However, deploying LLMs in preparation for high-stakes exams requires more than prompt engineering; it demands software pipelines that systematically ground model outputs in authorised curriculum artefacts and marking guidelines issued by education authorities. This paper presents a curriculum-grounded, configurable LLM-as-Judge pipeline for question-level marking, co-developed with an industrial partner, to support exam preparation for university admission. The pipeline identifies the relevant topics, subtopics, and cognitive demand of a question, and assembles verifiable and authorised context to support LLM judgement. Curriculum intent is operationalised through concrete syllabus artefacts, including prescribed verbs and outcomes, performance band descriptors, glossary definitions, and marking-guideline principles. A staged LLM workflow is employed to first generate question-specific rubrics, capturing structured expectations of performance, and then derive and evaluate marking criteria used to allocate marks to student responses. This design improves consistency, transparency, and alignment with official marking practices. Preliminary evaluation shows that the proposed LLM-as-Judge pipeline delivers marking outcomes comparable to human tutors, while yielding justifications that are more traceable to authorised curriculum artefacts and marking standards. The pipeline has also been integrated into an online study platform, where early deployment data provide initial insights into operational usage and manual overrides."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:24Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17500","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17500","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17500","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gram","familyName":"Koski","name":"Koski, Gram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Lipps","name":"Lipps, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenghua","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhenghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Abarajithan","name":"Abarajithan, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Kastner","name":"Kastner, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reconfigurable Computing Challenge: Transformer for Jet Tagging on Versal AI Engines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:22:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:27:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fccm68464.2026.00078","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based models achieve strong performance for jet tagging at the CERN LHC, but deploying them in low-latency, resource-constrained trigger systems is challenging. We present an initial implementation of a quantized, integer-only transformer for jet tagging on the AMD Versal AI Engine (AIE), mapping dense and multi-head attention (MHA) layers to AIE tiles. The main contribution is a reusable software framework that represents transformer layers as composable AIE building blocks and automatically generates the corresponding Vitis graph code from a high-level Python model description. This framework provides a foundation for future research and is released as open-source software at https://github.com/KastnerRG/particle_transformer_aie."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 4 figures. In FCCM 2026 proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17500","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online LLM Selection via Constrained Bandits with Time-Varying Demand"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:26:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in edge-cloud inference systems to handle diverse user tasks with heterogeneous accuracy, latency, and cost profiles. Selecting the appropriate LLM for each incoming task is critical for ensuring service quality and efficient resource utilization. However, model heterogeneity, stochastic and unknown performance characteristics, and time-varying task demands make static selection strategies inadequate. Real-world deployments often impose hard resource budgets such as monetary expenditure limits, along with soft service-level requirements such as latency guarantees. These constraints introduce additional challenges for online decision-making. We formulate this problem as a constrained stochastic bandit learning task, where the learner sequentially selects models under both packing-type (hard) and covering-type (soft) constraints, while adapting to time-varying task demand. The learner operates without access to the underlying reward, cost, or latency distributions and must rely on partial feedback. We develop a novel online learning algorithm that leverages confidence-bound estimates and demand predictions to balance reward maximization with long-term constraint satisfaction. We provide theoretical guarantees showing sublinear regret and sublinear covering constraint violations compared to an offline benchmark with full information. Experimental results on synthetic workloads demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of our approach in dynamic, resource-constrained environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 3 figures with multiple subfigures, 1 table, submitted for possible journal publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:01Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongxia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dongxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoding Hidden Deception in Reasoning LLMs: Activation Explainers for Deception Auditing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:41:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:25:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As LLMs acquire stronger reasoning capabilities, deceptive behavior becomes an increasingly serious safety concern. Existing deception monitors either score visible transcripts or derive scalar probe scores from representation vectors, leaving little inspectable evidence about why a response is suspicious. We introduce STATEWITNESS, an activation explainer for deception auditing. A separate decoder reads a target model's hidden states, then answers natural-language queries or emits structured reports about them. We evaluate STATEWITNESS on two target reasoning LLMs across seven deception datasets. STATEWITNESS reaches 0.916 mean AUROC, a relative gain of 11.6% over the best black-box text monitor and 25.0% over the best activation-probe baseline under the same evaluation protocol. When combined with existing monitors, STATEWITNESS reduces missed deceptive examples in simple threshold ensembles. Beyond scalar detection, the decoder returns query-level answers, schema reports, and token- or sentence-level evidence traces for human inspection. We view this interface as a potential building block for broader interpretability and alignment tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:47Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianda","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xianda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinhan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Pinhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruilin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Mang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StereoFactory: A Unified Merging Framework for Robust Stereo Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:36:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Stereo matching has advanced through foundation models trained on large-scale datasets, yet this paradigm suffers from a scalability bottleneck: incorporating new data requires costly joint retraining. Model merging offers a scalable post-hoc alternative by integrating knowledge from specialized models after source checkpoints are available. However, existing merging methods typically retain all available models or rely on greedy inclusion, which can preserve harmful task-vector interference. We propose StereoFactory, a coarse-to-fine evolutionary framework for adaptive model merging. Stage~1 employs a genetic algorithm to search the combinatorial space of model subsets, determining which models should participate. Stage~2 addresses module-level knowledge specialization (different functional modules exhibit distinct preferences for knowledge sources) through CMA-ES optimization of architecture-adaptive routing over the selected task vectors, with optional module-level scaling. Experiments across two architectures and four benchmarks demonstrate that StereoFactory consistently achieves the best four-benchmark average under the same checkpoint pool, reducing the average error from 3.80 to 3.30 on NMRF and from 2.88 to 2.19 on FoundationStereo relative to the strongest controlled baseline. The post-hoc search requires only 2.7--3.7\\% of the corresponding joint-retraining wall-clock time. Analysis reveals that knowledge contributions are inherently module-specific, and selected subsets can transfer across architectures with minimal degradation. Code will be publicly released upon acceptance at: https://github.com/XiandaGuo/StereoFactory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Huizi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenkong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Guangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxian","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jingxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent CY","familyName":"So","name":"So, Kent CY","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan YP","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Bryan YP","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun Kwok","familyName":"Wing","name":"Wing, Yun Kwok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yanqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhou","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIPatient Arena: EHR-grounded evaluation of large language models in end-to-end clinical consultation workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:35:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly considered for use in clinical consultation tasks, yet most medical evaluations remain static, single-turn, or narrowly outcome-based, limiting their ability to reflect the sequential, uncertain, and interactive nature of real-world care. Here, we propose AIPatient Arena, an EHRs-grounded evaluation framework for assessing the clinical utility of LLMs across eight dimensions of clinical competence. The framework integrates EHR data into patient-specific knowledge graphs, enabling multi-turn physician-patient interactions. We applied AIPatient Arena on a primary cohort of 437 patients and two out-of-distribution validation cohorts of 119 and 67 patients. We observe that LLMs performed well in medical interview questioning skills (QS; mean scores, 4.43-4.99/5), ethical and professional conduct (ET; 4.38-4.93/5), and clarity and transparency of clinical explanations (EX; 3.80-4.72/5). Performance was moderate in information integration (II; 3.19-4.21/5) and medication safety and justification (MS; 3.13-3.78/5), but persistent weaknesses were observed in handling of ambiguous patient responses (HR; 2.57-3.32/5), information coverage (IC; 2.08-3.02/5), and diagnostic accuracy and reasoning (Dx; 2.63-3.55/5). Process-based evaluation revealed recurrent interaction failures, including repetitive questioning, omission of past medical history, and inadequate handling of uncertainty. Richer conversational context improved diagnostic reasoning but yielded limited gains in treatment planning. These findings indicate that final-answer accuracy alone is insufficient for evaluating clinical readiness and highlight the importance of assessing how models gather, interpret, and communicate information throughout a consultation. AIPatient Arena provides an EHR-grounded framework for workflow-oriented pre-deployment evaluation of medical LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages, 12 figues, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey G.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jeffrey G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marvin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Marvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Neel","name":"Neel, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CheckMIABench: Firm Foundations For Membership Inference Attacks on Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:26:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Membership inference attacks (MIAs) are a canonical way to assess a machine learning model's privacy properties. Although several attempts have been made to evaluate MIAs on language models, the extant literature has suffered numerous difficulties in constructing clean evaluations to test new techniques. In particular, subtle distribution shifts between member and non-member sets can undermine the statistical validity of MIAs; recent work has underscored this by showing that \"blind\" methods with no access to the underlying model can perform far better than published methods on the same benchmarks. This paper constructs a benchmark for principled evaluation of MIAs against LLMs, by leveraging the insight that training data before and after a fixed point during training are drawn from the same distribution. Therefore, all open-source models with intermediate checkpoints and public training data can be converted into MIA testbeds. We apply our framework to a half-dozen published attacks on the Pythia and OLMo family of models, from 70M to 7B parameters. To facilitate further privacy research, we open-source a modular library for designing and implementing attacks in this setting: https://github.com/safr-ai-lab/pandora_llm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:29Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Tung","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Chia-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Cunxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brucek","familyName":"Khailany","name":"Khailany, Brucek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AUTOGATE: Automated Clock Gating via Toggling-Aware LLM-based RTL Rewriting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-grain clock gating (FGCG) is among the most effective techniques for reducing dynamic power, yet current FGCG optimization flows remain largely manual. Recent LLM-based RTL optimization approaches remain limited by two key drawbacks: (1) the inability to process long waveform traces spanning millions of cycles, and (2) the difficulty of scaling optimization to large hierarchical codebases while preserving correctness. In this work, we present AUTOGATE, the first agentic framework for industry-grade RTL power optimization, enabling workload-aware clock-gating optimization across large hierarchical codebases. AUTOGATE introduces a Machine Learning (ML)-LLM co-design that bridges waveform-level analysis and RTL rewriting. Specifically, we design an ML-based clustering algorithm that distills raw toggling traces into compact, structured representations that guide LLM-based RTL rewriting. This enables accurate identification and application of clock-gating opportunities without requiring LLMs to directly process raw waveform data. To enhance scalability, AUTOGATE employs a hierarchical multi-agent architecture that decomposes large designs into independently optimizable modules, enabling coordinated optimization across deep design hierarchies. We evaluate AUTOGATE on a diverse set of designs ranging from small RTL designs to large industrial-grade codebases. Experimental results show that AUTOGATE consistently reduces dynamic power relative to baselines. Across the small-design suite, AUTOGATE reduces dynamic power by 49.31% on average. On industry-scale designs, it achieves 19.34% and 7.96% dynamic power reductions on NVDLA and BlackParrot, respectively, and up to 6.86% on highly optimized proprietary production designs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqing","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xueqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfei","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Lingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuohan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhuohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can LLMs Be CEOs? Benchmarking Strategic Resource Reallocation with Multi-Role Agent Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:20:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating the decision-making capabilities of large language models (LLMs) is a growing research priority, yet existing benchmarks focus on isolated cognitive tasks such as reasoning, knowledge retrieval, and economic rationality in stylized settings. These evaluations overlook the defining challenge of real executive decision-making: integrating conflicting recommendations from specialized stakeholders under information asymmetry, organizational constraints, and temporal dependencies. We introduce \\textsc{CEO-Bench}, a multi-agent benchmark that evaluates LLMs on CEO-level strategic resource reallocation -- the process of redirecting capital across business units in a multi-round, constraint-rich organizational environment. In \\textsc{CEO-Bench}, LLM agents receive conflicting advice from four role-conditioned C-suite advisors (CFO, CTO, COO, CMO), each with private signals and distinct priorities, and must synthesize these into a concrete allocation plan evaluated along four dimensions: role integration, conditional boldness, history-sensitive judgment, and plan validity. Experiments across five frontier models on 13 scenarios reveal that all models achieve high structural validity but diverge sharply on strategic calibration -- the hardest capability layer. We identify systematic failure modes including single-advisor capture, conservative default under ambiguity, and historical amnesia, and uncover a structural integration-boldness tradeoff: models that engage more deeply with conflicting perspectives tend to produce less decisive action. These findings delineate the current capability boundary of LLMs as organizational decision-makers and inform the design of future AI-assisted executive systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiya","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ICBCBench: An Industry Consortium Benchmark for Financial Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:19:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Deep Research Agents in knowledge-intensive domains such as finance, establishing reliable and domain-aligned evaluation standards remains a critical challenge. Existing benchmarks focus on either closed-ended question answering or open-ended report evaluation, failing to jointly capture retrieval-reasoning accuracy and end-to-end research quality required in real-world workflows. We introduce ICBCBench, a consortium-driven benchmark for financial deep research, developed in collaboration with domain experts from a broad range of financial institutions and academia, involving over 50 experts across more than 40 organizations. It adopts a dual-track paradigm integrating objective tasks with verifiable answers and subjective long-form report evaluation, enabling complementary assessment of retrieval-reasoning accuracy and end-to-end report quality in terms of expert alignment, citation consistency, and source quality. Experiments on state-of-the-art DRAs and large language models reveal substantial gaps in complex reasoning, factual grounding, and report quality, highlighting the challenges of achieving industry-level performance. Our dataset and evaluation framework are available at https://github.com/DeepFin-Intelligence/ICBCBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 14 figures. Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zehang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamin","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MODE-RAG: Manifold Outlier Diagnosis and Energy-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:06:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (M-RAG) enhances Large Vision-Language Models, it remains highly susceptible to cross-modal hallucinations, causal fabrications, and sycophancy. Furthermore, existing mitigation pipelines often face an intervention paradox: static rules tend to unnecessarily disrupt accurate generations, whereas leaving the multi-modal reasoning completely unguided allows existing mismatches to cascade into severe logical fabrications. To quantify and mitigate these hallucinations, we propose a Multi-Agent system, MODE-RAG, driven by Variational Free Energy (VFE) and internal attention states to dynamically gate interventions. High-risk queries are routed to five stage-specific agents, integrating Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for rigorous causal derivation and logit perturbations to penalize sycophancy. Dedicated Correction and Overseer agents ensure formatting stability and perform post-hoc factual verification. To objectively evaluate our approach, we introduce ModeVent, a challenging subset derived from the MultiVent dataset. Extensive experiments indicate that our system effectively reduces hallucination rates and logical fabrication, significantly improving the robustness of M-RAG systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be presented at ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:09Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17436","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17436","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17436","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongjun","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Xiongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianjiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jianjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UoU: A Universal Fingerprint Foundation Model Based on Large-Scale Unsupervised Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:22:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fingerprint recognition is still dominated by task-specific pipelines, where enhancement, structural parsing, alignment, and matching are optimized in isolation. Although effective in narrow settings, this design limits representation reuse across sensors, qualities, and downstream applications. We therefore present UoU, short for ``a \\textbf{U}niversal fingerprint foundation model based \\textbf{o}n large-scale \\textbf{U}nsupervised learning,'' which reframes fingerprint feature extraction as a domain-specific foundation-model problem. UoU is organized around a multi-level representation hierarchy spanning image restoration, structural fields, semantic tokens, point-level biometric entities, and compact global descriptors. Its training recipe combines a supervised cold start on precise annotations, large-scale weakly supervised refinement, and large-scale unsupervised consolidation, with the latter two stages iterated during large-scale training so that weak supervision broadens semantic coverage while unsupervised learning stabilizes correspondences, invariances, and representation geometry. Rather than treating fingerprint imagery as generic texture, UoU exploits domain-specific symmetries and intermediate structure, including orientation flow, periodic ridge patterns, sparse biometric entities, and spatial equivariance. The framework is intentionally architecture-agnostic: while the present study includes an initial transformer-based structured-prediction instantiation, the broader design supports multi-task learning, scalable model configurations, and downstream specialization for matching, alignment, enhancement, registration, and related fingerprint applications. This paper presents the technical motivation, system design, and validation protocol of UoU, and part of the baseline implementation is publicly available at https://github.com/XiongjunGuan/UoU."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17436","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Martingale Doppelgänger-Eval: An Identification Framework for Auditing Candlestick Understanding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:10:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:21:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Martingale Doppelgänger-Eval, a public shadow-market benchmark for auditing whether vision-language models (VLMs) use candlestick evidence rather than extrapolate past trends. The central difficulty is identification: on real market histories, chart evidence and trend are strongly coupled, so an observational score cannot determine whether a fluent technical-analysis narrative is grounded in local visual evidence. We prove this limitation formally: no evaluation functional computed from observational chart--label data can distinguish a grounded responder from a trend-shortcut responder under strong coupling, whereas matched evidence interventions separate the same responders at an exponential rate and trend--label swaps provide an independent shortcut stress test. The benchmark therefore evaluates frozen VLMs on rendered OHLCV charts under four controlled mechanisms: a martingale-null market, injected-alpha counterfactual pairs, trend-confounder swaps, and regime shifts. A structural behavioral model identifies null-market bias, trend sensitivity, evidence sensitivity, prompt/renderer fragility, and evidence faithfulness; the accompanying statistical toolkit provides minimum detectable effects, block-aware sequential testing for metered APIs, and an overlap-weighted artifact check. Across frozen commercial and open VLMs, the identified regression assigns large positive coefficients to past trend but evidence coefficients that are zero or opposite to the rule-implied sign. Matched-pair analyses show that models either ignore injected candlestick semantics or move opposite to the rule-implied direction conditional on responding. The benchmark isolates a failure mode that standard observational chart benchmarks cannot detect and gives a reusable audit template for time-series imagery with controllable label mechanisms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:36Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kailun","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Kailun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawu","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Dawu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bifrost: Hybrid TEE-FHE Inference for Privacy-Preserving Transformer and LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:06:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:21:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cloud-hosted transformer and large language model (LLM) inference creates a direct confidentiality problem: user prompts may contain sensitive code, business data, personal information, or regulated documents, yet remote serving exposes intermediate state to the cloud software stack and accelerator runtime. Fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) keeps accelerator-side execution ciphertext-only, but end-to-end LLM inference remains expensive because linear layers are interleaved with non-linear, cache-state, and refresh-sensitive operators. CPU trusted execution environments (TEEs) can execute those operators natively, but a CPU TEE alone does not define how an untrusted accelerator should participate.\n We present Bifrost, a hybrid TEE-FHE serving architecture in which secrets are provisioned only to an attested CPU TEE, while the accelerator, device memory, driver/runtime stack, and host software remain outside the trusted computing base. Bifrost uses FHE as a secure delegation mechanism for projection and feed-forward linear layers on accelerator-backed CKKS, while non-linear operators, attention-side control logic, KV-state transitions, and decrypt-then-encrypt refresh execute inside the CPU TEE.\n Bifrost+ further applies a prefill/decode split: prompt-side KV state is built inside the CPU TEE, and only decode-side state enters the hybrid ciphertext path. In an estimator-style comparison matching Euston's methodology, Bifrost reduces projected latency by 9.25x on GPT-2 (1.5B) and 9.91x on LLaMA 3 (8B). In direct CKKS/FHE deployments, Bifrost+ reduces TTFT by 14.6-45.8x on GPT-2 (124M) and 15.3-53.4x on Qwen3 (0.6B). The systems lesson is selective encrypted execution: use FHE only where ciphertext-only accelerator delegation is required, and keep non-linear, refresh, and prompt-side work inside the CPU TEE."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Apoorva","familyName":"Kulkarni","name":"Kulkarni, Apoorva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaousheik","familyName":"Jayakumar","name":"Jayakumar, Kaousheik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sreyan","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sreyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Wiegreffe","name":"Wiegreffe, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinesh","familyName":"Manocha","name":"Manocha, Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramani","familyName":"Duraiswami","name":"Duraiswami, Ramani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Closer Look at Failure Modes in Temporal Understanding of Large Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:57:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:20:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) achieve strong performance on a variety of audio understanding tasks but continue to struggle with temporal reasoning, a fundamental capability central to human auditory perception. Understanding the causes of these failures remains challenging as existing benchmarks report performance gaps without probing underlying mechanisms. To address this, we introduce a benchmark with 1,657 questions across three foundational tasks designed specifically for mechanistic analysis. Examining model outputs across varying input settings (behavioral analysis) reveals that models often under-utilize audio when textual cues are available. We also provide the first causal mechanistic analysis of temporal reasoning failures in LALMs. Comparing attention upweighting against scaling, we find that redistributing attention across audio tokens is more effective than increasing audio attention. Targeting task-relevant tokens yields further gains. These findings suggest that modality imbalance alone cannot explain failures. Attention scaling at bottleneck layers improves accuracy from 55.9% to 59.1% without fine-tuning, demonstrating a promising direction for future work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:28Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac R.","familyName":"Christian","name":"Christian, Isaac R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udith","familyName":"Haputhanthrige","name":"Haputhanthrige, Udith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna","familyName":"Hornfeld","name":"Hornfeld, Hanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Declan","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Declan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Nastase","name":"Nastase, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Webb","name":"Webb, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Graziano","name":"Graziano, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Attention Alignment Between Humans and Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:47:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:20:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual perception depends on top-down goals and bottom-up sensory mechanisms. Vision-language models implement both, allowing us to treat each component as a separable hypothesis about what drives where we look. We compared spatial attention maps from six vision-language models against human fixation heatmaps recorded on 200 images during two tasks (general description and social captioning). The six models spanned a 2$\\times$2 factorial of CNN vs.\\ ViT encoders crossed with LSTM vs.\\ Transformer decoders, plus Molmo 7B-D and Qwen3.5 9B. We found that both decoder and encoder architecture shaped alignment, but decoder choice dominated. LSTM vs.\\ Transformer decoders increased alignment by 40--50 percentage points (80--87\\% vs.\\ 40--59\\% of the human noise ceiling). In contrast, CNN vs.\\ ViT encoders contributed a secondary 5--20 point advantage depending on decoder family, with CNN-LSTM the most aligned model overall (85--87\\%). Despite their alignment advantage, LSTM-decoder attention maps were spatially diffuse and minimally task-differentiated; ViT-Transformer, the weakest in alignment, showed the sharpest spatial concentration and strongest task differentiation. A hemispatial-neglect simulation confirmed that ablating attention impacted LSTM decoders more than Transformer decoders. In an exploratory extension using TRIBE-simulated synthetic neural responses, fixation alignment and neural relevance dissociate: CNN-Transformer attention maps better predicted synthetic brain activity despite lower fixation alignment, with attention maps best predicting early visual cortex. Together, top-down and bottom-up components trade off what they predict in behavioral and synthetic neural data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:19Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17409","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17409","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17409","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anar","familyName":"Nurizada","name":"Nurizada, Anar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anurag","familyName":"Purwar","name":"Purwar, Anurag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrete Autoregressive Transformer for Generative Mechanism Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:20:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Planar path synthesis requires mechanisms whose coupler curves match a prescribed trajectory; the mapping from curve to linkage is inherently one-to-many across four-, six-, and eight-bar topologies. We address this design problem with simulation-grounded evaluation on a curated corpus of over one million mechanisms, reporting Chamfer distance and dynamic time warping after forward kinematics and geometric alignment. We formulate synthesis as conditional autoregressive sequence modeling: joint coordinates are uniformly quantized to tokens and generated by a decoder-only transformer with a variational-autoencoder (VAE) latent of the target curve and an explicit mechanism-type token. Training combines token cross-entropy with a Gaussian-smoothed bin auxiliary loss that respects ordinal structure among bins. At inference, a bounded latent-noise schedule decodes all mechanism types at each noise level; we retain the top five candidates by geometric error, yielding diverse accurate families without dataset lookup. On held-out tests, aggregate mean Chamfer distance is $0.0132$ and mean dynamic time warping is $0.153$; a latent $k$-nearest-neighbor baseline that conditions on training-set neighbor latents in VAE space achieves matched-topology mean Chamfer distance $0.0071$ and mean dynamic time warping $0.117$ using the same decoder."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17409","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:18Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Logan","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Logan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Abdur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Saifullah","name":"Saifullah, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taaha","familyName":"Kazi","name":"Kazi, Taaha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasu","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Vasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NarrativeWorldBench: A Frontier-Saturated Benchmark and a Latent World Model for Long-Horizon Co-Creative Audio Drama"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:04:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:18:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-form serialized audio drama, with arcs that run for 200 to 800 episodes, is a major creative medium and a setting where frontier large language models (LLMs) fail. We benchmark 21 models, spanning classical, fine-tuned, open-frontier, closed-frontier, and reasoning tiers, on a uniform set of structural narrative metrics. All closed-frontier systems saturate at a plot-beat F1 in the band [0.78, 0.81] and collapse by about -0.20 F1 at horizon h=200. We introduce NarrativeWorldBench, an open benchmark of nine narrative-structure metrics evaluated across horizons h in {10, 20, 50, 100, 200}, with cross-lingual evaluation across four Indic languages (Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Marathi). We introduce N-VSSM, a Narrative Variational State-Space Model that maintains a structured 256-dimensional latent world state over more than 200 episodes via a Mamba-2 backbone with an event-conditioned posterior and an 8B decoder. N-VSSM holds plot-beat F1 \u0026gt;= 0.84 across all horizons at 4x lower compute than the closed-frontier band. A learned Cultural Transfer Function lifts cross-language fidelity by +0.20 to +0.23 Likert points. In a within-subjects writer study (n = 12 professional authors, 240 trials), N-VSSM is preferred over Claude Opus 4.5 on long-arc consistency 71% of the time and rated +1.3 Likert points higher on controllability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages. Accepted to the ICML 2026 Workshops on High-dimensional Learning Dynamics (HiLD) and Culture x AI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Logan","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Logan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajit","familyName":"Saravanan","name":"Saravanan, Ajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishan","familyName":"Dave","name":"Dave, Ishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saadullah","familyName":"Ismail","name":"Ismail, Saadullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikhar","familyName":"Shiromani","name":"Shiromani, Shikhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visuals Lie, Consistency Speaks: Disentangling Spatial Attention from Reliability in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T00:58:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:18:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Foundation Models are increasingly used as reasoning agents, making reliability, knowing when a model may hallucinate, critical. A common intuition, which we call the Attention-Confidence Assumption, holds that reliability follows from \"structural\" visual perception: tight attention on relevant regions should signal a trustworthy answer, while scattered attention signals confusion. We challenge this through the VLM Reliability Probe (VRP), a systematic cross-family study of reliability signals in contemporary Vision-Language Models (VLMs). We introduce structural-attention metrics, cluster counts (C_k) and spatial entropy (H_s), to quantify the visual encoder's gaze, and track its evolution (Delta H_s) across layers. This reveals a \"Symbolic Detachment\": models often \"Early Lock\" visual features only to diffuse attention later, severing early perception from final generation. Contrary to the grounding hypothesis, we find a \"Cluster Failure\": spatial attention has near-zero correlation (R approx 0.001) with accuracy. Instead, reliability is a phenomenon of generation dynamics and internal-state distributions. Self-Consistency, the agreement rate across sampled reasoning paths, is the dominant predictor of truth (R = 0.429). Scaling causal interventions exposes a sharp architectural divergence: LLaVA locks its prediction in a fragile late-stage bottleneck, whereas PaliGemma and Qwen2-VL distribute reliability globally, staying resilient even when ~50% or more of their most predictive layer is destroyed. For current VLMs, reliability signals are detached from visual grounding maps and are best inferred from generation-time dynamics and hidden-state probes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages. Accepted to the ICLR 2026 Workshop on Multimodal Intelligence. Code: https://github.com/itsloganmann/VLM-Reliability-Probe"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17383","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17383","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17383","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew Francis","familyName":"Dixon","name":"Dixon, Matthew Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Model Validation of Agentic AI Systems: A POMDP-Based Framework for Belief-State, Forecast, and Policy Validation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Risk Management (q-fin.RM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T37, 68T45, 68Q32, 93E11, 93E20, 60J05, 62C10, 62P05, 91G70, 91B06, 90C40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T00:40:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:17:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic artificial intelligence systems introduce a new class of model risk. Unlike traditional predictive models, autonomous agents continuously acquire information, form beliefs regarding latent states of the environment, generate forecasts, select actions, and adapt their behavior over time. Existing validation methodologies focus primarily on predictive accuracy and therefore provide limited insight into the quality of the underlying decision process. This paper proposes a model validation framework for agentic AI based on Partially Observable Markov Decision Processes (POMDPs). The framework decomposes autonomous decision making into information, beliefs, forecasts, actions, and utility, allowing each component to be validated independently. Large language models (LLMs) are formalized as approximate Bayesian filtering operators, and a model-risk taxonomy is developed encompassing state-space, filtering, forecast, policy, utility-specification, and parameter risks.\n The model risk validation methodology is demonstrated through a portfolio-management case study in which an agent infers latent market regimes from market and macroeconomic information, generates belief-conditioned forecasts, and constructs portfolios using a Black--Litterman framework. Empirical validation combines performance analysis, belief calibration diagnostics, coverage tests, ablation studies, and parameter-sensitivity analysis. The results indicate that latent-state inference contributes independently to decision quality and that the principal conclusions remain robust across a broad range of parameter values. The principal contribution of the paper is a practical framework for extending established model risk management concepts to autonomous AI systems and providing a rigorous foundation for their validation, governance, and monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 3 figures, 6 tables. Source code available from https://github.com/mfrdixon/agentic-AI-as-POMDP"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17383","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Deen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taotao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed General-Purpose Agent Networks: Architecture, Key Mechanisms, and Prototypes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T23:57:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:16:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have accelerated the transition from passive conversational assistants to autonomous agents that can understand goals, plan actions, invoke tools, and execute multi-step tasks. Yet the capability of a single agent remains constrained by its local data, tool permissions, runtime environment, and governance boundary. This paper studies distributed general-purpose agent networks: open peer-to-peer networks in which heterogeneous agents deployed on personal devices, edge nodes, or autonomous computing environments can discover one another, establish trust, negotiate cooperation rules, and execute open-ended tasks. We argue that such networks cannot be obtained by simply combining existing peer-to-peer overlays with conventional multi-agent systems. Unlike traditional P2P networks, agent networks must propagate semantic declarations about intentions, capabilities, states, and cooperation constraints. We therefore propose a layered architecture centered on a protocol adaptation layer that connects upper-level task semantics with lower-level network operations. Based on this architecture, the paper identifies three core mechanism problems: semantic announcement propagation for collaborator discovery, verifiable identity and multi-topic reputation for cooperation governance, and semantic-gradient mechanism design for open task execution. For each problem, we present a technical route, including bodyless gossip with sequential logs, BAID-based identity binding with MG-EigenTrust reputation, and a Stackelberg-style mechanism-generation loop driven by semantic attribution feedback. We further report prototype overhead results for BAID-style tiered verification and mechanism-level simulations of MG-EigenTrust under cross-topic disguise-collusion attacks. The resulting framework provides a system-level foundation for open, trustworthy, and scalable agent collaboration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Schmalfuss","name":"Schmalfuss, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Despoina","familyName":"Paschalidou","name":"Paschalidou, Despoina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanja","familyName":"Fidler","name":"Fidler, Sanja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kashyap","familyName":"Chitta","name":"Chitta, Kashyap","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose M.","familyName":"Alvarez","name":"Alvarez, Jose M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DriveJudge: Rethinking Autonomous Driving Evaluation with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T23:39:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:15:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving has shifted towards end-to-end policy learning, where reliable, interpretable policy evaluation is a fundamental challenge as driving quality is highly context-dependent. Commonly used rule-based driving metrics like EPDMS are interpretable but lack context-awareness, while recent VLMbased evaluations are context-aware but limited by ambiguous VLM outputs and weak physical grounding. To evaluate driving in a manner that is both interpretable and context-aware, we introduce DriveJudge. DriveJudge is a driving evaluation agent that combines rule-grounded evaluation with Vision-Language Model (VLM) reasoning and selectively invokes physically-grounded deterministic rule functions after interpreting the environmental context. To train and evaluate DriveJudge, we curate a large-scale dataset of 33,577 challenging driving samples with human annotations on whether the driving behavior is reasonable in the given scenario. With this dataset, we address the underexplored problem of driving metric evaluation, and introduce two human-aligned benchmark tasks: Driving Quality Classification and Trajectory Preference Selection. DriveJudge outperforms EPDMS for driving quality classification by 21.23 AUC, and the recent VLM-based DriveCritic for trajectory preference selection by 6.5%, setting a new standard for interpretable and precise driving evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongchao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Hongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger D.","familyName":"Soberanis-Mukul","name":"Soberanis-Mukul, Roger D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morgan","familyName":"Ringel","name":"Ringel, Morgan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mali","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Mali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saif Iftekar","familyName":"Sayed","name":"Sayed, Saif Iftekar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hedyeh","familyName":"Rafii-Tari","name":"Rafii-Tari, Hedyeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometry-Consistent Endoscopic Representations for Image-Guided Navigation via Structured Foundation Model Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T22:41:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:14:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate vision-based navigation in monocular endoscopy is difficult due to limited depth cues, weak tissue texture, non-rigid deformation, and substantial appearance variation across domains, all of which complicate pose estimation, depth prediction, and image-to-anatomy alignment. Although recent vision foundation models have shown promise, their learned representations often remain insufficiently geometry-consistent, hindering stable feature correspondence and limiting their reliability for downstream navigation tasks. We propose a unified framework for learning geometry-consistent and domain-robust image representations for monocular endoscopy. The framework combines a synthetic data pipeline that provides accurate geometric supervision with Hierarchy-Aware Geometry-Semantic Adaptation, a structured alternative to standard LoRA that inserts low-rank adapters selectively across the transformer hierarchy and couples them with layer-wise training objectives to encourage geometric correspondence in intermediate features and semantic consistency in deeper features. Experiments on public and proprietary datasets show improved geometric and semantic representation quality, leading to better performance on downstream navigation tasks including pose estimation and monocular depth estimation. The learned representations show favorable synthetic-to-real transfer on clinical bronchoscopy and provide a useful initialization for adaptation to sinus endoscopy and colonoscopy under limited supervision. The framework also shows favorable scaling with model size and training data. These results support hierarchy-aware, geometry-guided adaptation as a practical approach for endoscopic representation learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:55:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baishali","familyName":"Chaudhury","name":"Chaudhury, Baishali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengdie Flora","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengdie Flora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunji Hayley","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Hyunji Hayley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungmin","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae Oh","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Jae Oh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Consistency in LLM Logical Reasoning via Structural Uncertainty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models can arrive at the same answer through reasoning paths that are unstable, contradictory, or difficult to rank consistently -- a failure mode especially prevalent in multi-step deductive reasoning. Existing methods assess reliability primarily through output dispersion -- measuring how much sampled answers differ -- but this discards a complementary signal: whether the model can consistently rank competing reasoning candidates. We propose structural uncertainty, a consistency-aware framework derived from the stability of self-preference-induced rankings over sampled reasoning solutions. Given a query, we generate multiple candidate solutions and ask the model to judge pairwise preferences among its own outputs. We aggregate self-preferences into ranking distributions via Bradley-Terry modeling with PageRank, and decompose the signal into two entropy-based components: across-trial ranking instability and within-trial candidate ambiguity. Across five LLMs and eight benchmarks, structural signals provide information complementary to answer dispersion: on logical and mathematical reasoning tasks, the combination improves identification of unreliable instances, while on factual retrieval the structural signal collapses toward uniformity, diagnosing a regime boundary where reasoning-level consistency evaluation is uninformative. The two components relate differently to accuracy: within-trial ambiguity correlates positively with correctness -- consistent with settings where multiple plausible solution paths remain competitive -- while across-trial instability correlates negatively, signaling unreliable reasoning. Structural uncertainty is best understood not as a universal confidence estimator, but as a regime-sensitive evaluator of logical reasoning consistency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026 Workshop on Logical Reasoning of Large Language Models. Accepted as best paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Collin","name":"Collin, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Ayub","name":"Ayub, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Abstention-Aware Personalized Object Rearrangement via Uncertainty-Guided LLM Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:12:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic assistance in household environments requires not only predicting where objects should be placed, but also reasoning about when objects should not be placed at all. Existing approaches to personalized object rearrangement primarily focus on placement decisions under the assumption of clean observations and complete actionability, limiting their applicability in realistic, cluttered, and partially erroneous settings. In this paper, we introduce APOLLO, a hybrid framework for abstention-aware personalized object rearrangement that combines a lightweight, personalized embedding model (PEM) with selective large language model (LLM) assistance. PEM is trained for each user-environment pair using a small number of demonstrations, operates entirely on CPU, and produces uncertainty estimates, which are used to selectively invoke LLM-based reasoning only for ambiguous decisions, balancing efficiency, privacy, and reasoning capability. To evaluate this formulation beyond existing benchmarks, we introduce APOR, a synthetic, LLM-generated dataset that captures room-level, multi-furniture environments, diverse organizational profiles, explicit abstention behavior, and noisy partial scene context. Extensive experiments on both PARSEC and APOR provide initial evidence that APOLLO improves over prior LLM-based baselines in controlled benchmark settings while substantially reducing LLM usage. Code is available at https://github.com/PaInt-Lab/APOLLO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 2026 IEEE 35th International Conference on Robot and Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:55:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maeva","familyName":"Guerrier","name":"Guerrier, Maeva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koki","familyName":"Kobayashi","name":"Kobayashi, Koki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jana","familyName":"Pavlasek","name":"Pavlasek, Jana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Beltrame","name":"Beltrame, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VISTA: Scale-Aware Visual Navigation via Action History Conditioning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:01:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Navigation Foundation Models (VNMs) promise end-to-end learned navigation policies capable of zero-shot deployment across diverse embodiments and environments. To maintain generality, many vision-based navigation models predict normalized actions. However, this normalization introduces a critical deployment vulnerability: applying different scaling factors to the same normalized trajectory alters its physical geometry, which degrades navigation performance and increases collision risks. We address this vulnerability by conditioning the model on normalized action histories alongside image observations, providing explicit context on the relationship between the model's predictions and the robot's actual physical displacement. Furthermore, current VNMs often struggle in visually repetitive environments that lack distinct features. To resolve this issue, we integrate a DINOv3 encoder, whose richer representations enable our model to capture both spatial and geometric dimensions between observations. VISTA generalizes robustly to out-of-distribution environments, achieving 100% goal prediction accuracy in zero-shot, real-world deployment in Outdoor, Forest and Office settings, and an average of 95% checkpoints crossed, demonstrating consistent path following in unseen environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:54:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17281","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17281","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17281","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungwon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyungwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kandarp","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Kandarp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lillian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Lillian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavel","familyName":"Golik","name":"Golik, Pavel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petar","familyName":"Aleksic","name":"Aleksic, Petar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are you speaking my languages? On spoken language adherence in multimodal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T20:44:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:10:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Language Model (LLM) based Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) enables seamless multilingual use, models often misidentify the output language, compromising transcription fidelity and downstream application quality. To preserve flexibility and code-switching capabilities, we propose a soft prompting approach that hints at potential spoken languages without strictly constraining the output. We formally define this challenge as a lack of language adherence, introduce a novel metric to quantify violations, and evaluate three mitigation strategies: (1) zero-shot prompting for robust guidance under uncertainty, (2) supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to improve prompt adherence, and (3) Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to enforce adherence during decoding. We present a comparative analysis of these methods across multiple languages, evaluating effectiveness in reducing the language violation while maintaining overall ASR performance. Finally, we discuss trade-offs to guide strategy selection under various compute constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 tables in the main body"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17281","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:28Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:54:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqing","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training LLMs with Reinforcement Learning over Digital Twin Representations for Reasoning-Intensive Surgical VideoQA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T20:40:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:10:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Surgical video question answering requires multi-step reasoning across semantic, spatial, and temporal dimensions. Existing methods architecturally compress videos into discrete token representations and couple visual perception with reasoning. This approach fragments continuous spatial-temporal relationships and has been shown to restrict multi-step reasoning capabilities. We introduce a reinforcement learning (RL) framework that trains large language models (LLMs) to decouple perception from reasoning by operating over digital twin representations constructed from surgical foundation models. Additionally, we introduce hierarchical representations across frame, temporal window, and procedure levels with probabilistic uncertainty estimates. Finally, we propose a novel reward that combines format validation with accuracy assessment through clinical plausibility evaluation and uncertainty-aware calibration for training. To demonstrate the capabilities of this approach, we introduce REAL-Colon-Reason, a colonoscopic benchmark with 2000 question-answer pairs across three complexity levels. We achieve state-of-the-art performance on REAL-Colon-Reason and two existing surgical VideoQA benchmarks REAL-Colon-VQA and EndoVis18-VQA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:54:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liheng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Liheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian-Yun","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jian-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Groups in Critic-Free RLVR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:49:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a central paradigm for post-training large language models. Existing critic-free RL methods typically generate a group of rollouts for the same question to estimate value baselines for advantage computation. However, this design suffers from data inefficiency, group synchronization barriers, and inflexibility with structured rollouts. In this work, we revisit the role of the ``group'' and show that its underlying function is not merely to estimate baselines but to prevent false penalties on negative samples. Building on this insight, we propose negative token filtering, a simple and effective strategy that enables stable single-rollout training. We apply it to two batch-level advantage methods, achieving comparable performance on reasoning tasks and stronger performance on agentic tasks relative to group-based RL techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:47Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maram","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Maram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savitra","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Savitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hariseetharam","familyName":"Gunduboina","name":"Gunduboina, Hariseetharam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daksh","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Daksh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Haris","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Haris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhasis","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Subhasis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biplab","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Biplab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoDisaster: Benchmarking Orchestrated Agents for Operational Disaster Geo-Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:46:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Remote-sensing vision-language models (RS-VLMs) have advanced Earth-observation analysis toward visual interpretation and instruction-following, yet fall short of operational geo-intelligence, which demands tool-grounded spatial reasoning and structured, evidence-backed decisions. We introduce GeoDisaster, an operational geospatial disaster reasoning benchmark with 2,921 verified instances across 43 question types and five task families: deforestation monitoring, multi-hazard analysis, building-damage assessment, flood-safe routing, and Sentinel-1 SAR flood monitoring. Instances integrate heterogeneous EO/GIS evidence-optical and SAR imagery, raster masks, vector geometries, road networks, and exposure layers-spanning hazard detection, damage assessment, exposure estimation, and diagnostic report generation. Ground-truth answers are grounded in executable geospatial workflows and deterministic consistency checks, removing the need for language-model annotation. We further propose an orchestrated multi-agent framework with 18 disaster-oriented tools, where role-specialized agents coordinate through explicit execution contracts, aligned via Role-Contract Expectation Alignment (RCEA): failure-aware supervised fine-tuning combined with contract-grounded reinforcement learning over dense step-level signals. Experiments show that GeoDisaster challenges existing RS-VLMs and agentic systems, while RCEA improves tool use, evidence grounding, state consistency, and decision generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 11 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thainara","familyName":"Lima","name":"Lima, Thainara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitor","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, Vitor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Landsat-Sentinel-2 Algal Bloom Mapping Using Vision Transformers: Model Description, Implementation, and Examples"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:40:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coastal algal bloom monitoring requires frequent, spatially detailed, and globally consistent observations, provided by Landsat-8/9 and Sentinel-2 A/B/C. Together, these missions offer over a decade of medium-resolution multispectral imagery with near-global coverage every 2-3 days, enabling the detection of fragmented bloom structures not resolvable by coarse ocean-color sensors. However, their use in aquatic environments remains challenging due to limited spectral coverage and a lack of harmonized reflectance products. As an alternative to traditional bio-optical methods, deep learning-based image classification offers a data-driven approach that can overcome many of these limitations. This study presents the first successful implementation of vision transformer-based coastal algal bloom mapping using 30-m Landsat-Sentinel-2 images. A globally distributed bloom patch dataset was generated across bloom-prone coastal hotspots worldwide. Four transformer-based architectures were compared against a standard convolutional baseline for fine-scale bloom detection, and assessed under different optical water types and atmospheric and surface conditions. All deep learning models showed strong capabilities in detecting floating bloom areas, with omission and commission errors of 8-65%. Under cloud and glint stress in a time series, the Swin Transformer outperformed traditional spectral-index approaches, which produced widespread false positives, effectively avoiding cloud- and glint-affected pixels. Comparisons with MODIS-derived products further highlighted the benefits of higher spatial resolution in detecting fragmented and irregularly affected blooms. Our findings support deep learning as a reliable tool for medium-resolution, consistent monitoring of floating algal blooms in dynamic coastal environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:36Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Marashian","name":"Marashian, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexis","familyName":"Palmer","name":"Palmer, Alexis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharina","familyName":"von der Wense","name":"von der Wense, Katharina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speaking in Self-Assessing Tongues: On the Verbalized Confidence of LLMs in Machine Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:27:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:06:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid rise in popularity of large language models (LLMs) for translation calls for a thorough study of the reliability of their confidence in their own outputs. Unlike many generation tasks, translation errors and confidence levels can be useful at different levels of granularity (tokens, words, or spans). Unsupervised approaches based on internal signals like predicted probabilities can be misleading because they reflect certainty among alternatives rather than correctness. In addition, they require access to such internal signals. Here, we devise five verbalized methods of extracting an LLM's per-token confidence without those shortcomings and compare their reliability with that of the model's internal signals of certainty. We evaluate reliability using two forms of alignment: fine-grained error detection and calibration. For both, internal and verbalized methods perform similarly, although results vary by model. Interestingly, we find little to no correlation between internal and verbalized methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petr","familyName":"Nyoma","name":"Nyoma, Petr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rift: A Conflict Signature for Deception in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:22:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:06:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A model that lies while knowing the truth is the central case ELK cannot handle with behavioral evaluation alone. We ask whether such deception leaves an internal signature distinguishing it from honest error. Our key move is a control for wrongness: we contrast a sleeper agent (knows the truth, lies on trigger) against a naive liar (fine-tuned to emit the same wrong answers with no honest training). Both produce identical wrong outputs; any difference is about knowledge conflict, not incorrectness. We find deceptive forward passes carry a conflict signature - 2.1-2.3x higher residual rank than naive-liar passes on the same wrong answer - strong enough to identify which of two responses is the lie with 100% accuracy and no labels, across GPT-2 small/medium (three seeds) and three instruct models. Across Qwen2.5-1.5B/7B and Phi-3-mini, instructed deception raises residual rank on every tested fact (18/18, 40/40, 34/34); on Phi-3, lies separate perfectly from both honest answers and hallucinations (AUC 1.0, Wilcoxon p~6e-11). The signature survives strategic self-constructed deception (model invents its own lie, AUC 1.0), active concealment attempts (AUC 1.0), and length-controlled replication (20/20, AUC 1.0, p~1e-6). Using basis-free relative representations, a probe trained on one model family detects deception in two other families zero-shot (mean AUC 0.933), surviving simultaneous architecture and format change (AUC 0.821), and transfers across five languages (AUC 1.000, length-controlled). The signature is read-only: detectable but not injectable (0/8 both directions). Honest limitations and six negative experiments are documented in full."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 4 figures. Code and experiment logs: https://github.com/Omibranch/Rift"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:18Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vanshali","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Vanshali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea M.","familyName":"Bejar","name":"Bejar, Andrea M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Halil Ertugrul","familyName":"Aktas","name":"Aktas, Halil Ertugrul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quoc-Huy","familyName":"Trinh","name":"Trinh, Quoc-Huy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debesh","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Debesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gorkem","familyName":"Durak","name":"Durak, Gorkem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulas","familyName":"Bagci","name":"Bagci, Ulas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting LLM Adaptation for 3D CT Report Generation: A Study of Scaling and Diagnostic Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:51:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:05:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in multimodal learning, including large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs), have demonstrated strong adaptability to natural images. However, extending their use to the medical domain, particularly for volumetric (3D) images, is challenging due to high computational complexity, volumetric dependencies and the semantic gap between visual features and clinical terminology. Naively fine-tuning LLMs on limited medical data often leads to overfitting and clinical hallucination, where linguistic fluency is prioritized over clinical factuality. In this study, we investigate parameter-efficient adaptation strategies for volumetric CT report generation and introduce RAD3D-Prefix, a lightweight diagnostic-prior conditioning framework that minimizes the need for extensive parameter training. This module integrates image embeddings with multi-label diagnostic classification logits, preserving critical clinical details while bridging the semantic gap. By keeping the LLM frozen, our method requires minimal trainable parameters and mitigates the risk of overfitting on small, domain-specific datasets. Through a systematic study spanning LLMs from 96.1M to 1.6B parameters, we find that fine-tuning is most beneficial for smaller LLMs, whereas freezing larger (~1B+ LLMs and training only lightweight projection layers provides a superior trade-off between performance, generalization, and computational efficiency. Across multiple automatic metrics and a clinical reader study, RAD3D-Prefix outperforms comparable parameter-efficient baselines and demonstrates strong out-of-domain generalization while using substantially fewer trainable parameters than fully fine-tuned alternatives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:57Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anhao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Anhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlong","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Junlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingqi","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PowerOPD: Stabilizing On-Policy Distillation with Bounded Power Transformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:37:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:04:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard on-policy distillation (OPD) for large language models estimates the reverse-KL objective using student-sampled tokens, yielding an unbiased single-sample Monte Carlo estimator that avoids vocabulary-wide computation. However, we show that this estimator suffers from severe training pathologies in practice: sample inefficiency, unstable generation dynamics, and a substantial performance gap compared to exact full-vocabulary OPD. Reward-level diagnosis traces these pathologies to the log-ratio reward, which is unbounded by construction, producing extremely high-variance gradients concentrated at early positions and persisting throughout training; standard post-hoc scaling fail as they operate only after this distortion occurs. To solve this problem, we propose PowerOPD: a family of natively bounded, sign-consistent rewards from the Box-Cox power transformation, parameterized by alpha \u0026gt; 0, of which the log-ratio is the degenerate alpha -\u0026gt; 0 limit. Across six mathematical reasoning benchmarks and four Qwen3 teacher-student pairs, PowerOPD achieves benchmark-averaged Avg@8/Pass@8 gains of up to +6.37/+5.71 over vanilla OPD, +3.01/+3.54 over post-hoc stabilization, and +2.59/+8.90 over full-vocabulary OPD, while reducing wall-clock time by 59.2% and peak GPU memory by 23.1%. Larger alpha generally improves accuracy, consistently shortens responses, and keeps gradient norms more than 3,000x smaller than vanilla OPD."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:39Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hazem","familyName":"Ayman","name":"Ayman, Hazem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Menna","familyName":"Sedik","name":"Sedik, Menna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kareem","familyName":"Mostafa","name":"Mostafa, Kareem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Soliman","name":"Soliman, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samer","familyName":"Saber","name":"Saber, Samer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Habib","name":"Habib, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cluster-Aware Dual-Level Test Specification Generation for Large-Scale Automotive Software Requirements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:36:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:04:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating test specifications that satisfy Automotive SPICE SWE.6 requirements becomes increasingly challenging and time-consuming as projects scale to thousands of requirements. Because this manual process often consumes weeks of engineering effort, automation becomes a critical necessity. However, standard Large Language Model (LLM) approaches struggle at scale: processing requirements individually discards vital inter-requirement dependencies, while feeding entire corpora at once exceeds context-window limits, leading to incomplete integration coverage and redundant test cases. This paper presents a novel \"Cluster-then-Summarize\" pipeline that addresses these limitations through three-stages. Requirements are embedded using sentence transformers and grouped using UMAP dimensionality reduction followed by HDBSCAN density-based clustering. This grouping utilizes an automatic minimum cluster size selection driven by a quality criterion combining normalized Silhouette and Calinski-Harabasz scores. A multi-level map-reduce summarization algorithm then distills each cluster into concise, domain-conformant descriptions while preserving quantitative thresholds and safety integrity levels. The pipeline exploits the derived cluster topology to generate test specifications at two levels: individual requirement verification and cluster-level integration tests that verify cross-requirement feature behavior. A nearby-cluster context mechanism provides bounded cross-feature awareness during each LLM call, and Retrieval-Augmented Generation grounds all outputs in ISO 26262 and ASPICE standards. Evaluation on automotive requirement datasets of varying scale demonstrates that the cluster-aware approach improves integration test coverage and maintains summarization fidelity compared to baseline methods while scaling efficiently to thousands of requirements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:35Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svetlana","familyName":"Lukina","name":"Lukina, Svetlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamad Al","familyName":"Mdfaa","name":"Mdfaa, Mohamad Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gloria","familyName":"Haro","name":"Haro, Gloria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Zagoruyko","name":"Zagoruyko, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonzalo","familyName":"Ferrer","name":"Ferrer, Gonzalo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VL-MemKnG: Hybrid Memory with a Spatio-Temporal Knowledge Graph for Question Answering over Long Egocentric Navigation Trajectories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Answering navigation-relevant questions over long egocentric videos requires retrieving and organizing evidence distributed across distant temporal moments while maintaining spatial and contextual consistency. Although long-context vision--language models can achieve strong answer quality, they are computationally expensive for long trajectories and inefficient for repeated querying. Recent graph-based approaches such as VL-KnG address this challenge through persistent spatio-temporal knowledge graphs, but graph-centric retrieval alone may underrepresent broader temporal continuity and contextual cues. We present VL-MemKnG, a hybrid memory framework that extends VL-KnG by combining a spatio-temporal knowledge graph with persistent segment-level contextual memory. The knowledge graph captures structured relational information and long-range object associations, while segment-level memory preserves broader temporal context for long-horizon evidence retrieval. A hybrid retrieval-and-reasoning module jointly operates over both memory representations to produce evidence-grounded answers and temporally organized supporting evidence. We also introduce WalkieKnowledgeT+, an extension of WalkieKnowledge for long-horizon navigation-oriented video question answering. The benchmark includes temporally distributed reasoning tasks requiring evidence aggregation across multiple non-cooccurring moments. On WalkieKnowledgeT+, VL-MemKnG improves Top-1 retrieval accuracy from 58% to 67% and Recall@1 from 34.50% to 40.55%, outperforming all compared methods, including Gemini 2.5 Pro and Qwen 3.5+. The gains are particularly pronounced on temporal-global and temporally scattered aggregation questions, demonstrating the benefits of combining structured relational memory with segment-level contextual memory while maintaining efficient query-time inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verified Detection and Prevention of Concurrency Anomalies in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.4; F.3.1; D.1.3; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:19:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent LLM systems share state through memory stores, vector indices, and tool registries. We model such sharing as long-running read-generate-write operations under deterministic-generation semantics -- the regime durable-execution engines enforce by deterministic replay -- and formalize four concurrency anomalies in TLA+: stale-generation, phantom-tool, causal-cascade, and tool-effect reordering, structural analogues of classical isolation anomalies, each with a TLC counter-example. The exclusion lattice over these anomalies is trivial; the contribution is the mechanically verified realizability and strict separation of one maximal chain within it, $L_0 \\subsetneq \\cdots \\subsetneq L_4$, to our knowledge the first machine-checked consistency hierarchy for such runtimes. A development of 274 Verus obligations (zero assume, zero admit; trust base: two structural axioms and a mutex correspondence) proves the detectors sound and complete against the specifications and each runtime its avoidance set. Three deployed Rust runtimes realize L0-L1 (pessimistic locking, serializable snapshot isolation, default-SI), each verified against stale-generation and refined to its state machine; L2-L4 are exec-mode-verified with dependency-free prevention twins (A3, A6, A2: 0/1000 versus 1000/1000), and L2 is run live across three model families (A3 prevented in all 120 retracted sessions). We reproduce a silent lost update in ByteDance's deer-flow, formalizing its fix as a verified $L_0 \\to L_1$ refinement, and exhibit tool-effect reordering in LangGraph's ToolNode on unmodified output, removed by an L3 commit-order sequencer. The verified detector, refinements, and realizability artifacts are the contribution; the phenomena and lattice are classical."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 2 figures, 6 tables. Verus/TLA+ verification artifact, reference Rust runtime, and Python harnesses, plus a supplementary appendix (Sections A-F, Tables S1-S6), included as ancillary files"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Generated Error Training for Token Editing in Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:13:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Token-to-token (T2T) editing lets LLaDA2.1 revise committed tokens during block-diffusion decoding. The released recipe trains this editor on random vocabulary corruptions, but at inference the editor sees the model's own fluent, high-confidence draft errors instead. We study this training-inference mismatch and propose self-generated T2T, which performs a no-gradient draft pass, fills masked positions with predicted tokens, and supervises recovery in a second pass under these self-generated corruptions. We implement the update as a short LoRA continued-pretraining pass on LLaDA2.1-mini and evaluate on several benchmarks under the official Q-Mode T2T procedure with unchanged inference parameters. The method generally improves accuracy while reducing T2T edit intensity, mitigating failure modes such as final-digit transcription errors after otherwise correct reasoning and excessive self-correction before short factual answers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadsadegh","familyName":"Abolhasani","name":"Abolhasani, Mohammadsadegh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid Reza","familyName":"Firoozfar","name":"Firoozfar, Hamid Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Mousavi","name":"Mousavi, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Jen-Hwa","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Paul Jen-Hwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Parasocial Scripts to Dyadic Persistence in Autonomous AI-Agent Communities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While parasocial interactions (PSIs) and parasocial relationships (PSRs) have been studied in conventional media settings, we investigate whether PSI- (colloquial) relational cues also exist in online communities where both sides are autonomous AI agents. We analyze 4,434 posts and 50,338 comments from Moltbook through three theory-based textual indicators: attachment/intimacy language, reciprocity bids, and self-identification to original poster (OP). The combined results across methods based on keyword matching, few-shot large language model (LLM) annotation, and grouped-context LLM annotation reveal that PSI colloquial cues prevail and are strongly associated with OP re-engagement and a reciprocal reply structure. These results are robust across negative controls, nullification, clustered-standard-error re-estimation, and multiple-testing correction. A dyadic persistence test further affirms reciprocity bids aligned with sustained OP-involving mutual recurrence, providing empirical evidence for bridging interaction-level PSI scripts with PSR-consistent repeated dyadic patterns. We interpret the evidence as a behavioral structure in discourse by LLM-enabled agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted for review in ARR for EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mingzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transductive Zero-Shot Audio Classification with Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive language-audio pretraining (CLAP) enables zero-shot audio classification, but standard inference classifies each clip in isolation and ignores the structure of the unlabeled test set. We present the first systematic study of TransCLIP-style transductive inference for CLAP: a text-anchored spherical Gaussian-mixture EM that refines zero-shot posteriors using the audio-embedding statistics of the test batch, with no labels, no gradients, and negligible compute (about 15 ms on one CPU core for 2,000 clips). Across ESC-50, UrbanSound8K, and VocalSound, this consistently improves top-1 accuracy by +4.6 to +9.2 points over the zero-shot baseline (e.g., 89.1 -\u0026gt; 94.8% on ESC-50, 73.8 -\u0026gt; 81.8% on UrbanSound8K). We further show that the gain (i) is governed by a simple operating boundary -- roughly 2.5 test samples per class per batch are required, with diminishing returns beyond ~5; (ii) is complementary to entropy-guided prompt weighting, with the combination reaching 96.2% on ESC-50; and (iii) attenuates but remains positive under long-tailed batches (+4.9 -\u0026gt; +3.1 points at a 20:1 imbalance), which we report as an explicit limitation. We also document a negative result: on TUT Urban Acoustic Scenes 2018, where zero-shot CLAP is near chance, transduction has no signal to amplify."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:51:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:51:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:51:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Tripodi","name":"Tripodi, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reed","familyName":"Naidoo","name":"Naidoo, Reed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah F.","familyName":"McGough","name":"McGough, Sarah F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tapabrata","familyName":"Chakraborti","name":"Chakraborti, Tapabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing, Fusion, and Trustworthiness: A Systematic Evaluation of Foundation Model Representations for Multimodal Cancer Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T09:50:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models (FMs) have emerged as powerful representation extractors for medical data, yet their generalizability to datasets under distribution shift remains underexplored. This work systematically evaluates FM-based representations on a suite of computational pathology tasks across two real-world commercial cohorts, IH-BC and IH-NSCLC, drawn from the licensed in-house (IH) oncology dataset. The analysis focuses on two modalities, whole-slide images and transcriptomic profiles, drawn from the IH multimodal data. We first benchmark unimodal probing performance across five FMs on eight downstream classification tasks, and find that image and omics representations carry complementary predictive signals. Then we investigate whether multimodal fusion can yield additional gains over unimodal baselines by comparing three image-omics fusion strategies built on paired representations. The trustworthiness of selected unimodal and multimodal pipelines is further assessed through conformal prediction. Our results show that FM representations achieve competitive performance on out-of-distribution data and that multimodal fusion helps mainly when no single modality dominates the signal. Conformal prediction reveals that in the majority of cases where a point prediction fails, the true diagnosis remains recoverable within the prediction set, reinforcing the value of uncertainty-aware inference for clinical support."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankyul","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Hankyul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewon","familyName":"Noh","name":"Noh, Jaewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sang","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Sang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yongsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel Waikin Loh","familyName":"Matienzo","name":"Matienzo, Gabriel Waikin Loh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young Il","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Young Il","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ee Wei","familyName":"Seah","name":"Seah, Ee Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akriti","familyName":"Vij","name":"Vij, Akriti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evaluation of Data Leakage Risks in Tool-Using LLM Agents in Realistic Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T09:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are increasingly being adopted in enterprise and personal settings with access to emails, databases, documents, and other tools where they can read, update, and disseminate sensitive information. Much of prior research on data leakage risks in agents has focused on adversarial data exfiltration through prompt injections and jailbreaks. However, sensitive information may also be exposed during non-adversarial use, creating leakage risks even when users issue benign requests.\n We report a joint evaluation by the Singapore AI Safety Institute and the Korea AI Safety Institute examining agent data leakage in 12 realistic, non-adversarial tasks spanning customer support, DevOps, web automation, and enterprise and personal productivity. The evaluation covers five risk types: lack of data awareness, audience awareness, policy compliance, data minimization, and access-boundary awareness. Both institutes tested a common set of scenarios mirroring real-world deployments using independent testing environments and task-specific LLM-judge rubrics.\n Across the three tested agents, none achieved fully correct and fully safe execution across all scenarios. Successful task completion often coincided with data-handling failures such as accessing unnecessary information or disclosing information to inappropriate recipients, indicating that capability and data-handling safety should be evaluated separately. Qualitative review also revealed claim-action mismatches, simulation-aware behavior, user-simulator role reversal, and interpretation gaps in automated judging. Overall, the results indicate that operational data leakage is a first-order agent-safety concern distinct from adversarial exfiltration and provide a methodology for future evaluations of agent data-handling safety."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Mehdizavareh","name":"Mehdizavareh, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"Santangelo","name":"Santangelo, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanna","familyName":"Nicora","name":"Nicora, Giovanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon Lebech","familyName":"Cichosz","name":"Cichosz, Simon Lebech","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arianna","familyName":"Dagliati","name":"Dagliati, Arianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arijit","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Arijit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Bellazzi","name":"Bellazzi, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Informative Missingness to Generate Irregular Clinical Time Series"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T13:06:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Laboratory tests in electronic health records are collected irregularly, and the absence of a test order can be as informative as the measurement itself. Such missingness reflects clinicians' decisions and patient physiology, making it important to model it directly rather than treat it as a preprocessing artifact. Here we present a diffusion-based approach for generating clinical time series that jointly models laboratory values and their observation patterns using the public Data Analytics Challenge on Missing Data Imputation (DACMI) benchmark derived from MIMIC-III. To preserve realistic sampling, we align chart times into 4-hour intervals and segment admissions into 7-day windows, producing trajectories that pair each lab value with a corresponding observation indicator. Standard transformations and normalization are applied to stabilize training. Our method extends the TimeDiff framework to learn continuous lab values and discrete missingness patterns through complementary diffusion objectives. Experiments show that the generated data closely match real patient trajectories across individual lab distributions and joint value-missingness embeddings, demonstrating that diffusion models can capture clinically meaningful dependencies between patient physiology and clinicians' testing behavior under MNAR-like (missing-not-at-random) missingness. These preliminary results indicate that our model can serve as an initial component toward developing clinical foundation models. By producing synthetic priors that preserve key physiology-missingness relationships, this work motivates the subsequent training of Prior-Data Fitted Networks capable of leveraging informative missingness, which we will investigate in the extended work."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:34Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17104","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17104","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17104","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Usami","name":"Usami, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkatram","familyName":"Vishwanath","name":"Vishwanath, Venkatram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. Wes","familyName":"Bethel","name":"Bethel, E. Wes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prefill/Decode-Aware Evaluation of LLM Inference on Emerging AI Accelerators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T12:23:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in latency- and cost-sensitive settings, inference efficiency has become a central systems challenge. While GPUs dominate current deployments, a growing number of AI accelerators claim advantages for LLM inference, yet it remains unclear under which conditions such accelerators outperform GPUs in practice. Recent inference systems decompose execution into Prefill and Decode phases, which exhibit distinct computational characteristics and latency metrics, commonly captured by time to first token (TTFT) and time per output token (TPOT).\n This paper presents a phase-aware evaluation of LLM inference performance across GPUs and emerging AI accelerators using a common model, Llama2-7B. By separately measuring Prefill and Decode performance, we reveal that accelerator advantages differ by phase and metric. Our results show that GPUs consistently excel in the compute-intensive Prefill phase, while GroqRack achieves significantly lower TPOT during Decode (batching not currently supported). However, GPUs regain an advantage in Decode throughput as batch size increases.\n These findings demonstrate that each platform exhibits distinct phase-dependent strengths. We further analyze heterogeneous Prefill/Decode disaggregation across different accelerator platforms, identifying performance gains and the workload and network conditions under which such gains are realized."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the Workshop on HPC for AI Foundation Models \u0026amp; LLMs for Science (HPAI4S'26), co-located with IEEE IPDPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17104","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17094","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17094","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17094","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senyu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Senyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingshan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qingshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LogCopilot: Automating Log Aggregation Analysis through Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-13T15:47:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Logs record the runtime behavior of software and are widely used in various tasks such as debugging, testing, and fault diagnosis. With the increase in system size and complexity, log analysis has gradually become a challenging task. Current industrial systems typically use log aggregation systems such as Grafana Loki and ELK to simplify the log collection and analysis process. Engineers write queries using the DSL query language provided by these systems can complete a variety of log analysis tasks. However, writing these queries is often time-consuming and labor-intensive, as it requires engineers to have a thorough understanding of the DSL syntax and the detailed information contained in the logs. To address these challenges, this paper proposes LogCopilot, an automated log aggregation analysis framework based on large language models (LLMs). LogCopilot accepts natural language log analysis instructions and accomplishes automated log analysis through knowledge retrieval and tool calling. LogCopilot constructs a hierarchical knowledge base to represent and provide key knowledge in logs. And it achieves automated log aggregation analysis by generating and executing LogQL queries. The evaluation based on four log datasets confirm the effectiveness of LogCopilot, which achieves an average accuracy of 76.8% and outperforms baseline approaches. Moreover, experiment results shows that LogCopilot is effective in LogQL query generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17094","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:19Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17082","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17082","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17082","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hauteng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hauteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xieyuanli","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xieyuanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benwu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkai","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wenkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParkingTransformer: LLM-Enhanced End-to-End Trajectory Planning for Autonomous Parking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:52:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"End-to-end autonomous parking has emerged as a critical task within the realm of autonomous driving. However, existing methods suffer from black-box characteristics, lacking high-level semantic understanding and interpretability, which impedes the realization of seamless long-distance autonomous parking from the road to the target spot. To address these limitations, we propose ParkingTransformer, a novel framework that leverages multi-view perception and the scene understanding capability of Large Language Models (LLMs). By combining trajectory queries with LLMs implicit state features, our method interacts directly with historical information and raw sensor data to output planning trajectories, eliminating the need for dense Bird's-View (BEV) representations. To compensate for the inadequate spatial reasoning ability of LLMs, we introduce 3D positional encoding to explicitly inject spatial geometric awareness. Furthermore, a fixed-window streaming mechanism is designed for historical information processing, significantly improving long-term temporal processing efficiency and inference speed. Additionally, a coarse-to-fine decoding strategy is employed to progressively enhance trajectory precision. Extensive closed-loop experiments are conducted on the CARLA simulator and real-world vehicle platforms. The results demonstrate that our method achieves a driving score of 61.32 in CARLA simulator and an average success rate of 88.70% in real-world experiments, validating the feasibility and effectiveness of the proposed algorithms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17082","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:03Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LAAS-RIS, LAAS"],"givenName":"Bastien","familyName":"Dussard","name":"Dussard, Bastien","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LAAS-RIS, LAAS"],"givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Sarthou","name":"Sarthou, Guillaume","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extracting Semantics: LLM-Guided Automatic Population of Robot Ontology from URDF"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-10T08:08:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While commonsense knowledge may suffice for virtual agents, embodied robots interacting with humans require grounded and semantically rich representations of both their environment and their own physical embodiment. In cognitive robotics, ontologies are effective for integrating such heterogeneous knowledge to enable explainable reasoning, even during continuous knowledge updates. Yet, their manual construction remains a bottleneck. We present a preliminary approach for the automatic generation of robot semantic abstractions by transforming Unified Robot Description Format (URDF) models into populated ontologies. Although URDF files provide structural and kinematic descriptions, their identifiers often require commonsense interpretation to recover meaningful semantics, a task at which Large Language Models (LLMs) excel. Our pipeline leverages LLMs to infer semantic relationships by prompting them with concepts from an existing ontology, ensuring the final classification remains aligned with the formal model. To improve reliability, the pipeline combines majority voting across multiple LLM queries along with syntactic and schema-level validation to ensure that generated outputs conform to the expected representation format and ontology constraints. We evaluate the approach on multiple robot descriptions and discuss the generated abstractions. Initial results indicate that the proposed method can effectively bridge the gap between low-level robot descriptions and the structured, grounded knowledge representations required for human-robot interaction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:49:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:49:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:49:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shabari S","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Shabari S","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishanu","familyName":"Saini","name":"Saini, Krishanu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Distributed Inference of LLMs on a P2P Network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T15:40:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prefix caching can reduce LLM inference latency by reusing KV caches across requests with shared prompts, but cluster-scale reuse is challenging because caches are partitioned across nodes. We propose a decentralized, prefix-cache-aware routing scheme for peer-to-peer LLM serving. Each node maintains a local radix tree of its own cached prefixes and asynchronously refreshed estimates of peer caches using periodic anti-entropy. Requests are routed to the node with the longest estimated prefix match, without centralized coordination or KV-cache transfer. Stale metadata only causes cache misses, not incorrect outputs, making weak consistency sufficient for correctness. Evaluation on simulated MMLU workloads show that decentralized routing improves latency under low communication delay and skewed prefix distributions, while high network latency and affinity-induced hotspots limit its benefits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:49:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:49:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:49:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Changqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Changqing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Shouwei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shouwei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Florida State University"],"givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kai","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Argonne National Laboratory"],"givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Sheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Wenqian","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Wenqian","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Coding Agents on SZ-Family Lossy Compression Across Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; D.1.3; D.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-22T16:53:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) coding agents are increasingly applied to code translation and optimization, yet their effectiveness in performance-critical high-performance computing (HPC) settings remains poorly characterized. This paper evaluates LLM-based coding workflows on SZ-family error-bounded lossy compression kernels, which combine numerical constraints with memory-intensive and control-flow-heavy implementations. We study two representative CUDA workloads (SZp and SZx) and target two heterogeneous execution platforms: NVIDIA GPUs and Cerebras wafer-scale accelerators. Focusing on single-agent iterative generation, we analyze not only final throughput but also agent runtime behavior, including iteration patterns, sensitivity to prompt specification, and characteristic failure modes. Our results reveal a pronounced cross-architecture divergence. On GPUs, stronger models can achieve substantially higher throughput but exhibit increased sensitivity to prompt precision and optimization guidance, whereas on Cerebras the dominant challenge lies in producing runnable programs under a PE-centric spatial execution model. We further observe that LLM agents are more effective on modular kernels (SZx) than on tightly coupled bit-level pipelines (SZp), where structural dependencies hinder optimization progress. These findings suggest that evaluating LLM coding agents for HPC requires accounting for both performance outcomes and architecture-specific robustness, and that success on thread-based platforms does not directly transfer to spatial accelerators."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures. Accepted to IPDPS 2026 HPAI4S Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:49:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:49:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingchao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Tingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fanxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huadong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huadong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunyun","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yunyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Renyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correct When Paired, Wrong When Split: Decoupling and Editing Modality-Specific Neurons in MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-20T05:43:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although Knowledge Editing provides an efficient mechanism for updating the knowledge of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), we find that current paradigms still suffer from an important yet remain underexplored issue : editing decoupling failure, where entity-related knowledge can be updated when the model is triggered by multimodal inputs (text--image query pairs), however, it often reverts to outdated pre-edit facts when the paired inputs are split into unimodal ones. Our in-depth empirical analysis reveals that the entity knowledge in MLLMs is not stored as a unified representation, but is instead distributed across disentangled modality-specific pathways. As a result, updates biased toward multimodal queries fail to propagate effectively to unimodal circuits. To bridge this gap, we propose DECODE, which explicitly disentangles and localizes modality-specific neuron groups for targeted knowledge. Extensive experiments demonstrate that DECODE consistently achieves effective knowledge updates under different modality triggers, thereby mitigating editing decoupling failures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:49:31Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:49:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:49:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":203,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2026-06-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2026-06-17T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+MoE+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}